following break/lunch schedules. \n○​ Mitigation: Send reminders via chat/email; escalate to TLs. \n●​ Event: SLA breach due to unexpected call surge. \n○​ Mitigation: Implement volume-based skilling to balance the load. \n○​ Mitigation: Coordinate with operations to reallocate tasks. \n●​ Event: Identify P0 or P1 events due to a surge in call/chat volumes. \n○​ P0 (Priority 0): Critical impact on service levels (e.g., service is completely down \nor severely impaired). \n○​ P1 (Priority 1): Major impact but still functioning (e.g., high call volumes causing \ndelays, but service is up). \n■​ Alert counterpart WFM or the client immediately upon identifying P0/P1 \nevents. Communication should be prompt, clear, and actionable. Use \ncommunication channels like email, chat, or call based on urgency. \n■​ Set mitigation actions to alleviate the situation.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nReal-Time & Workforce Management \n \n●​ Mitigation Actions for Surge: \n○​ Overtime Activation: Offer voluntary overtime to agents to increase coverage. \n○​ Shift Swaps: Reassign agents from non-essential tasks (e.g., training, emails) to \nhandle incoming calls/chats. \n○​ Queue Prioritization: Prioritize high-impact queues (e.g., calls over emails or \nchats) to reduce the impact on key metrics. \n○​ Break Adjustments: Adjust break times for agents to ensure continuous coverage \nduring peak hours. \n○​ Escalate to Upper Management: Inform management to be on standby for \npotential escalations or approvals needed for extended mitigation actions. \n \n2. Scheduling & Shift Optimization \nTask: \n●​ Maintain and adjust agent schedules in workforce tool. \n●​ Approve/deny schedule change requests based on business needs. \n●​ Adjust schedules dynamically to align with demand. \n●​ Track compliance of scheduled breaks/lunches. \nSituational Events & Mitigations: \n●​ Event: More agents requesting schedule swaps than available slots. \n○​ Mitigation: Implement a rotation policy to ensure fairness. \n○​ Mitigation: Limit last-minute changes to avoid service disruptions. \n●​ Event: Multiple agents taking breaks at the same time, causing low coverage. \n○​ Mitigation: Stagger break/lunch schedules. \n●​ Event: Shortage of staff during peak hours. \n○​ Mitigation: Offer voluntary overtime or early login incentives. \n \n3. Break Schedule Optimization \nTask:\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nReal-Time & Workforce Management \n \n●​ Align break schedules with forecasted call volume. \n●​ Balance breaks to maintain adequate coverage. \n●​ Ensure that agents do not take breaks outside of assigned slots. \nSituational Events & Mitigations: \n●​ Event: Agents taking breaks simultaneously, causing queue build-up. \n○​ Mitigation: Split breaks based on call volume trends. \n●​ Event: Agents extending breaks, causing schedule imbalances. \n○​ Mitigation: Implement adherence scorecards and escalate repeat offenders. \n \n4. Shift Bid Recommendations \nTask: \n●​ Analyze historical data and call trends. \n●​ Recommend shift bid structures aligning with business needs. \n●​ Suggest rotation policies to balance agent preferences and coverage. \n●​ Implement schedule adjustments for seasonal changes. \nSituational Events & Mitigations: \n●​ Event: Shift bid results in too many agents in off-peak hours. \n○​ Mitigation: Adjust shift start times before finalizing bids. \n●​ Event: Agents unsatisfied with shift assignments. \n○​ Mitigation: Allow limited shift swaps within predefined rules. \n \n5. Reporting & Analytics \nTask: \n●​ Generate real-time and daily reports on adherence, SLA, and productivity. \n●​ Provide insights on workforce trends and operational gaps. \n●​ Share actionable recommendations based on data.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nReal-Time & Workforce Management \n \nSituational Events & Mitigations: \n●​ Event: Reports showing consistent SLA breaches at specific hours. \n○​ Mitigation: Optimize staffing and reassign agents accordingly. \n●​ Event: Inaccurate or delayed reports. \n○​ Mitigation: Automate report generation and cross-check data before submission. \n \n6. Client & Operations Communication \nTask: \n●​ Provide real-time updates on SLA and staffing. \n●​ Escalate operational concerns to clients and management. \n●​ Collaborate with operations to implement workforce adjustments. \nSituational Events & Mitigations: \n●​ Event: Client request for sudden schedule changes. \n○​ Mitigation: Assess feasibility and suggest alternative solutions if needed. \n●​ Event: TLs not responding to adherence alerts. \n○​ Mitigation: Escalate to higher management. \n \n7. Future Schedule Planning \nTask: \n●​ Analyze forecast staffing requirements based on call volume trends. \n●​ Prepare seasonal and event-based staffing plans. \n●​ Recommend proactive coverages if gaps are identified. \nSituational Events & Mitigations: \n●​ Event: Increased attrition affecting future staffing. \n○​ Mitigation: Suggest early coverages to fill forecasted gaps.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nReal-Time & Workforce Management \n \n●​ Event: Unexpected demand spike outside of forecast. \n○​ Mitigation: Develop contingency staffing strategies. \n8. Call Volume Pattern Analysis & SLA Risk Management \nTask: \n●​ Analyze call patterns to predict peak and low-demand hours. \n●​ Identify risk periods where SLA breaches may occur. \n●​ Proactively recommend adjustments to schedules. \nSituational Events & Mitigations: \n●​ Event: SLA breach predicted for upcoming hours. \n○​ Mitigation: Recommend break adjustments and optimize staffing. \n●​ Event: Calls unexpectedly dropping due to system issues. \n○​ Mitigation: Notify IT support immediately and reassign calls as needed. \n \n \nChecklist \n1. Real-Time Monitoring \n●​ Monitor Agent Adherence \n​Track agent productivity and compliance using workforce tool. \n​Send adherence alerts and follow up with Team Leads (TLs). \n●​ Ensure SLA & KPI Compliance \n​Monitor service levels and key performance indicators. \n​Identify and report deviations immediately. \n●​ Address Absenteeism & Tardiness \n​Track and report attendance issues. \n​Communicate staffing gaps to operations.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nReal-Time & Workforce Management \n \n2. Scheduling & Shift Optimization \n●​ Maintain & Adjust Schedules \n​Approve/deny schedule change requests. \n​Adjust schedules dynamically to meet business needs. \n●​ Monitor & Optimize Break Schedules \n​Ensure breaks align with call volume forecasts. \n​Balance breaks to maintain coverage. \n \n3. Shift Bid Recommendations \n●​ Analyze Historical Data \n​Review call trends and demand. \n​Identify optimal shift structures. \n●​ Recommend Shift Changes \n​Propose shift bid schedules aligned with business needs. \n​Suggest rotation policies for fair scheduling. \n \n4. Reporting & Analytics \n●​ Generate Performance Reports \n​Create real-time and daily reports on adherence and SLA. \n​Identify trends and operational gaps. \n●​ Provide Insights & Recommendations \n​Share actionable workforce insights with operations. \n​Suggest improvements based on data trends. \n \n5. Client & Operations Communication \n●​ Provide Real-Time Updates\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nReal-Time & Workforce Management \n \n​Communicate SLA and staffing updates to stakeholders. \n​Escalate operational issues as needed. \n●​ Collaborate on Workforce Adjustments \n​Work with operations to implement schedule changes. \n​Address client concerns regarding workforce performance. \n \n6. Future Schedule Planning \n●​ Forecast Staffing Needs \n​Analyze historical call volume data. \n​Prepare staffing plans for peak periods. \n●​ Recommend Hiring Plans \n​Identify staffing gaps in advance. \n​Suggest proactive hiring strategies. \n \n7. Call Volume Analysis & SLA Risk Management \n●​ Analyze Call Patterns \n​Predict peak and low-demand hours. \n​Identify high-risk SLA breach periods. \n●​ Recommend Schedule Adjustments \n​Optimize workforce allocation to prevent SLA breaches. \n​Implement contingency staffing plans. \n \nConclusion \nThis SOP ensures efficient real-time workforce management, proactive problem-solving, and \noptimal staffing alignment for enhanced service delivery.\n✅ Shift Bid/Scheduling\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nShift Bid/Scheduling \n \nShift Bid/Scheduling \n \nListen to this tab\n1. Initial Request & Strategy Planning \nTask: \n●​ Upon receiving a shift bid request or required FTE update, the workforce team will conduct \na strategic planning meeting. \n●​ The meeting will focus on: \n○​ Identifying staffing needs. \n○​ Aligning schedule structures with business requirements. \n○​ Evaluating workload distribution and peak-hour coverage. \n●​ The strategy plan should include: \n○​ Proposed shift structures. \n○​ Special scheduling considerations for students/pregnant employees. \n○​ Timeline for execution. \nSituational Events & Mitigations: \n●​ Scenario: Request received with unrealistic FTE allocation. \n○​ Mitigation: Highlight constraints, propose an alternative FTE distribution, and seek \nclient approval. \n●​ Scenario: Incomplete data from client or operations. \n○​ Mitigation: Request missing information before finalizing the strategy. \n \n2. Schedule Shell Creation & Approval \nTask: \n●​ Schedulers will draft initial schedule shells based on business needs and workforce \navailability.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nShift Bid/Scheduling \n \n●​ The proposed schedule shells will be submitted for approval to the client and operations \nteam. \n●​ Upon approval, a formal email will be sent to all stakeholders (client & operations) for \ndocumentation. \nSituational Events & Mitigations: \n●​ Scenario: Client or operations requests changes after approval. \n○​ Mitigation: Assess feasibility, provide rationale for existing structure, and make \nadjustments only if necessary. \n●​ Scenario: Approval is delayed, causing timeline shifts. \n○​ Mitigation: Follow up proactively and escalate if approval is not received within the \nagreed timeline. \n \n3. Shift Bidding Process \nTask: \n●​ RTAs will facilitate the shift bidding process based on the last three months\' Achieve Score \nor performance ranking. \n●​ Special scheduling accommodations will only be granted to: \n○​ Students (must be pre-identified by operations before finalizing schedule shells). \n○​ Pregnant employees (must be pre-identified by operations before finalizing \nschedule shells). \n●​ Stack ranking will determine priority in selecting schedules. \n●​ Employees failing to submit their shift preference by the deadline will be assigned an \navailable schedule. \n●​ The shift bidding process should take 1-2 weeks to complete. \nSituational Events & Mitigations: \n●​ Scenario: Employees fail to submit their preferences on time. \n○​ Mitigation: Send reminders before the deadline; unresponsive employees will be \nassigned schedules automatically. \n●​ Scenario: Employees request exceptions beyond students/pregnant employees. \n○​ Mitigation: Clearly communicate that no other exceptions will be allowed.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nShift Bid/Scheduling \n \n●​ Scenario: Employees dispute their shift bid ranking. \n○​ Mitigation: Provide transparency on Achieve Score calculations and ensure \nranking data accuracy. \n \n4. Transition Week Implementation \nTask: \n●​ Once the shift bid process is complete and all schedule shells are selected: \n○​ RTAs will formally announce the transition week through Zoho Announcement. \n○​ The transition period is considered a critical work week due to potential \ndisruptions. \n●​ RTAs will monitor adherence closely to prevent SLA breaches. \nSituational Events & Mitigations: \n●​ Scenario: High levels of absenteeism during transition week. \n○​ Mitigation: Have backup staffing in place and ensure TLs reinforce attendance \nexpectations. \n●​ Scenario: Employees struggle with adapting to new schedules. \n○​ Mitigation: Offer reminders and reinforcement from TLs to ensure compliance. \n \n5. Communication & Finalization (EXPANDED)\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nShift Bid/Scheduling \n \nPre-Creation of Schedule Shells \n●​ Meeting with Operations & Client to:​\n \n○​ Discuss upcoming possible movements. \n○​ Identify any requests for specific shift structures (e.g., AM/PM preference, workload \nalignment). \n○​ Align on the final deadline for requests before schedule creation begins.​\n \nPre-Transition Week \n●​ Finalization Meeting to:​\n \n○​ Confirm all approved schedule requests and exceptions. \n○​ Set clear expectations: Any additional requests during or after this point may \ncause delays and will be discouraged to maintain scheduling integrity.​\n \nTransition Schedule Sign-Off \n●​ Sign-Off Meeting with all key stakeholders (Client, Ops Manager, STL, RTA Manager, TL, \nScheduler, RTAs) to:​\n \n○​ Officially approve the transition schedule and stable shift assignments. \n○​ Reinforce: Additional change requests at this stage may disrupt implementation \nand will not be entertained unless critical and approved by all stakeholders.​\n \nRelease of Final Schedule Shells \n●​ Once finalized and signed off, the schedule shells are considered FINAL.​\n \n●​ Communication will be done via:​\n \n○​ Zoho Announcement. \n○​ Cliq Group (for additional reminders).\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nShift Bid/Scheduling \n \n○​ Workforce tool or direct schedule file if a tool is not available.​\n \nOps Team Reminders \n●​ Do not pre-release or modify schedules without coordination with Workforce.​\n \n●​ Ensure agents are aware of the finality of the schedule and avoid entertaining late change \nrequests.​\n \n \n \n \nTask: \n●​ Zoho Announcement will be used for formal communication to frontline employees. \n●​ Workforce tool (if available) will be updated to reflect the new schedule. \n○​ If a workforce tool is not available, frontline employees will receive a copy of their \nnew schedule. \n●​ Operations team will receive the finalized schedule, but they must not share it with \nfrontline employees. \n●​ Final Pre-Implementation Meeting: \n○​ RTAs will conduct a meeting with the operations team and client to finalize sign-offs \nbefore implementing the transition schedule. \n■​ Included should be the following: \n●​ Client \n●​ Operations Manager and Senior Team Leader \n●​ Workforce RTA Manager \n●​ RTA Team Leader \n●​ Workforce Scheduler \n●​ Workforce RTAs of the program \nSituational Events & Mitigations:\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nShift Bid/Scheduling \n \n●​ Scenario: Employees claim they were not informed of schedule changes. \n○​ Mitigation: Ensure multiple communications via Zoho Announcement and Cliq \nGroup messaging. \n●​ Scenario: Workforce tool updates are delayed or unavailable. \n○​ Mitigation: Provide an alternative schedule sheet for reference. \n \nConclusion \nThis SOP ensures a structured and efficient shift bidding process, minimizing scheduling \ndisruptions while maintaining fairness and operational stability.\n✅ Attendance Pointing System\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nAttendance Pointing System \n \nAttendance Pointing System \n \nListen to this tab\n1. Checking APS \nRTAs must check the PowerBI Dashboard (APS_Zoho_PBI) daily to monitor attendance points for \nemployees under their assigned programs. This ensures real-time tracking and immediate \nresolution of any discrepancies. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n2. Updating APS Points: Guidelines \nTo ensure accurate and efficient attendance validation, a four-level verification process must be \nfollowed within seven days from the attendance issue date. \nLevel 0: Daily Updates by RTA \n●​ RTAs are responsible for daily attendance updates in Zoho. \nLevel 1: 1 to 3 Days After Absence (Employee’s Time Frame)\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nAttendance Pointing System \n \n●​ Employees must dispute logins and submit supporting documents within three days. \n●​ RTAs and company nurses review and process disputes daily. \n●​ If additional information is needed, the processing period extends to 4-6 days. \n●​ All points should be updated before issuing a Notice to Explain (NTE). \nLevel 2: 4 to 5 Days After Absence (Leads and Support’s Time Frame) \n●​ Employees lose direct access to submit documents in Zoho. \n●​ Leads and support teams must upload necessary documents. \n●​ Employees are encouraged to dispute points before logging out. \n●​ Company nurses and RTAs have 2-3 additional days for final processing. \nLevel 3: 6 to 7 Days After Absence (Final Processing Period) \n●​ RTAs publish a pending NTE list displaying APS points. \n●​ Final disputes can still be submitted within this period. \n●​ Last-day allowance of 1-2 days before NTE issuance. \nPost 7-Day Period \n●​ On the 8th day, APS points are finalized. \n●​ Disputes submitted after this period require NTE inclusion. \n●​ Leads and RTAs failing to validate points correctly may receive an NTE. \n●​ NCNS (No Call, No Show), Critical Working Day (CWD), absences (half/full day), lates, \nundertime, suspensions, and leaves must be updated within seven days. \n3. Leave of Absence (LOA) Handling \n●​ Employees on approved or pending LOA/ML are excluded from APS updates. \n●​ RTAs review and update APS points within seven days of the employee’s return. \n4. How to Update APS Points \nDefinition of Terms \n●​ New APS: Displays points per attendance issue. \n●​ Total APS Points: Running total of New APS minus rollback points. \n●​ Rollback Points: Deducted for every 30 and 60 consecutive days of perfect attendance.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nAttendance Pointing System \n \n \n \n \nAPS Updating Steps \n1.​ Verify attendance points and check for errors in Zoho. \n2.​ Ensure Total New APS matches the employee’s current APS points. \n3.​ Bulk Updating: \n○​ Select all dates by ticking the box next to the Eye icon. \n○​ Select specific dates by ticking individual boxes. \n○​ Click “Get New APS” or “Recalculate.” \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n4.​ Verifying APS Points: \n○​ Filter employee records by name or employee number. \n○​ Date range: January 16, 2023, up to seven days ago. \n○​ Validate points using Attendance Tracker or consult leads. \n​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nAttendance Pointing System \n \n​\n \n \n \n \nAttendance Issues & Handling Procedures \n●​ Late & Undertime: Must be disputed upon logout. Employees have seven days to \nprocess disputes. \n●​ Absences: Initially marked in Zoho. Employees must submit required documents to \nreduce APS points. \n●​ Sick Leave Without Pay: Medical certificates uploaded in Zoho. \n●​ Leave Without Pay: Management discretion applies; additional documentation may be \nrequired. \n●​ Suspensions: Points removal must be filed under Leave Request in Zoho. \n●​ LOA: No APS points incurred. Must be recorded in Zoho | LOA Notification Report. \n●​ Medical Documents: Validated by company nurses, uploaded within three days of \nabsence. \n●​ Additional Documents: Validated by RTAs, uploaded within three days. \n●​ NCNS: Updated in Zoho immediately or the next day. \n●​ CWD: Auto-updated in Zoho; manual checks required.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nAttendance Pointing System \n \nFinal Step: \nIf unresolved, report incorrect APS points to Imee via the #Zoho APS - All Accounts Cliq Channel.\n✅ Issuance of NTE and NOD\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nIssuance of Notice to Explain (NTE) and Notice of Decision (NOD) \n \n \nListen to this tab\nIssuance of Notice to Explain (NTE) and Notice of Decision (NOD) \nEffective Date: March 1, 2025 \n​\nDepartment: Workforce RTA Team​\nSubject: Issuance of Notice to Explain (NTE) and Notice of Decision (NOD) for Attendance and \nWorkforce-Related Violations \n \nScope and Responsibilities \nThe Workforce RTA team is responsible for issuing NTE/NOD for attendance-related and \nworkforce-related violations only. Any other violations must be handled by the Operations Team. \nThe following list outlines the specific violations under the RTA\'s jurisdiction: \n1.​ Attendance Pointing System (APS) Violations \n2.​ Failure to Observe Critical Workday \n3.​ Gross and Habitual Neglect of Duties (Absenteeism) \n4.​ Unauthorized Extension of Break \n5.​ No Call No Show \n6.​ Excessive Tardiness and/or Undertime \n7.​ Significant Delay in Rendering Service to Clients/Customers (Work Avoidance) \n8.​ Protraction of Work (Work Avoidance) \n \n \nProcedure for Issuing an NTE \n1.​ Violation Confirmation \n○​ The RTA will monitor attendance and workforce-related adherence reports.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nIssuance of Notice to Explain (NTE) and Notice of Decision (NOD) \n \n○​ If a potential violation is detected, the RTA will validate the issue using attendance \nrecords, system logs, and shift adherence reports. \n○​ For APS violations, the RTA must ensure that all accumulated points align with the \nAttendance Pointing System rules. \n○​ If a violation is confirmed, the RTA will refer to the Revised Company Handbook \n(Effective March 1, 2025) to determine the appropriate disciplinary action. \n2.​ Documentation \n○​ The RTA must document the violation with the following details: \n■​ Date of offense \n■​ Screenshots as evidence (including timestamps and system-generated \nlogs) \n■​ Complete details of the offense \n○​ If multiple violations occur within a short period, the RTA must specify each \ninfraction separately with corresponding evidence. \n3.​ Filing the NTE \n○​ Once all necessary details are gathered, the RTA will complete the NTE Form in \nZoho Sign. \n○​ The document must be sent to the employee with the Operations Team CC’d for \nacknowledgment. \n○​ The employee is required to respond within 5 days from the time the NTE was \nsent. \n4.​ Follow-up and Escalation \n○​ If the employee does not respond within the given timeframe, the RTA must \nescalate the issue to HR for further action. \n○​ For repeated violations, the RTA must document patterns and provide a \nrecommendation for further disciplinary action. \n \nProcedure for Issuing an NOD \n1.​ Review of Employee’s Explanation \n○​ Once the employee submits their response to the NTE, HR will evaluate the \njustification. \n○​ If the explanation is insufficient or unacceptable, HR will provide a final decision. \n2.​ Issuing the NOD\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nIssuance of Notice to Explain (NTE) and Notice of Decision (NOD) \n \n○​ The RTA will issue the Notice of Decision (NOD) via Zoho Sign, indicating the \nfollowing: \n■​ The disciplinary action taken (e.g., written warning, suspension, \ntermination) \n■​ The final decision from HR \n■​ Any corrective actions required \n○​ The NOD must be acknowledged by the employee within 24 hours after issuance. \n3.​ Record Keeping \n○​ All issued NTEs and NODs must be documented and stored in the employee’s HR \nrecord. \n○​ The RTA must ensure that all workforce violations are tracked to monitor \nrecurrence. \n \nSituational Cases and Mitigation Strategies \n1. Employee Denies Violation: \n●​ The RTA must provide documented evidence, including screenshots and system logs. \n●​ If the dispute remains unresolved, escalate the case to HR for review. \n2. Employee Refuses to Sign the NTE/NOD: \n●​ If the employee does not acknowledge the document within the given timeframe, the RTA \nmust escalate it to HR. \n●​ HR will then proceed with the necessary disciplinary actions as per company policy. \n3. System-Related Errors (e.g., Incorrect APS Points): \n●​ If the violation is linked to a system-generated error, the RTA must validate and adjust APS \npoints accordingly before issuing an NTE. \n●​ If the issue persists, report to HR for further resolution. \n4. Employee Requests an Appeal: \n●​ Employees may submit an appeal through HR within 3 working days from NOD issuance. \n●​ The RTA must provide all relevant documentation to support the case review.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nIssuance of Notice to Explain (NTE) and Notice of Decision (NOD) \n \n \nConclusion \nThis SOP ensures a structured approach to managing attendance and workforce-related \nviolations. The RTA team must adhere to these guidelines to maintain consistency, fairness, and \ncompliance with company policies. Any deviation from this SOP must be escalated to HR for \nreview and approval. \n \nUploading an NTE: \n \n1.​ From Zoho homepage, you can access the Zoho Sign Request Form by clicking on HR, \nand then clicking on Zoho Sign Request Form. \n \n \n2.​ Provide all the needed information: \n●​ Employee Name \n●​ Choose NTE: If the NTE also has an admin hearing schedule, tick Admin Hearing. (see \nbelow)\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nIssuance of Notice to Explain (NTE) and Notice of Decision (NOD) \n \n●​ Category: All NTEs sent out by the Workforce RTA team fall under Provisions on \nPerformance of Duties. \n \n●​ Type of Offense: Choose which best fits the violation. \n○​ Attendance Pointing System (APS) Violations \n○​ Failure to Observe Critical Workday \n○​ Gross and Habitual Neglect of Duties (Absenteeism) \n○​ Unauthorized Extension of Break \n○​ No Call No Show \n○​ Excessive Tardiness and/or Undertime \n○​ Significant Delay in Rendering Service to Clients/Customers (Work Avoidance) \n○​ Protraction of Work (Work Avoidance) \n \n●​ Details: This part usually contains who prepared the document, or a brief summary of the \noffense \n \n \n●​ Date of Offense: This is the date when the employee committed the violation, or the latest \noccurrence on a series of violations (i.e. Unauthorized Extension of Break) \n \n3.​ Provide the Approver for the NTE. This person is usually the operations manager or the \nprogram manager.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nIssuance of Notice to Explain (NTE) and Notice of Decision (NOD) \n \n \n \n4.​ Upload the NTE Document. \n \n \n \nUploading an NOD:​\n​\n1. From Zoho, click HR, and then click on My Zoho Sign Request. From this page, you should \nsee all the NTEs and NODs you have submitted.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nIssuance of Notice to Explain (NTE) and Notice of Decision (NOD) \n \n \n \n2. To display the NTEs without connected NODs, you can apply the following filters: \n \nRequest Type - Contains NTE \nConnected NOD - is Empty \n \n \n \n \n3. Select the correct NTE, then click on Submit NOD.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nIssuance of Notice to Explain (NTE) and Notice of Decision (NOD) \n \n \n \n \n \n4. Just like sending an NTE, you’ll have to provide the needed information: \n●​ Sanction: The assigned disciplinary action based on the progression of the violation. If the \nsanction is suspension, provide the suspension dates.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nIssuance of Notice to Explain (NTE) and Notice of Decision (NOD) \n \n \n \n●​ Effective Date: Usually set to the local time when the NOD was sent. This option \ndisappears if you choose Void as the sanction. \n●​ Reviewed and Approved by: Provide the same approver as the NTE. \n \n5. Upload the NOD Document.\n❗ Billable Hours\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nBillable Hours Report Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nBillable Hours Report Process \n \nListen to this tab\n1. Purpose This SOP outlines the process for reviewing, validating, and transferring billable hours \n(BH) to ensure accuracy before submitting the final BH template file to Norma. Proper adherence \nto this process helps maintain payroll accuracy, minimize billing discrepancies, and ensure \nalignment between different reporting systems. \n2. Scope This procedure applies to all Workforce Real-Time Analysts (RTA) responsible for \nmanaging and verifying billable hours before final submission. \n3. Responsibilities \n●​ Ensure accuracy in the billable hours data. \n●​ Follow the BH checklist to validate all required fields. \n●​ Conduct a final review before transferring data to the final BH template file. \n●​ Cross-check CRM vs Billable Hours vs Payroll hours to ensure alignment. \n4. Procedure \nStep 1: Obtain the Required Documents \n●​ Download the Billable Hours file template from the designated repository. \n●​ Ensure access to the BH Checklist for reference and validation. \n●​ Gather all necessary logs from Zoho and other reporting tools. \nStep 2: Review Time Log and Break Log Entries in Zoho \n●​ Use the BH Checklist to verify all required checks. \n●​ Identify any discrepancies or missing data and make necessary corrections. \n●​ Clear out all Zoho adjustments, including disputes, overtime, and leave entries. \n●​ Review attendance records to ensure tardiness and undertime are accounted for. \n●​ Identify time log records with missing logout times and update accordingly. \n●​ Confirm that approved OT entries match the PA w BH records. \n●​ Verify overbreaks of 0.25 hours or more and correct invalid entries. \n●​ Ensure break and clinic logs have complete start and end times.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nBillable Hours Report Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n●​ Validate transfer dates to ensure billing accuracy. \n●​ Ensure hours before promotions or reassignments are billed under the correct category. \n●​ Properly classify employees as billable or non-billable as per program guidelines. \n●​ Validate training hours for new hires and transfer them to the Operations Tab once they \nenter the nesting phase. \n●​ Check leave records and half-day absences for accurate deductions. \n●​ Confirm correct lunch break deductions for employees on half or full shifts. \n●​ Ensure that employees on whole-day absences reflect zero billable hours. \nStep 3: Use the BH Review Template \n●​ Before transferring the hours to the final BH template, use a designated review template \nto validate the data. \n●​ Ensure all entries comply with billing guidelines and match operational data. \nStep 4: Validate Incentives and Reimbursements \n●​ Check with Operations if there are client incentives or reimbursable expenses. \n●​ Attach proof of expenses and confirm the correct currency exchange rates when \napplicable. \nStep 5: Validate US Holiday Hours \n●​ Ensure all holiday hours are recorded in the Holiday Tab. \n●​ Tag holidays correctly in the Operation Tab. \n●​ Even if an entire account is on Holiday Leave, names must still be listed with zero hours \nin the Holiday Tab. \n●​ Holiday OT hours should be categorized in the correct Holiday OT tab. \nStep 6: Cross-Check CRM vs Billable Hours vs Payroll Hours \n●​ Ensure that CRM hours, Billable Hours, and Payroll Hours are aligned. \n●​ Investigate any discrepancies, resolve or notate them before final submission. \n●​ Coordinate with Payroll and RTAs if there are inconsistencies. \nStep 7: Final Review and Submission \n●​ Download the billable hours from PA w BH and conduct a final review. \n●​ Complete the QC Checklist to verify all details before submission.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nBillable Hours Report Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n●​ Submit the file for quality checking to the assigned verifier. \n●​ Once approved, send the final BH file to Norma. \n5. Compliance and Updates \n●​ Regularly review this SOP to ensure alignment with current BH policies and processes. \n●​ Implement necessary updates to enhance efficiency and accuracy. \n●​ Conduct periodic audits to prevent billing errors and disputes. \n6. Situational Events and Mitigations \nScenario 1: Missing Log-Out Times \n●​ Issue: An employee’s time log is missing a logout entry. \n●​ Mitigation: Cross-check CRM records for activity logs and confirm the correct logout time \nwith the operations team. \nScenario 2: Incorrect Classification of Non-Billable vs. Billable Employees \n●​ Issue: An employee marked as billable should be non-billable or vice versa. \n●​ Mitigation: Review program assignment records and update the BH template accordingly. \nConfirm with Ops before finalizing the update. \nScenario 3: Unapproved Overtime Included in Billable Hours \n●​ Issue: Overtime hours are reflected in billable hours but were not approved. \n●​ Mitigation: Verify approval status in Zoho Overtime Requests and adjust accordingly. \nCoordinate with Payroll for any necessary corrections. \nScenario 4: Discrepancy Between CRM, Billable Hours, and Payroll Hours \n●​ Issue: An employee’s hours do not match across the three systems. \n●​ Mitigation: Perform a side-by-side comparison and identify the root cause. Escalate \nunresolved discrepancies to Payroll and HR. \nScenario 5: Incorrect Holiday Hours Reflected \n●​ Issue: Employees have incorrect holiday hours logged. \n●​ Mitigation: Cross-check Holiday Tab entries and update accordingly. Ensure holiday OT \nis in the correct Holiday OT tab.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nBillable Hours Report Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nBy following this SOP, the Workforce RTA team ensures an efficient and error-free process in \nhandling billable hours, preventing disputes, and maintaining payroll integrity. \n \nBillable Hours Mapping (Step by Step Process)​\n​\n \nAlbert \nAshley \nBubble \nCitcon \nClearwater \nCoalition \nD&L Transport \nDaily Harvest \nEarnin \nFlex \nHippo \nHitch \nHomebase \nIlluminz \nKMicro \nLytx \nMinoan \nNewport \nPLS T&T \nPapaya \nPrefix \nResident Home \nRocco Fridge \nSharebite \nShef \nSpireon \nTremendous \nWalmart \nWyze \nZenBusiness \nPerfect Serve \nILS\nZoho Related Tasks\n➕ Zoho Discrepancy Process\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nZoho Discrepancy Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nZoho Discrepancy Process​\n \nListen to this tab\nI. Purpose \nThe purpose of this SOP is to establish a clear and standardized process for reviewing, verifying, \nand approving payroll discrepancies to ensure accurate employee compensation while \nmaintaining compliance with company policies and labor regulations. \nII. Scope \nThis procedure applies to all employees involved in payroll processing. \nIII. Responsibilities \n●​ Employee: Reports payroll discrepancies promptly. \n●​ Approvers: \no​ Reviews and validates the claim. \no​  Investigates the discrepancy, gathers the necessary documentation, and \ncoordinates with the Real-Time Analyst of the account. \no​  Approves the payroll discrepancy. \n●​ Payroll Administrator: \no​ Reviews and verifies the accuracy of absence and leave hours filed against the \ndownloaded attendance before processing salary credits. \no​ Makes the necessary adjustments to the upcoming salary. \nIV. Payroll Discrepancy Filing Procedure \nStep 1: Submission of Payroll Discrepancy Requests \n●​ Employees must submit discrepancies within the next payroll period (once the payslip \nbecomes available) to be credited to the next regular salary. \n●​ Discrepancies may include incorrect wages, overtime, deductions, or missing payments. \n●​ Employees report issues via Payroll Discrepancy form.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nZoho Discrepancy Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nHow to file a Payroll Discrepancy: \n \n \n \n2.​ Access the Payroll Discrepancy Form \nand fill out the required fields. (Required \nfields are marked with an asterisk (*).)\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nZoho Discrepancy Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n●​ Payroll Month – The month in which the salary is credited. \n●​ Payroll Period – The timeframe corresponding to the reported discrepancy. \n␀​ First Half – The payroll period covering the 26th to the 10th. \n␀​ Second Half – The payroll period covering the 11th to the 25th. \n●​ Discrepancy Type – refers to the specific category of payroll errors or inconsistencies that \nneed to be reviewed and corrected. Common payroll discrepancy types include, but are \nnot limited to: \n␀​ Unfiled/Pending/Denied Leave – Refers to leave requests that were filed late, are \nstill awaiting approval, or have been denied. \n \n \n \n \n \n❖​ However, if VL hours were filed on time but approved late, resulting in salary \ndeductions, the employee may file a payroll discrepancy, as the delay was due to \nthe approver\'s fault. \n-​\nSubmit a payroll ticket for VL retraction and advise the employee to file for \nSL instead if they still have available SL hours. \n-​\nIf no SL hours are available, you must still submit a payroll ticket for VL \nretraction to credit back the leave hours, as they were not paid, but the \ndeduction will remain.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nZoho Discrepancy Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n␀​ Unfiled/Pending OT - Refers to overtime requests that were submitted late or are \nstill pending approval. \n␀​ Unfiled/Pending Late/UT/OB/Absent – Refers to Late, Undertime (UT), Overbreak \n(OB), or Absences that were recorded incorrectly but were not disputed for \ncorrection. \n \n●​ Hours – Enter the number of hours only for absences or unpaid hours; otherwise, input 0. \n●​ Amount – Enter the amount only if referring to Late, Undertime, or Overbreak; otherwise, \nleave blank. \n●​ Notes – Provide a clear and concise note to help the approver understand the discrepancy \nyou are reporting. \n●​ Payslip Screenshot – A required document for accurate review and validation of the \ndiscrepancy. Without this, the payroll discrepancy request will be disapproved. \n \n3.​ Once all required fields are completed, click the Submit button to send your request for \nreview. \n \nStep 2: Initial Review & Verification of Payroll Discrepancy \n●​ The approver reviews the claim for accuracy and supporting proofs. \n●​ Check the attached payslip to determine the pay period for which the employee is filing. \n \n \n \n \n●​ Look for the deduction in the payslip and ensure that the \ndiscrepancy hours/amount match the hours/amount stated \nin the payslip.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nZoho Discrepancy Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n●​ Review the employee\'s time log records to identify the specific date(s) being filed for. \nEmployees may not always specify the affected dates, so you will need to verify them \nyourself. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n●​ If valid, ensure that all information in the discrepancy form is accurately completed to \nprevent errors during the validation process (via Validate Payroll Discrepancy form). \nTo check, hover your mouse pointer over the row of the employee’s discrepancy and click \nthe three dots next to the checkbox.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nZoho Discrepancy Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \nThe Payroll Discrepancy form will be displayed.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nZoho Discrepancy Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n●​ If everything is in order, the approver forwards the discrepancy hours to the payroll team \nfor validation if the discrepancy falls under any of the following categories: \no​ Late filing or approval of leave; \no​ Late filing or approval of overtime (OT) \n●​ Once done, you can proceed with approving the payroll discrepancy. \n \nStep 3: Approval Process \n1.​ Click the Update Status button. \n \n \n \n●​ The Payroll Discrepancy Update form will show. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nBasic Income: \n8049.60 \nHourly Rate: \n100.62 \n \n \n \nLate from payslip (hrs): \n1.81 \n182.12 \n# of hrs to credit: \n1.77 \n178.10\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nZoho Discrepancy Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n●​ To know if the date selected is not yet previously selected for other discrepancy hours. \n \n␀​ Go to Payroll Adjustments > Edit Discrepancy \n \n \n \n \n \n␀​ Make sure that the columns from Discrepancy Hours to Netpay Adjustments are \nset to 0.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nZoho Discrepancy Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n2.​ Click the Validate button. The Validate Payroll Discrepancy form will show. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n●​ Click \"+ Add New,\" select the correct status from the dropdown list, and choose the \naffected date.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nZoho Discrepancy Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \n3.​ Once the validation is successful, click the Update button. The request will then be \nremoved from the pending list. \n \n \nStep 4: Process for Denying a Payroll Discrepancy Request \n1.​ Review the request \n●​ Verify the details of the discrepancy claim. \n●​ Check supporting documents. \n2.​ Determine the validity of the request \n●​ If the request does not meet the required criteria (e.g., incorrect filing or lack of \ndocumentation), proceed with denial. \n3.​ Click the Update Status button.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nZoho Discrepancy Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \n \n4.​ Update the status to “Disapproved” and clearly state the reason for denial in the \n“Manager’s Note” field. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n●​ The common disapproval reasons include but are not limited to: \n❖​ Incorrect Payroll Month \n❖​ Incorrect Payroll Period \n❖​ No screenshot of payslip is attached \n❖​ Duplicate Request \n \n5.​ Click the “Update” button and the discrepancy filed will be removed from the pending list. \nV. Final Payroll Validation\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nZoho Discrepancy Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n●​ The payroll team validates approved discrepancies and makes necessary adjustments. \nVI. Compliance & Confidentiality \n●​ All payroll discrepancies must comply with company policies and labor laws. \n●​ Employee information must be handled with confidentiality and in accordance with data \nprotection regulations.\n➕ Zoho Break Logs for Outages\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nZoho Break Logs for Power/Internet Outages \n \nZoho has 3 available break auxes for situations where there are Power outages, internet outage \nor system issue. \n \n \n \nSystem Error : Tool issue within the PC unit (client or ECE regardless). Non deductible. \nLost Power : Power / Electricity outage causing non-productive hours. \nLost Internet : Internet connectivity outage causing non-productive hours. \n \n \n \nOffice Based Employees \n \nFor all office based employees with any issues with regards to power outage or internet outage, \nwe’ll automatically add a break log for the respective outage. Take note that none of these logs \nwill be deductible from the employees payroll so MAKE SURE TO TICK the Not Payroll \nDeductible Checkbox.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \n \n \n \n \nWork From Home Based Employees \n \nFor all work from home employees with any issues with regards to power outage or internet \noutage, we’ll follow the flowchart outlined below: \n \n \nOutages TL;DR: \n \n●​ If the outage cannot be resolved within an hour, have the agent report to the office. \n●​ If an agent can report, then add outage logs from the time the issue started to the time the \nagent got to the office. Make sure to not tick the NOT PAYROLL DEDUCTIBLE box as this \nshould be docked from their pay. \n●​ If the agent cannot report to the office, then logout agent and add outage logs from start of \nissue to end of shift. Make sure to not tick the NOT PAYROLL DEDUCTIBLE box as this \nshould be docked from their pay. \n●​ If outage is resolved within an hour, add outage logs from start of issue to end of issue.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \n \nIssue TL;DR: \n \n●​ If issue is resolvable by the IT, then add issue logs from start of issue to end of issue \n●​ If the issue is not resolvable, have the agent report to the office. \n●​ If an agent can report, then add system issue logs from the time the issue started to the \ntime agent got to the office. \n●​ If the agent cannot report to the office, then logout agent and add system issue logs from \nstart of issue to end of shift. \n \nFAQs: \n1.​ Why am I being docked if my PC failed to work properly? This is a company issued \ndevice. \n●​ Upon being deployed to the Work from Home environment, agents have been set with an \nexpectation that should there be any work disruptions brought about system issues or \noutages, they are to report to work in the office. To continue being paid for their hours, they \nhave to continue working in the office. \n \n2.​ Can I use lunch instead of having break aux for outages and issues? \n●​ Yes, agents can request to use their lunch instead for the 1 hour of outage/issue so that \nthey are not docked any additional from their pay. RTAs will adjust their break schedule to \nmeet this request and agents are no longer expected to take any extra Lunch breaks \nwithin their shift afterwards.\n✅ HR Movement\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nHR Movement Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nHR Movement Process \n \nListen to this tab\nEffective Date: February 24, 2025 \nPurpose:​\nTo ensure a standardized and organized approach in processing HR movements, the Workforce \nRTA team will be responsible for submitting HR Movement Forms to Zoho. This includes handling \nall employee transitions such as promotions, demotions, team leader and senior team leader \nmovements, floating statuses, and other personnel changes within the operations team.​\n​\nPlease refer to this \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for HR Movement Notification For…\n \nProcess Flow: \n1.​ Operations Team Submission​\n \n○​ Any employee movement request must be formally submitted by the Operations \nTeam via a Workforce Management (WFM) ticket. \n○​ The WFM ticket must contain all necessary details, including employee name, ID, \ntype of movement, effective date, and any required supporting documents. \n○​ The ticket serves as an official record of the request, ensuring accountability and \ntracking.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nHR Movement Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \n \n2.​ Verification by Workforce RTA​\n \n○​ The assigned RTA will review the submitted WFM ticket to verify completeness and \naccuracy. \n○​ If any details are missing or unclear, the RTA will coordinate with the requestor to \nclarify and update the ticket before proceeding. \n○​ If the movement request is invalid or does not align with company policies, the RTA \nwill escalate to Workforce Manager for further validation. \n \n3.​ Submission to HR Movement Form​\n \n○​ Once validated, the RTA will input the details into the HR Movement Form in Zoho \nfollowing standard processes. \n○​ After submission, the RTA must record the transaction and confirm with the \nOperations Team that the request has been processed. \ni.​\nSpecific Cases: \n1.​ Change of Account: The receiving program’s RTA will process \nthe movement. The Operations team must clearly indicate the new \nprogram and the effective date. \na.​ Wrong Account Assignment: If an employee is moved to \nthe incorrect program, the RTA must correct the entry \nimmediately and inform both the previous and receiving \naccount teams. \n2.​ Change of Account (BENCH or Floating): The previous \nprogram’s RTA will process the movement, indicating the start of \nfloating status and its expected duration. \na.​ Floating Status Extended Beyond Expected Duration: If \nan employee remains on floating status longer than the \nindicated period, RTA should follow up with the Operations \nteam for resolution.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nHR Movement Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n4.​ Follow-Up & Confirmation​\n \n○​ Management team will process the movement request based on the submitted \nform. \n○​ RTAs are responsible for monitoring the status of each request and following up if \nprocessing delays occur. \n○​ Once management team finalizes the movement, RTAs will update the WFM ticket \nand close it for record-keeping purposes. \n \nSituational Events & Mitigations \n1.​ Incomplete or Incorrect Ticket Submission​\n \n○​ If details in the WFM ticket are incomplete or inaccurate, the RTA will notify the \nOperations Team immediately. \n○​ The movement request will not proceed until all required information is provided. \n2.​ Urgent or Immediate Movements​\n \n○​ For time-sensitive changes (e.g., same-day promotions, demotions, or floating \nstatuses), RTAs must escalate to Workforce Management for priority processing. \n○​ The Operations Team must indicate urgency in the WFM ticket for faster \nturnaround. \n3.​ Delayed Processing​\n \n○​ If management team does not update the movement within the expected \ntimeframe, the RTA must follow up within 48 hours. \n○​ Persistent delays will be escalated to leadership for resolution. \n4.​ Discrepancies in Movement Execution​\n \n○​ If an employee’s movement does not reflect correctly in payroll or records, RTAs \nwill coordinate with the management team for corrections. \n○​ RTAs or operation team must report discrepancies within 7 days of implementation \nfor timely adjustments.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nHR Movement Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n5.​ Employee Disputes on Movement​\n \n○​ If an employee contests their movement status, RTAs will refer them to the \nOperations Team for clarification. \n○​ No adjustments will be made unless a new WFM ticket is submitted with approved \ncorrections. \n6.​ Mass or Multiple Employee Movements​\n \n○​ For batch movements (e.g., restructuring or large promotions), RTAs must prepare \nin advance and coordinate closely with management team. \n○​ A consolidated WFM ticket with all employees listed must be submitted to \nstreamline processing. \n \nCompliance & Accountability \n●​ RTAs are responsible for ensuring all movements are properly documented and processed \naccording to company policy. \n●​ Operations Teams must adhere to the requirement of submitting a WFM ticket before any \nmovement is processed. \n●​ Any failure to comply with this process will be subject to review and corrective actions as \nnecessary. \nBy following this SOP, the Workforce RTA team ensures efficiency, accuracy, and transparency in \nprocessing HR movements, reducing errors and improving coordination between Workforce, HR, \nand Operations.\n➕ TL/STL Database Update Process\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nTL/STL Database Update Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nSenior TL/TL Database Update \n \nListen to this tab\nOverview \nThis SOP outlines the process for updating the Senior Team Leader (STL) and Team Leader (TL) \nassignments in the ECE system. The Workforce Real-Time Analysts (RTAs) are responsible for \nensuring that any leadership changes are accurately documented and reflected in the database. \nThese updates help maintain proper reporting lines, coaching assignments, and leadership \naccountability.​\n​\nPlease refer to this for complete process of HR Movement \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for HR Movement Notification Form_9/19/2023\nRationale \nKeeping the STL/TL database up to date ensures: \n●​ Clear accountability in agent supervision​\n \n●​ Accurate routing of reports, coaching, and escalation​\n \n●​ Alignment of leadership with performance data​\n \n●​ Consistency in HR and payroll coordination​\n \n \nI. Updating TL Changes via Movement Notification Form \nFor Single Employee Update \n1.​ Go to:​\n Movement Notification Form – Single Update​\n \n2.​ Select the Employee Name​\n Use the drop-down to choose the affected employee.​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nTL/STL Database Update Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n3.​ Select Movement Type:​\n \n○​ Choose Change of Team Leader​\n \n○​ If the employee is also moving to a new account, select Change of Account as \nwell.​\n \n4.​ Set the Effective Date​\n Enter the official start date of the new TL assignment.​\n \n5.​ Update the Account Field​\n Ensure the correct account is selected based on the movement.​\n \n6.​ Tick the “TL & STL Update” checkbox​\n This instructs the system to update the leadership information.​\n \n7.​ Add Detailed Notes and Supporting Documents​\n Provide context or justification for the change. Upload any related memos, approvals, or \nleadership changes.​\n​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nTL/STL Database Update Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nNote: Upon submission, you will be redirected to the new TL & STL Update Form. \nFor Bulk Employee Update \n1.​ Go to:​\n Movement Notification Form – Bulk Update​\n \n2.​ Select the Site and Account​\n \n3.​ Select Employee Name(s)​\n Choose multiple employees or use the “All Employees” option.​\n \n4.​ Select Movement Type: Change of Team Leader​\n Include Change of Account if needed.​\n \n5.​ Set the Effective Date​\n \n6.​ Update the Account Field​\n \n7.​ Tick the “TL & STL Update” checkbox​\n \n8.​ Add Notes and Supporting Documents​\n \n​\n​\nNote: Upon submission, you will be redirected to the new TL & STL Update Form.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nTL/STL Database Update Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \nII. Updating STL Assignments via Pending Management \nUpdates \nFor Senior Team Leader (STL) Update \n1.​ Go to:​\n Pending Management Updates​\n \n2.​ Click “Add Record”​\n \n3.​ Select Update Type:​\n Choose Senior Team Leader Update​\n \n4.​ Select Site and Account​\n Make sure the site and account are aligned with the STL assignment.​\n \n5.​ Select Employee Name(s)​\n Use the multiple selection feature or select “All Employees” if applicable.​\n \n6.​ Update the “Senior Team Leader” Field​\n Input the full name of the new STL who will be assigned to the selected employees.​\n \n7.​ Review and Submit​\n Ensure all fields are correct and submit the update for processing.​\n​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nTL/STL Database Update Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \nIII. Notification and Approval Process \n●​ All STL/TL updates trigger a Zoho Cliq notification to the designated channel:​\n #Zoho STL and TL Update Approval​\n \n●​ Notifications will include an “Open” button which redirects to:​\n Pending Management Updates​\n \n●​ Approvers:​\n \n○​ All Workforce RTA Leaders and Workforce RTAs (SMEs) are responsible for \napproving these requests.​\n \nHow to Approve: \n1.​ Click the “Open” button from the Zoho Cliq alert.​\n \n2.​ On the page:​\n \n○​ Click “Approve” for individual records, or​\n \n○​ Use “Select All” and click “Bulk Update” for multiple approvals.​\n \n3.​ This action will update the database accordingly. \nReminders \n●​ Always confirm leadership changes with operations or HR before filing.​\n \n●​ Avoid duplicate entries by checking if a change has already been logged.​\n \n●​ Supporting documents must be clear and valid.​\n \n●​ All updates should reflect in internal reporting, dashboards, and performance tools.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nTL/STL Database Update Process – Workforce RTA Team\n✅ Resignation Form\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nResignation/Termination Form Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nResignation/Termination Form Process \n \nListen to this tab\nEffective Date: February 24, 2025​\n​\nDepartment: Workforce RTA​\n​\nScope: This SOP outlines the step-by-step process for handling employee resignations within the \nWorkforce RTA team, ensuring accuracy, compliance, and seamless coordination between \nOperations, HR, and Workforce RTA. \n \n1. Notification of Resignation \nAll resignations must be formally communicated to the Workforce RTA team. Employees must \nsubmit a resignation letter, and Workforce RTA, along with HR, must be included in the email \nthread as CC’d recipients. The resignation letter should be addressed to the Operations Team, \nwho will acknowledge receipt and confirm the resignation details.​\n \nEmail to CC:​\n​\nworkforcerta@ececontactcenters.com \nhr@ececontactcenters.com \nIf an employee verbally resigns or expresses intent to resign without submitting a formal letter, the \nOperations Team should instruct the employee to send a written resignation email. If the \nemployee refuses, Operations must escalate the case to HR for documentation. \n \n2. Filing the Resignation in Zoho\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nResignation/Termination Form Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nOnce the resignation is acknowledged by the Operations Team, Workforce RTA will be \nresponsible for filing the resignation in Zoho under the Resignation Form, following the standard \nprocess. \nWorkforce RTA must ensure all required details are collected before submission: \n●​ Employee’s personal email address and contact number (for HR exit interview and \nclearance processing). \n●​ Reason for resignation or termination. \n○​ If Resignation, the resignation date should be recorded as the day after their last \nworking day or any approved leave availed before their official exit. \n●​ If Termination, Operations will initially use the return-to-work (RTW) letter date. However, \nHR will later update the official termination date to reflect when the physical Notice of \nDecision (NOD) was sent to the employee.​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nResignation/Termination Form Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nIn situations where resignation dates are unclear (e.g., if an employee extends their leave \nor disputes the resignation date), Workforce RTA must verify details with Operations \nand HR before finalizing in Zoho. \n \n3. Additional Employee Information for Processing \nWhen submitting the resignation form, Workforce RTA must include the following additional details \nfor record-keeping and future reference: \n●​ APS Points (Attendance Pointing System record): Include the employee’s latest APS \npoints to document their attendance standing before resignation. \n●​ Eligibility for Rehire: Clearly indicate whether the employee is eligible for rehire based on \ncompany policy and HR assessment. \n●​ Detailed Reason if Not Eligible for Rehire: If the employee is not eligible for rehire, \nprovide a detailed explanation (e.g., policy violations, poor performance, attendance \nissues). \n●​ ACHIEVE Score for the Last Three Months: Include the employee’s last three months of \nACHIEVE scores to provide an overview of their performance before resignation. \nIf there are missing details, Workforce RTA must coordinate with HR or Operations before \nsubmitting the form to avoid processing delays. \n4. Situational Events & Mitigation Strategies \nScenario 1: Employee Submits a Late Resignation Letter \n●​ If an employee fails to submit a resignation letter within the standard notice period, \nOperations must escalate the issue to HR. Workforce RTA should proceed with \ndocumentation based on Operations\' acknowledgment. \nScenario 2: Employee Resigns and Stops Reporting to Work Without Notice \n●​ If an employee stops reporting to work and does not provide a formal resignation, \nOperations should attempt to contact them. If unresponsive, Workforce RTA should \ndocument the case as an unauthorized absence and coordinate with HR to process the \ntermination accordingly.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nResignation/Termination Form Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nScenario 3: Employee Requests a Change in Last Working Day After Submission \n●​ Any request for a change in the last working day must be reviewed by HR. Workforce RTA \nshould not update the resignation details unless HR approves the modification.  \nScenario 4: Resignation Submitted but Employee is on Floating Status \n●​ If an employee on floating status submits a resignation, Workforce RTA must verify if their \nfloating status affects their notice period. HR will determine if adjustments are needed. \nScenario 5: Discrepancy in Termination Date Between RTW Letter and NOD \n●​ If the termination date differs between the RTW letter and the physical NOD, HR must \nconfirm the official date before Workforce RTA finalizes the entry in Zoho. \n \n5. Final Submission & Record Keeping \nAfter completing the form, Workforce RTA must double-check all details for accuracy before \nsubmitting the resignation entry in Zoho. A confirmation email must be sent to HR and Operations \nto notify them of the completed submission by sending the screenshot of filed resignation form. \nIf HR or Operations request any corrections post-submission, Workforce RTA must coordinate the \nnecessary changes promptly. \n \n6. Compliance & Accountability \nFailure to follow the resignation submission process correctly may lead to misfiling, processing \ndelays, or compliance issues. If Workforce RTA submits incomplete or incorrect data, an internal \nreview will be conducted, and corrective actions may be taken. \nTo avoid errors, Workforce RTA must: \n●​ Ensure all resignation-related communications are properly documented. \n●​ Verify resignation dates, termination dates, and additional details before filing. \n●​ Follow up with HR and Operations if discrepancies arise.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nResignation/Termination Form Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nStep-by-Step Process for Accessing the Zoho \nResignation/Termination Form \nThe Zoho Resignation/Termination Form allows RTAs and authorized personnel to \nsubmit and review resignation or termination requests. Follow the detailed steps below to \nensure proper documentation and compliance with company policies. \n \nStep 1: Log In to ECETrack \n1.​ Open your preferred web browser (Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, or \nMicrosoft Edge). \n2.​ Navigate to the ECETrack website by entering the designated URL provided by \nyour company. \n3.​ Enter your username and password in the respective login fields. \n4.​ Click the “Sign In” button to access your dashboard. \n \nStep 2: Navigate to the Resignation/Termination Form \n1.​ Once logged in, locate the left-side menu bar on the ECETrack home page. \n2.​ Click on the \"HR\" tab at the Left panel of the page. \n3.​ A drop-down menu will appear—select \"Resignation/Termination Form\". \n4.​ This will redirect you to the form submission page.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nResignation/Termination Form Process – Workforce RTA Team\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nResignation/Termination Form Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nStep 3: Fill Out the Required Details \nOnce on the Resignation/Termination Form page, you must complete the necessary \ndetails. \nAfter filling out the required fields, review the details carefully before submitting the form. \nStep 4: Submitting the Resignation/Termination Form \n1.​ Once all fields are completed, click the “Submit” button. \n2.​ A confirmation message will appear, verifying that the form has been successfully \nsubmitted. \n3.​ The request will then be forwarded to the HR for further processing. \n \nStep 5: Accessing Submitted Resignation/Termination Requests \nRTAs can monitor submitted forms using the following options: \n1.​ My Resignation/Termination Notifications \n○​ This section allows RTAs to track their own submitted forms. \n○​ You can check the status (Pending, Approved, or Rejected) and review \nsubmission details. \n2.​ View Resignation/Termination Notifications \n○​ This section provides access to all submitted resignation/termination forms. \n○​ RTAs can filter requests based on approval status, employee name, or \nsubmission date. \n \nStep 6: Follow-Up and Documentation \n1.​ Ensure all supporting documents are properly uploaded to avoid processing \ndelays. \n2.​ Coordinate with HR and Operations for clearance, final pay, and exit processes.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nResignation/Termination Form Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n3.​ Monitor the approval status and follow up with the concerned teams if \nnecessary.\n❗ Resignation: No Show/Withdrawn\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nResignation/Termination Form Process​\nFor NO-SHOW and WITHDRAWN Employees\n✅ Daily Schedules\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nDaily Schedule Change Request Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nDaily Schedules \n \nListen to this tab\nRTA Schedule Change Request Approval Process \nObjective:​\nTo ensure schedule change requests in Zoho are validated and approved correctly, ensuring that \nthe schedule is accurate and aligns with operational needs. \n \nKey Takeaways for Approving Schedule Change Requests: \n1.​ Verify the Schedule in Zoho: \n○​ Check the request in Zoho to ensure it reflects the correct, updated schedule. If the \nschedule shows discrepancies, determine whether it was mistakenly recorded or if \nan update is required. \n2.​ Validate the Request: \n○​ Ensure the schedule change request aligns with an approved schedule or is a \nlegitimate update. If the request does not match the approved schedule, verify with \nthe employee or their supervisor for clarification. \n○​ Ensure that any request submitted outside the approved time frame is justified and \nvalid. \n3.​ Evaluate Operational Impact: \n○​ Assess the impact of the schedule change on team coverage. Confirm that the new \nschedule will not create gaps in staffing or disrupt service levels. \n4.​ Check the Timing of the Request: \n○​ Ensure that the schedule change request in Zoho is submitted within the allowed \ntime frame (e.g., advance notice required). Any changes outside this time frame \nshould be validated with the employee for justification. \n5.​ Review Supporting Documentation (if applicable): \n○​ If the request requires supporting documentation (e.g., medical leave, personal \nreasons), verify that it is included in Zoho or attached to the request. \n6.​ Document the Approval or Denial:\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nDaily Schedule Change Request Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n○​ Once the request is validated, approve or deny the change directly in Zoho. If the \nrequest is denied, communicate the reason to the employee in a timely manner \nand update Zoho accordingly. \n7.​ Monitor for Patterns: \n○​ Regularly monitor schedule change requests for recurring issues, especially those \ninvolving frequent or last-minute changes. Address any patterns with employees to \nensure their schedules are properly managed. \n \nKey Reminders: \n●​ Always verify the Zoho request to ensure it’s correctly recorded and matches the \nemployee’s approved schedule. \n●​ Consider the operational impact of any change and maintain adequate staffing levels. \n●​ Keep communication clear and document all decisions in Zoho for transparency.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nDaily Schedule Change Request Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \nStep-by-Step Guide: Accessing the Daily Schedule – Schedule Change \nRequests (Approver\'s View) \nManaging Schedule Change Requests accurately is essential to maintaining workforce \nefficiency. Below is a detailed step-by-step guide for RTAs to access, review, approve, \ndisapprove, and manually adjust schedules in the Approver\'s View of the Daily Schedule \nChange Requests. \n \nStep 1: Log in to ECETrack \n1.​ Open your preferred web browser (Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, or Microsoft \nEdge). \n2.​ Navigate to the ECETrack website by entering the designated URL provided by your \ncompany. \n3.​ In the login fields, enter your username and password. \n4.​ Click the “Sign In” button to access the ECETrack dashboard. \n \nStep 2: Navigate to the Daily Schedule Tab \n1.​ Once logged in, locate the left-side menu bar on the ECETrack home page. \n2.​ Click on the “Daily Schedule” tab at the top of the page. \n3.​ A drop-down menu will appear—click \"Schedule Change Requests (Approver\'s View)\". \n4.​ You will be redirected to the Schedule Change Requests Page, where all pending and \napproved schedule change requests are listed.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nDaily Schedule Change Request Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \nStep 3: Reviewing and Processing Schedule Change \nRequests \nComparing Requests with the Master Schedule \n1.​ RTAs must compare the requested schedule change with the account’s Master List of \nSchedules or Workforce Tool. \n2.​ Ensure the requested schedule adheres to business requirements and workforce staffing \npolicies. \n3.​ If the request does not comply with guidelines, it must be disapproved with a valid reason.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nDaily Schedule Change Request Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nRTA’s Checklist When Approving Requests \n●​ Check the Date Range (From and To): \n○​ Ensure that the new schedule always starts on a Monday of the week. \n●​ Verify Rest Days: \n○​ Ensure that the selected rest days align with the account’s shift rotation and \nbusiness needs. \n●​ Confirm Working Hours per Day: \n○​ Check that the requested shift start time and end time meet the required shift \nlength.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nDaily Schedule Change Request Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nStep 4: Approving or Disapproving Schedule Change \nRequests \nTo Approve a Schedule Change Request: \n1.​ Locate the request from the Schedule Change Requests (Approver\'s View). \n2.​ Click the \"Approve\" button. \n3.​ The system will update the employee’s schedule accordingly. \n4.​ Notify the employee and their immediate supervisor that the schedule request has been \napproved. \nTo Disapprove a Schedule Change Request: \n1.​ Click the \"Disapprove\" button next to the request. \n2.​ A pop-up window will appear, requiring you to enter the reason for disapproval. \n3.​ Provide a clear explanation (e.g., does not meet shift requirements, overlaps with an \nexisting request, or violates business rules). \n4.​ Click \"Submit\", and the system will update the request status as Disapproved. \n5.​ Notify the employee and their immediate supervisor of the decision. \n \nStep 5: Manually Changing Employee Schedules \nRTAs also have the ability to manually change schedules for individual employees or bulk \nemployees if necessary. \nManual Schedule Adjustment Process: \n1.​ Navigate to the Employee Schedule Update page. \n2.​ Select the site, account, and name(s) of the employee(s) requiring a schedule change. \n3.​ Set the effective date range for the new schedule. \n4.​ Adjust the following details as needed: \n○​ Working hours per day \n○​ Shift start and end times \n○​ Rest day selection\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nDaily Schedule Change Request Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n5.​ If applicable, click the \"Part-time\" box to categorize the employee\'s schedule accordingly. \n6.​ Click \"Only for this range\" if the schedule change is temporary and only applies within \nthe specified date range. \n○​ If left unchecked, the system will copy the last two weeks of the employee\'s \nschedule for the next week\'s schedule. \n7.​ Click \"Submit\" to apply the changes.​\n​\n \n \nStep 6: Ensuring Accuracy and Compliance \n1.​ Validate all schedule changes before finalizing approvals. \n2.​ Ensure adherence to company policies regarding shift lengths, rest day rotations, and \nworkforce requirements.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nDaily Schedule Change Request Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n3.​ Regularly update workforce tracking tools to reflect approved schedule changes in real \ntime. \n4.​ Notify employees and supervisors immediately after changes are approved or denied. \nBy following this step-by-step process, RTAs can effectively manage schedule change requests, \nensuring compliance, operational efficiency, and proper workforce planning.\n✅ Zoho Dispute Process\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nZoho Dispute Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nZoho Dispute Process \n \nListen to this tab\nObjective: \nTo ensure accurate and timely review of disputes related to workforce scheduling and payroll, \nminimizing errors and ensuring proper validation before payroll and billables cutoffs. \n \n \nReview Schedule: \n \n●​ ALL disputes must be reviewed before the payroll cutoff (10th and 25th of every month). \n●​ ALL disputes must be reviewed before the billables cutoff (15th and last day of the month). \n●​ RTAs should check for any pending disputes daily to avoid backlog and ensure timely \nresolution. \n \nDispute Review Guidelines: \n \n1.​ Validation of Fields:\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nZoho Dispute Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \na.​ Schedule start and end times. \nb.​ Working hours per day. \nc.​ Log status (Whole Day, Pre-shift OT, Rest Day OT, etc). \nd.​ Rest day tickbox (if applicable). \ne.​ Start and end times. \n \n2.​ Verification Process: \n●​ Cross-check start and end times using the CRM Tool, if available. \n●​ If CRM Tool is not available, validate using the screenshot provided in the dispute. \n \n3.​ Resolution Actions: \n●​ If the dispute is valid, update necessary fields to ensure accuracy and prevent the need for \nresubmission, and then approve the request. \n●​ If the dispute is invalid, deny the request with a brief explanation. \n●​ If the dispute requires further clarification or validation, skip the dispute and escalate it to the \nimmediate supervisor of the disputee for further review.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nZoho Dispute Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \nResponsibilities of RTAs: \n \n●​ Conduct daily checks on pending disputes. \n●​ Ensure all disputes are reviewed and addressed before payroll and billables cutoffs. \n●​ Maintain accuracy and consistency in reviewing disputes. \n●​ Communicate unresolved disputes to the appropriate supervisor for timely resolution. \n \nSample Disputes: \nScenario 1 \n \nResolution: Check the CRM tool for the actual login time. If the agent logged in on time in the \nCRM tool, approve the dispute; otherwise, disapprove it. If the CRM tool is unavailable, check the \nattached screenshot. If the screenshot provides sufficient proof that the agent was indeed on time, \napprove the dispute. If there is insufficient proof, deny the dispute. All employees are required to \narrive early to work to allow enough time to prepare.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nZoho Dispute Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nScenario 2\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nZoho Dispute Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nAttached Screenshot:​\n \n \nResolution: The actual login time in the screenshot and dispute form does not match the stated \nschedule (Start) of 00:00. Approving this dispute would result in the agent incurring 2.5 hours of \ntardiness. Verify the validity of the slide shift and whether it was approved. If the slide shift is valid, \nconfirm the correct adjusted schedule start time. \n \nScenario 3.​\n \n \nResolution: This highlights the importance of meticulously reviewing every detail of the dispute. A \ncloser inspection reveals that the agent took VL during the second half of their shift. However, the \ndispute form indicates a status of Half Day (1st Half). Approving the dispute without correcting this \ndetail would result in the agent incurring 5 hours of tardiness. Instead, update the Log Status to \nHalf Day (2nd Half) before approving.\n✅ Overtime Approval\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nOvertime Approval Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nOvertime Approval Process \n \nListen to this tab\nObjective:​\nAs RTAs, it is our responsibility to correctly review all OT Requests on time. Reviewing all OT \nRequests on time ensures that all approved OT will be credited on the next payday. Correctly \napproving or denying OTs also ensures first-time resolution, preventing resubmission of OT \nRequests, and preventing company losses through incorrect approval of OT hours. \n \nOT Review Deadlines:​\nAll OT Requests should be reviewed before the following cutoffs: \n●​ Payroll Cutoff: 10th and 25th of every month \n●​ Billable Hours Cutoff: 15th and last day of the month \n \nCriteria for Approving OT Requests: \n1.​ Maximum OT Limits: \n○​ Regular OT, including pre-shift, post-shift, and OT lunch, should not exceed 4 \nhours per day. \n2.​ Rest Day OT Consideration: \n○​ There must be a 12-hour gap between: \n■​ Logout time of the previous regular shift and login time of the Rest Day OT \nshift. \n■​ Logout time of the Rest Day OT shift and login time of the next regular shift. \n○​ If the 12-hour gap is not met, the OT will only be considered Regular OT, not Rest \nDay OT. \n \nClient-Mandated OT Consideration: \n●​ If the OT was performed to cover for an employee on PTO, it is NOT client-mandated. \n●​ A program\'s client must explicitly give the signal to solicit for OT before it can be \nconsidered client-mandated.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nOvertime Approval Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \nApproval Process: \n1.​ Verify if the request meets the OT limits and the Rest Day OT criteria. \n2.​ Confirm if the OT is client-mandated based on the guidelines. \n3.​ Check the time start and time end of OTs through the actual CRM logs. If it\'s not available, \nconsider the productivity of the employee. The employee should have agreeable \nproductivity during his/her OT time. \n4.​ Approve or deny the OT request accordingly. \n5.​ Ensure all approved OT requests are reviewed before the designated cutoffs. \n6.​ Communicate any denials with the appropriate justification to prevent resubmission. \n \nCompliance and Accountability: \n●​ RTAs must ensure adherence to this SOP to avoid payroll discrepancies. \n●​ Any incorrect approvals leading to company losses may result in corrective action. \n●​ Consistent failure to review OT requests on time will be subject to performance evaluation.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nOvertime Approval Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n​\nStep-by-Step Process for Accessing the OT Approval Form \nThe Overtime (OT) Approval Form in ECETrack allows RTAs to review, approve, or \ndisapprove OT requests based on company policies. Follow the detailed steps below to \naccess and process OT requests efficiently. \n \nStep 1: Log In to ECETrack \n1.​ Open your preferred web browser (Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, or \nMicrosoft Edge). \n2.​ Navigate to the ECETrack website by entering the designated URL provided by \nyour company. \n3.​ In the login fields, enter your username and password. \n4.​ Click the “Sign In” button to access the dashboard. \n \nStep 2: Navigate to the OT Request Page \n1.​ Once logged in, locate the left-side menu bar on the ECETrack home page. \n2.​ Click on the \"OT\" tab at the top of the page. \n3.​ A drop-down menu will appear—select \"OT REQUESTS - ALL STATUSES\". \n4.​ This page displays all submitted OT requests, regardless of status (Pending, \nApproved, or Disapproved).​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nOvertime Approval Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n​\n \n \nStep 3: Filter OT Requests to Pending Status \n1.​ In the OT Requests page, locate the filter option at the top. \n2.​ Click on the filter and select \"Pending\" to display only the OT requests awaiting \napproval. \n3.​ Review each request carefully before proceeding to approval or disapproval. \n \nStep 4: Review and Validate OT Requests \nRTAs should review OT requests based on the following guidelines: \n●​ Check if the employee meets OT eligibility (e.g., shift coverage, business need, \nand prior approval).\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nOvertime Approval Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n●​ Verify time logs in Zoho to ensure the requested OT hours match the recorded \nwork time. \n●​ Confirm if the OT is pre-approved by the supervisor or required for urgent \noperational needs. \n●​ Cross-check for any overlapping OT requests that may lead to duplicate \nentries. \n \nStep 5: Approve or Disapprove OT Requests \nApproving OT Requests \n1.​ If the OT request meets all requirements, click the \"Approve\" button. \n2.​ The system will confirm approval, and the request status will update automatically. \n3.​ Notify the employee that their OT has been approved. \nDisapproving OT Requests \n1.​ If the OT request does not meet company guidelines, click the \"Deny\" button. \n2.​ A pop-up window will appear, requiring you to enter the reason for disapproval. \n3.​ Provide a clear explanation (e.g., exceeding allowed OT limit, incorrect request \ndetails, or missing approval from the supervisor). \n4.​ Click \"Submit\" to finalize the disapproval, and the system will update the request \nstatus. \n5.​ Notify the employee of the decision and provide guidance if they need to correct \nand resubmit their request.​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nOvertime Approval Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n​\n \n \nStep 6: Final Review and Documentation \n1.​ After approving/disapproving OT requests, ensure the records are updated in \nECETrack. \n2.​ Coordinate with Payroll to ensure approved OT hours are reflected correctly.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nOvertime Approval Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n3.​ Monitor upcoming OT requests to ensure compliance with workforce planning \nand business needs.\n➕ OT Commitment Form\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nOvertime Commitment Form – Workforce RTA Team \n \nOT Commitment \nAll Employee Access:​\nForm:OT_Commitment_Form \nMy_OT_Commitments \n \nRTA Access:  \nPending_OT_Commitments \nAll_OT_Commitments1 \n \nListen to this tab\n1. Purpose \nThe purpose of this SOP is to establish a clear, detailed, and standardized process for filing, \nreviewing, and approving Overtime (OT) Commitment Requests using the OT Commitment Form \nin Zoho Creator. \nThis ensures: \n●​ Transparency – Employees know the exact process and criteria for OT requests.​\n \n●​ Accountability – RTAs and employees follow proper documentation and approvals.​\n \n●​ Accurate Scheduling – Approved OT is reflected in the scheduling system to avoid \nconflicts and overstaffing.​\n \n \n2. Scope \nThis SOP applies to: \n●​ All employees required to file OT commitments.​\n \n●​ All designated RTAs responsible for approving or disapproving OT requests.​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nOvertime Commitment Form – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n3. Context \nThere are cases where operations require employees to render overtime on rest days to meet \nbusiness demands. To manage this fairly and efficiently, all OT commitments must be logged, \nreviewed, and tracked in Zoho Creator. \nThis ensures: \n●​ Proper workforce allocation based on business needs.​\n \n●​ Prevention of unauthorized overtime.​\n \n●​ Accurate reflection in employee time logs and payroll processing.​\n \n \n4. Process Flow \nStep 1: Filing of OT Commitment by Employee \n1.​ Employee opens the OT Commitment Form in Zoho Creator.​\n \n2.​ Employee details (Name, ID, Site, Account) are auto-populated by the system.​\n \n3.​ Employee completes the required fields:​\n \n○​ Date of Committed OT → Must fall on the employee’s rest day.​\n \n○​ Time Limit → Allowed range is 4 to 10 hours only (to balance operational needs \nand labor compliance).​\n \n○​ Start Time → The time OT is scheduled to begin.​\n \n○​ End Time → Auto-calculated based on Start Time + Time Limit; not editable to \nprevent errors.​\n \n○​ Notes/Remarks → Employee must provide context if required (e.g., urgent \ndeliverable, system downtime recovery, etc.).​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nOvertime Commitment Form – Workforce RTA Team \n \n○​ Agreement Checkbox → Employee must tick this to acknowledge that OT is \nvoluntary and subject to RTA approval.​\n \n4.​ Employee submits the request.​\n \n5.​ System automatically sets the request Status = Pending.​\n \n6.​ System notifies designated RTAs via Zoho Cliq channel message.​\n \n7.​ The filed request is automatically reflected in the employee’s daily schedule for \nvisibility.​\n \n \nStep 2: RTA Review and Approval \n1.​ RTA receives notification in the designated Zoho Cliq channel.​\n \n2.​ RTA opens the Pending OT Commitments Report (filtered by “Pending”).​\n \n3.​ RTA reviews the request by clicking Approve/Disapprove.​\n \n○​ Employee details, requested date, and hours are displayed.​\n \n4.​ RTA updates the Status field:​\n \n○​ Approved → Employee’s OT is confirmed and locked into the schedule.​\n \n○​ Disapproved → Request is declined (with optional remarks for documentation).​\n \n○​ Pending (default) → If unchanged, the request remains pending until action is \ntaken.​\n \n5.​ Status update is saved and recorded in reports.​\n \n6.​ The employee’s time log is updated automatically once approved.​\n \n \n5. Form Rules and Validations\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nOvertime Commitment Form – Workforce RTA Team \n \nTo ensure accuracy and consistency, the form enforces the following rules: \n●​ Employee details are auto-filled on load to avoid manual errors.​\n \n●​ Date of OT must fall only on an employee’s rest day.​\n \n●​ Time Limit restricted strictly between 4–10 hours.​\n \n●​ End Time is auto-calculated and not editable.​\n \n●​ Agreement Checkbox must be ticked before submission.​\n \n●​ Default Status = Pending upon submission.​\n \n \n6. Notifications \n●​ Upon submission, RTAs are instantly notified via Zoho Cliq.​\n \n●​ Employees can view the status in their request record.​\n \n●​ Approval/Disapproval actions remain visible in reports for monitoring, audit, and payroll \nalignment.​\n \n​\n​\nStep-by-Step Process: Filing OT Commitment Form in Zoho - \nAgent Point of View \nStep 1: Access the OT Commitment Form \n1.​ Log in to Zoho Creator / ECETrack.​\n \n2.​ On the navigation menu, go to the OT tab.​\n \n3.​ Select OT Commitment Form.​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nOvertime Commitment Form – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \nStep 2: Fill Out the OT Commitment Form \n1.​ Date of Committed OT → Select the date (must be on your rest day).​\n \n2.​ Time Limit → Choose the number of hours (between 4–10 hours only).​\n \n3.​ Start Time → Indicate the time when your OT is set to begin.​\n \n4.​ End Time → This will auto-calculate based on the Start Time and Time Limit.​\n \n5.​ Notes/Remarks → Add any important details related to your OT.​\n \n6.​ Agreement / Acknowledgement Checkbox → Tick the box to confirm your \nunderstanding and agreement.​\n \n7.​ Once complete, click Submit.​\n \n \nStep 3: View Your Filed OT Requests \n1.​ After submission, go to the My OT Commitments tab.​\n \n2.​ Here, you can track the status of your request:​\n \n○​ Pending → Awaiting RTA review.​\n \n○​ Approved → OT is confirmed.​\n \n○​ Disapproved → OT request has been declined.​\n \n \nStep 4: Notifications \n●​ Employees will receive updates through:​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nOvertime Commitment Form – Workforce RTA Team \n \n○​ Zoho Cliq ChatBot notification, or​\n \n○​ Zoho announcement in ECETrack.​\n \nStep-by-Step Process: OT Commitment Form — RTA Approval \nProcess \nPurpose & Context \nThis process ensures every OT Commitment filed by employees is reviewed, approved, or \ndisapproved by RTAs in a timely, consistent way that protects staffing levels, SLA, and policy \ncompliance. RTAs use the Pending OT Commitments for Approval tab to act on requests and \nthe All OT Commitments tab to monitor, audit, and report across all statuses. \n \nSystem Paths (RTA) \n●​ Pending OT Commitments for Approval – where you review and act on new/pending \nrequests.​\n \n●​ All OT Commitments – full registry of requests across \nApproved/Disapproved/Pending/Retracted for analytics and reporting.​\n \n \nStep-by-Step \nStep 1 — Open the Pending Queue \n1.​ Log in to ECETrack/Zoho Creator.​\n \n2.​ Go to OT → Pending OT Commitments for Approval.​\n \n3.​ Apply filters as needed (Date range, Site, Account, Status = Pending).​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nOvertime Commitment Form – Workforce RTA Team \n \nStep 2 — Pre-Review Checks (at a glance) \nBefore opening a record, scan columns for: \n●​ Date (should be the employee’s rest day per policy).​\n \n●​ Time Limit (must be 4–10 hours).​\n \n●​ Start/End Time (no conflicts/overlaps).​\n \n●​ Notes/Remarks (context for business need).​\n \n●​ Duplicate entries (same user, same date).​\n \nStep 3 — Open the Request (Record View) \nClick the request (or the Approve/Disapprove action) to open details. Validate: \nA. Identity & Assignment \n●​ Employee name/ID, Site/Account, role/team.​\n \n●​ Ensure the request belongs to your assigned program/site.​\n \nB. Policy & Form Validations \n●​ Date is a rest day (not a scheduled workday).​\n \n●​ Time Limit is 4–10 hours; End Time is system-calculated.​\n \n●​ Agreement/Acknowledgement checkbox is ticked.​\n \n●​ No overlapping OT commitments or entries already approved.​\n \nC. Operational Fit \n●​ Staffing plan: Is OT required for the stated interval?​\n \n●​ SLA/Volume: Will this OT meaningfully support SLA risk periods?​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nOvertime Commitment Form – Workforce RTA Team \n \n●​ Conflicts: No approved leave, training, suspension, LOA/ML, or preventive suspension on \nthe same date.​\n \n●​ Compliance flags (if applicable): critical NTE/NODs that bar OT (follow your local policy).​\n \nD. Financial/Payroll Alignment (sanity check) \n●​ Within OT cap or policy limits (daily/weekly/monthly).​\n \n●​ Will flow cleanly to Billable Hours and Payroll (avoid split ranges that violate rules).​\n \nStep 4 — Decide & Action \nUse the action buttons at the top/right of the record or in the grid: \n●​ Approve​\n \n○​ Use when all policy, operational need, and no-conflict checks pass.​\n \n○​ Add an Approval Note (e.g., “Rest-day OT for coverage 14:00–22:00; interval \nunderstaffed”).​\n \n●​ Disapprove​\n \n○​ Use when request fails a check (no need, conflicts, not a rest day, exceeds cap, \netc.).​\n \n○​ Enter a clear reason (e.g., “Not a rest day”; “Allocation full”; “Conflict with \napproved VL”; “Over OT cap”).​\n \n●​ Retract​\n \n○​ Use when the employee asked to cancel or you detect a clerical error (wrong \ndate/time/account).​\n \n○​ Add the reason (“Employee requested cancellation via Cliq”; “Duplicated entry”; \n“Filed under wrong account”).​\n \n●​ Bulk Approval / Bulk Disapproval​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nOvertime Commitment Form – Workforce RTA Team \n \n○​ Multi-select only when requests share the same account/site, date, and \noperational rationale.​\n \n○​ Always confirm uniform rest-day status and time-limit compliance before bulk \naction.​\n \n○​ Add a concise bulk note (e.g., “Weekend recovery coverage for surge \n10:00–18:00; 8 hrs per agent”).​\n \nSave the status change to commit the decision. \nStep 5 — Post-Decision Updates (System & Comms) \n●​ Schedule reflection: Approved OT auto-lands in the employee’s daily schedule.​\n \n●​ Notifications: Employees receive updates via Cliq ChatBot and/or Zoho \nAnnouncement in ECETrack.​\n \n●​ Intraday plan: Adjust interval staffing grids and break optimization to incorporate the \nnewly approved OT.​\n \n●​ Escalations (if disapproving critical requests): Inform Ops/TL with reason and alternatives \n(e.g., different time window, different agents).​\n \nStep 6 — Monitor, Audit, and Report \n1.​ Go to OT → All OT Commitments to view everything (Approved, Disapproved, Pending, \nRetracted).​\n \n2.​ Build filters/views for:​\n \n○​ Status breakdown by site/account/date.​\n \n○​ Fill rate (approved vs. filed).​\n \n○​ Denial reasons (capacity full, policy conflict, non-rest day, etc.).​\n \n○​ Coverage vs. need (compare approved OT hours to understaff deltas).​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nOvertime Commitment Form – Workforce RTA Team \n \n3.​ Export or snapshot for weekly Ops/WFM governance and BH/Payroll reconciliation.​\n \n \nRTA Responsibility & Decision Standard \n●​ Every OT Commitment must be closed (approved or disapproved) before the OT date \nto avoid on-shift ambiguity.​\n \n●​ RTAs ensure approvals align with staffing needs by interval and do not create \noverstaffing or policy violations.​\n \n●​ RTAs maintain a documented reason for every decision to support audit, client queries, \nand payroll reconciliation.​\n \n \nCommon Situations & Mitigations \n●​ Not a Rest Day → Disapprove; advise employee to follow standard OT process for \nworking days (if any) or refile on an actual rest day.​\n \n●​ Slot/Capacity Full → Disapprove with reason; suggest alternate time window or \nalternate rest-day if need persists.​\n \n●​ Conflict with Approved Leave/Training/Suspension/LOA → Disapprove; instruct \ncorrection (retract leave or pick another date once status changes).​\n \n●​ Exceeded OT Cap → Disapprove; escalate to Ops if business-critical and policy allows \nexception routing.​\n \n●​ Late Filing (inside freeze window) → Disapprove per policy; if SLA-critical, escalate for \nexception approval and document fully.​\n \n●​ Bulk Needs Change (business demand dropped) → Use Bulk Disapprove/Retract \nwith a clear note; notify impacted employees and Ops.​\n \n●​ Clerical Errors → Use Retract, then advise refiling with correct details.​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nOvertime Commitment Form – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \nQuality & Compliance Checklist (Quick) \n●​ Date is a rest day​\n \n●​ Time limit 4–10 hours; End Time auto-calculated​\n \n●​ No overlaps/duplicates/conflicts (leave/training/suspension/LOA)​\n \n●​ Operational need confirmed (interval coverage/SLA)​\n \n●​ Within OT caps and policy limits​\n \n●​ Clear decision note entered​\n \n●​ Intraday staffing plan updated (on approval)​\n \n●​ Reflected in All OT Commitments views for reporting\n✅ Leaves Approval\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nLeave Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nProcess Guide: Vacation Leave (VL) Approvals \n \nListen to this tab\nObjective:​\nThis guide outlines the process for filing, reviewing, and approving Vacation Leave (VL) requests \nto ensure proper scheduling and adequate staffing coverage. \n \n1. Filing a Vacation Leave Request \n●​ Agents must submit their VL requests in Zoho at least 7 days in advance to allow for \nproper review and approval. \n●​ The request should include the date(s) of leave and any other relevant details required in \nthe system. \n \n2. Review and Approval by RTA \n●​ RTAs check VL allocations for specific LOBs to determine if the requested leave can \nbe accommodated based on staffing needs.c \n●​ VL requests will be approved or disapproved based on the following criteria: \n○​ Approval: If VL allocations allow for the requested leave without negatively \nimpacting operations. \n○​ Disapproval: If VL allocations have been exhausted, approving the leave would \nresult in insufficient staffing, affecting SLAs and overall team performance. \n●​ Once a decision is made, RTAs will: \n○​ Approve the request in Zoho, if eligible. \n○​ Disapprove the request in Zoho, if staffing constraints prevent approval. \n \n3. VL Plotting in Assembled \n●​ After approving the VL request in Zoho, RTAs must plot the leave in Assembled to \nensure schedules reflect the approved leave.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nLeave Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n4. Important Reminders \n●​ Advance Filing: Requests must be submitted at least 7 days in advance for \nconsideration. Late requests may not be accommodated. \n●​ Staffing Considerations: VL approvals are subject to staffing levels and business \nneeds to prevent operational disruptions. \n●​ Communication: Agents will be notified via Zoho regarding the status of their VL request. \nIf disapproved, they may reach out for further clarification or alternative scheduling \noptions. \nProcess Guide: Sick Leave (SL) Approval \nObjective:​\nThis guide outlines the proper procedure for filing, reviewing, and approving Sick Leave (SL) \nrequests to ensure compliance with company policies while maintaining smooth operations. \n \n1. Filing a Sick Leave Request \n●​ Agents must file their SL request in Zoho immediately after returning from an \nabsence. \n●​ If the SL request covers less than 2 days, no additional documents are required. \n \n \n2. Documentation Requirements for Extended Absences \n●​ If the absence is 2 days or more, agents are required to submit: \n○​ A medical certificate from a licensed doctor. \n○​ A fit-to-work validation confirming their ability to resume duties.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nLeave Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n3. Review and Approval by RTA \n●​ RTAs will review and approve SL requests in Zoho based on the provided information. \n●​ For SL of 2 days or more, additional validation from the Clinic is required before \napproval. The Clinic will verify the medical certificate and fit-to-work status before RTAs \nproceed with approval. \n●​ SL used for recreation or vacation purposes shall not be allowed. RTAs who \napproved this requests will be reprimanded.  \n \n4. Schedule Update in Workforce tool (if applicable) \n●​ Once the SL request is approved in Zoho, RTAs must update the agent’s schedule in  \nWorkforce Tool (if available) to reflect the approved leave. \n \n5. Important Reminders \n●​ Timely Filing: SL must be filed immediately upon return from absence. \n●​ Medical Certification: A valid medical certificate with a fit-to-work clearance is \nrequired for consecutive absences of 2 days or more. \n●​ Clinic Validation: SL requests for 2+ days require verification from the Clinic before \napproval. \n●​ Schedule Accuracy: RTAs must ensure that schedules in Workforce Tool (if available) \naccurately reflect approved SLs.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nLeave Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \nStep-by-Step Process for Approving Sick Leave (SL) for Consecutive \nAbsences Exceeding 2 Days \nProper validation of Sick Leave (SL) requests is essential to maintain compliance and workforce \nefficiency. RTAs must follow the steps below before approving any SL request for consecutive \nabsences of 2 or more days. \n \nStep 1: Review the SL Request and Check Zoho Time Logs \n1.​ Log in to the Zoho Leave Request Portal via ECETrack. \n2.​ Navigate to “Leaves for Approval” in the left-side menu. \n3.​ Locate the pending SL request submitted by the employee. \n4.​ Open the Zoho Time Logs to review the employee’s attendance history. \n5.​ Verify if there are any previously approved SLs or absences that require additional \ndocumentation before further approval. \n \nStep 2: Determine If a Medical Certificate (MedCert) is Required \nCase 1: If There Is Already ONE Approved SL and Additional Absences Are Filed \n●​ If the employee already has one approved SL and has further absences pending \napproval, then: \n1.​ The employee must submit a Medical Certificate (MedCert). \n2.​ The MedCert must be approved by the clinic before RTAs can approve the \nadditional SL requests. \nCase 2: If No SL Is Approved Yet, but the Employee Is Absent for 2 or more Consecutive \nDays \n●​ If the employee has been absent for 2 or more consecutive days and has not yet had \nany SL approved, then: \n1.​ The employee must file a Medical Certificate to the clinic for validation.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nLeave Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n2.​ The clinic team must review and approve the MedCert before any SL request \ncan be processed. \nStep 3: Verify the Medical Certificate Approval Status \n1.​ Go to the APS Tab in Zoho. \n2.​ Open either of the following reports to check the employee’s MedCert submission: \n○​ Upload Medical Certificate Report \n○​ Additional Docs Report \n3.​ Locate the employee’s submitted Medical Certificate. \n4.​ Check if the clinic has approved or denied the MedCert. \n \nStep 4: Take Action Based on MedCert Approval Status \nIf the Medical Certificate Is Approved by the Clinic \n✅ RTAs can approve the pending SL requests for the consecutive absences.​\n✅ Update the system to reflect the SL approval and ensure the employee is notified. \nIf the Medical Certificate Is Denied by the Clinic \n❌ RTAs must retract all approved/pending SL requests associated with the consecutive \nabsences.​\n❌ Inform the employee that their SL request cannot be approved due to non-compliance with \nmedical certification requirements.​\n❌ Advise the employee to comply with any additional requirements as directed by the clinic \nteam. \n \nStep 5: Notify the Employee of the SL Decision \n1.​ If the SL request is approved, send a notification to the employee confirming the approval. \n2.​ If the SL request is denied, inform the employee with clear instructions on what actions \nthey need to take (e.g., re-filing MedCert or providing additional documentation).\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nLeave Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \n \nFinal Notes \n●​ Always check for any previously approved SLs before processing new SL requests. \n●​ Do not approve additional SLs without a valid MedCert if the employee has been \nabsent for 2 or more consecutive days. \n●​ Ensure clinic validation of MedCerts before finalizing SL approvals. \n●​ Maintain accurate documentation in Zoho for transparency and tracking purposes. \nNOTE: If half-day absent on CWD, no medical certificate is needed.\n✅ SPL Approval\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nSolo Parent Leave Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nProcess Guide: Solo Parent Leave (SPL)\n \nListen to this tab\nObjective:​\nThis guide outlines the steps for filing, validating, and approving Solo Parent Leave (SPL) \nrequests to ensure compliance with company policies and legal requirements. \n \nProcess Flow: \n1. Filing an SPL Request \n●​\nEmployees must submit a leave application via Zoho and attach a copy of their SP ID at least \nseven (7) days before the intended leave date. \n●​\nIn cases of emergency, the leave may be availed of without prior notice, subject to approval and \nproper justification. \nEmergency Cases: ​\nEmergency Solo Parent Leave may be granted for the following reasons: \n●​\nHospitalization of the child due to a severe illness or injury. \n●​\nMedical emergencies require immediate parental attention. \n●​\nNatural disasters or calamities affecting the child’s welfare. \n●​\nOther urgent circumstances where the physical presence of the solo parent is crucial. \n \nIn such cases, the employee must notify their immediate supervisor and HR as soon as \npossible and provide supporting documents upon return to work. \n●​ The request should include the following: \n○​ Valid Solo Parent ID issued by the agent\'s respective city/municipal DSWD office. \n○​ Ensure the ID is uploaded as an attachment to the Zoho request. \n2. Verification by RTA \n●​ Ownership: RTAs will take responsibility for reviewing and approving SPL requests. \n●​ ID Verification Steps: \n1.​ Confirm that the uploaded Solo Parent ID is valid and clearly legible.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nSolo Parent Leave Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n2.​ Check the expiration or validity date of the ID to ensure it is current. \n3.​ Verify that the ID matches the agent’s personal details in Zoho. \n3. Approval of SPL Request \n●​ Once the verification is complete and all requirements are met: \n○​ The RTA will approve the SPL request in Zoho. \n○​ If there are discrepancies (e.g., expired ID, missing information), the RTA will notify \nthe agent and request corrections. \n \nImportant Reminders: \n●​ Validity of Solo Parent ID: The ID must be valid at the time of the SPL request. Expired \nIDs will not be accepted. \n●​ Timely Filing: Agents are encouraged to submit their requests and required \ndocumentation well in advance to allow sufficient time for review and approval. \n●​ Communication: Any issues or delays in the SPL approval process must be promptly \ncommunicated to the agent. \n \nBy following this process, we can ensure consistency and compliance with company policies while \nsupporting agents eligible for Solo Parent Leave. \nFor further questions or clarification, please feel free to reach out. \n \nStep-by-Step Process for Approving/Disapproving SPL (Solo Parent Leave) \nRequests \nApproving or disapproving Solo Parent Leave (SPL) requests requires careful verification of \nsupporting documents and compliance with SPL policies. Follow the steps below to ensure an \naccurate and efficient approval process.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nSolo Parent Leave Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \nStep 1: Access the SPL Approval Page \n1.​ Open your preferred web browser (Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, or Microsoft Edge).​\n \n2.​ Copy and paste the following URL into the address bar:​\n Zoho SPL Approval Page​\n \n3.​ Press Enter to open the SPL Leaves for Approval report.​\n \n \nStep 2: Verify Uploaded Supporting Documents \n1.​ Locate the employee’s SPL request in the approval queue.​\n \n2.​ Click on the request to review the supporting documents.​\n \n3.​ Ensure the documents meet company guidelines and are valid for SPL approval.​\n \n4.​ If documents are missing, unclear, or invalid, disapprove the request and notify the \nemployee to resubmit the correct documents.​\n \n \nStep 3: Check the Employee’s SPL Usage \n1.​ Open a new browser tab and go to the View Attendance Page:​\n Zoho View Attendance​\n \n2.​ Use the search/filter function to find the employee’s name.​\n \n3.​ Review the total number of SPLs already approved.​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nSolo Parent Leave Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n4.​ Important Rule:​\n \n○​ Employees are allowed only 7 SPLs in total.​\n \n○​ If the employee has already been approved for 7 SPLs, disapprove any additional \nrequests.​\n \n○​ If the total SPL usage is within the limit, proceed to Step 4.​\n \n \nStep 4: Approve or Disapprove the SPL Request \nTo Approve: \n1.​ Ensure the employee has not exceeded the 7 SPL limit.​\n \n2.​ Confirm that the supporting documents are valid.​\n \n3.​ Click the “Approve” button.​\n \n4.​ Notify the employee and their immediate supervisor about the approval.​\n \nTo Disapprove: \n1.​ If the supporting documents are missing or invalid, disapprove the request.​\n \n2.​ If the employee has exceeded the 7 SPL limit, disapprove the request.​\n \n3.​ Click the “Disapprove” button.​\n \n4.​ Provide a clear reason in the pop-up box (e.g., exceeded SPL limit, incomplete \ndocumentation).​\n \n5.​ Notify the employee and their immediate supervisor about the disapproval.​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nSolo Parent Leave Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \nStep 5: Ensure Compliance & Record Keeping \n1.​ Double-check the approval/disapproval entries to prevent errors.​\n \n2.​ Maintain an accurate record of SPL requests and their statuses in the system.​\n \n3.​ Regularly update the Workforce Tracking System to reflect approved SPLs.\n✅ Bereavement Filing\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nBereavement Leave – Workforce RTA Team \n \n Process Guide: Bereavement Filing Process \n \nListen to this tab\nObjective:​\nThis guide outlines the steps for the process of Bereavement Leave requests to ensure \ncompliance with company policies and legal requirements. \n \nProcess Flow: \n1. Upon the passing of an employee\'s immediate family member, the employee must immediately \ninform their direct supervisor or the account’s operations manager through direct/private \nmessages or formally by email that they will be availing their 3 days of bereavement leave. Emails \nmust be addressed to the following: \n \nTo: Team Lead / Senior Team Lead / Operations Manager / Site HR \nCc: workforcerta@ececontactcenters.com \n \nFor easy identification, the email body must contain the following information: \n \nName of Employee: \nAccount: \nSite: \nDates of Bereavement to be availed: \nRelationship to the deceased: \n \n2. Once the email has been received and acknowledged by the Operations team and/or HR team, \nAgent will file for the bereavement leave in Zoho Attendance (leave request form) with the family \nmember’s death certificate attached. \n●​ The Local Civil Registry office may not issue a death certificate immediately and as such, \nfiling of bereavement leave may be done after the dates to be availed has lapsed.  \n●​ In the meantime, it is important to note that the agent will be marked ABSENT and will \nincur APS points on their attendance during the period prior to submission of the BL \nrequest.  \n●​ Communicate to the agent and the ops team as needed that these will be removed \nautomatically once the BL is requested and approved.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nBereavement Leave – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \n \nBereavement Leave Filing Process (Agent Side) \n1.​ Access the Leave Request page through Zoho Attendance\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nBereavement Leave – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n2.​ Fill in the dates that will be availed for the Bereavement Leave, ensure that the dates has \na max range of 3 days. A pop-up confirmation will appear advising filing agent to ensure \nthat they upload a Death Certificate/Embalming Certificate that is stamped and processed \nby the Local Civil Registry Office. Uploading of any other file will be automatic disapproval \n(even if the uploaded image is a literal image of the deceased) \n \n \n \n \n \n3.​ Continue filling in the form with the following details: \na.​ Status: BL (Bereavement Leave; not to be confused with Birthday Leave) \nb.​ Relation: Your relation to the deceased \nc.​ Reason: Bereavement Leave (This is a standard field box shared by SL and VL so \nlet’s just reiterate BL here) \nd.​ Remarks: Notes or any reason in general that needs to be shared.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nBereavement Leave – Workforce RTA Team\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nBereavement Leave – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \n4. Click on the “Upload Supporting Document” button and a pop-up window will show \nrequesting for the document to be uploaded.​\n \n \n \nAttach the death certificate on this box and hit upload. \n​\n \n5. Once the Document has been uploaded, hit submit. Your Bereavement Leave request \nhas been filed.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nBereavement Leave – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \n6. For leave retractions, you may go to the My Records > My Attendance Page and click \non the “Retract Leave” button and inform any RTA to approve the retraction. \n \n \n \n \nBereavement Leave Approval Process (Workforce Side) \n1.​ Access the Leave Request page through Zoho Attendance. Go to the Leaves for approval \npage and filter “Type” to “BL” \n \n \n \nOn this page, make sure to check the attached Death Certificate/Embalming Certificate for \nvalidity. Let us make sure that the date is recent and matches any declared date on the email sent \nby the employee and also that the certificate is processed and stamped by the local civil registry \nor Philippine Statistic Authority. \n \n2.​ Once the certificate is reviewed and validated, click on the “Approve” button. \n \n3.​ Should there be any need to retract a bereavement leave, have the agent retract their filed \nleave and then navigate to the View > Retractions for Approval Page.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nBereavement Leave – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \n4.​ Click on the “Approve” button to retract the filed leave. \n \n \nFAQs: \n \nQ. Am I entitled to 3 days of bereavement leave per year? \nA: You are entitled to 3 days of bereavement leave per qualified family member regardless \nof year coverage. \n \nQ: Who are the eligible members of my family for me to avail this Bereavement \nLeave? \nA: As per the Handbook, the eligible members are as follows: \n \n \n \nQ: If I have unused Bereavement leave, can I have these converted to cash just like \nthe VL and SL credits? \nA: No, as per the handbook,\n \n \nQ: How many days do I have to submit the Death Certificate required from me? \nA: No specified number of days are set for this. Although, you are required to submit the \nsaid documentation as soon as it is released by the Local Civil Registry.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nBereavement Leave – Workforce RTA Team \n \nQ: Can I file for BL as soon as I’ve heard/known about the passing of my family \nmember? \nA: No, you can only file for BL as soon as you have the required documents in hand. \nSubmitting a BL request without the documentation will be an automatic denial of the \nrequest. \n \nQ: If multiple members of my family have sadly passed away, can I use Bereavement \nLeave credits separately for each member? For instance, if two family members \nhave died, would I be able to take a total of six days off—three days for each \nmember? \nA: This would depend on the timing of their passing. If they passed away at the same time, \nonly three days of bereavement leave can be used for both relatives. However, if their \npassing occurred at different times, bereavement leave can be applied separately for each \nrelative, as long as there is a gap of at least three days between their passing.\n✅ Leave Approval Page\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nLeave Approval Page – Workforce RTA Team \n \nStep-by-Step Guide: Accessing the Zoho Leave Approval Page\n \nListen to this tab\nManaging leave requests efficiently is crucial for workforce operations. Below is a detailed \nstep-by-step process to access, review, and approve/disapprove leave requests through Zoho. \n \nStep 1: Log in to ECETrack \n1.​ Open your preferred web browser (Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, or Microsoft Edge). \n2.​ Navigate to the ECETrack website by entering the designated URL provided by your \ncompany. \n3.​ Enter your username and password in the login fields. \n4.​ Click the \"Sign In\" button to access your dashboard. \nStep 2: Navigate to the Leave Request Page \n1.​ Once on the ECETrack home page, locate the top menu bar. \n2.​ Click on the Leave Request tab at the top of the page. \n3.​ This will redirect you to the Zoho Leave Request Portal, where all leave requests are \nprocessed.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nLeave Approval Page – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \nStep 3: Access the Leave Approval Section \n1.​ In the Zoho Leave Request Portal, locate the left-side navigation panel. \n2.​ Find the tab labeled “View” and click on it. \n3.​ From the dropdown menu, select “Leaves for Approval”. \n4.​ This section will display a list of all pending leave requests submitted by employees. \n \nNote: If you cannot view a certain program/account, please reach out to your RTA leader to \nrequest for the access/approver’s view of the program/account.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nLeave Approval Page – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \nStep 4: Reviewing Leave Requests \n1.​ In the Leaves for Approval section, you will see the following details: \n○​ Employee Name \n○​ Type of Leave (Sick Leave, Vacation Leave, Bereavement Leave, etc.) \n○​ Date Range of the Leave \n○​ Leave Reason (if provided by the employee) \n○​ Current Status (Pending, Approved, or Disapproved) \n2.​ Click on an employee’s request to view additional details. \n3.​ If necessary, cross-check the employee’s attendance records in Zoho Time Logs to verify \nleave eligibility. \n \nStep 5: Approving or Disapproving a Leave Request \n1.​ To Approve a Leave Request: \n○​ Click the \"Approve\" button next to the request. \n○​ The system will automatically update the leave status to \"Approved\" and notify \nthe employee. \n2.​ To Disapprove a Leave Request: \n○​ Click the \"Disapprove\" button. \n○​ A pop-up window will appear, prompting you to enter the reason for disapproval. \n○​ Type in a clear and specific reason (e.g., “Insufficient leave balance” or \n“Schedule conflict”). \n○​ Click \"Confirm\" to finalize the disapproval. \n○​ The employee will receive an automated notification regarding the disapproval \nalong with the reason provided. \n \nStep 6: Ensuring Accuracy and Compliance \n1.​ Double-check leave requests before approving to prevent scheduling conflicts and \nensure proper staffing levels.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nLeave Approval Page – Workforce RTA Team \n \n2.​ If the leave request is unclear or missing details, coordinate with the employee’s \nimmediate supervisor for clarification. \n3.​ Ensure the Zoho Leave Approval Page is updated daily to maintain real-time accuracy \nin scheduling. \n \nFinal Notes: \n●​ Always process leave approvals on time to avoid payroll discrepancies. \n●​ If there are technical issues accessing Zoho, report them to the Programming and Date \nTeam immediately. \n●​ For urgent leave requests, ensure real-time coordination with the operations team to \nmaintain adequate workforce coverage.\n✅ Retraction Approval\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nRetraction Approval – Workforce RTA Team \n \nStep-by-Step Guide: Accessing Zoho Retraction Page\n \nListen to this tab\nManaging employee leave requests and retractions accurately is crucial for workforce planning \nand scheduling. Below is a detailed step-by-step guide to access, review, approve, or deny \nretractions using the Zoho Leave Approval Page. \n \nStep 1: Log in to ECETrack \n1.​ Open your preferred web browser (Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, or Microsoft Edge). \n2.​ Navigate to the ECETrack website by entering the designated URL provided by your \ncompany. \n3.​ Enter your username and password in the login fields. \n4.​ Click the \"Sign In\" button to access your dashboard. \n \nStep 2: Navigate to the Leave Request Page \n1.​ Once on the ECETrack home page, locate the top menu bar. \n2.​ Click on the Leave Request tab at the top of the page. \n3.​ This will redirect you to the Zoho Leave Request Portal, where all leave requests are \nprocessed.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nRetraction Approval – Workforce RTA Team \n \nStep 3: Accessing Leave Retraction Requests \nMethod 1: Using the Retraction for Approval Page \n1.​ Inside the Zoho Leave Request Portal, go to the left-side navigation panel. \n2.​ Click on \"View\" and select \"Retraction for Approval\" from the dropdown menu. \n3.​ This page lists all pending retraction requests submitted by employees. \n4.​ Click on an entry to view the details of the leave request being retracted. \n​\n \nReviewing Retraction Requests: \n●​ RTAs must verify that the retraction request is valid before approval. \n●​ Consider factors such as: \n○​ Whether the employee has already taken the leave. \n○​ Whether retracting the leave will affect workforce coverage. \n○​ Any scheduling conflicts that may arise due to the retraction. \nTo Approve a Leave Retraction Request: \n1.​ Click the \"Approve\" button beside the request. \n2.​ The system will automatically update the leave status, removing it from the leave \nrecords. \n3.​ The employee will receive a notification confirming the retraction.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nRetraction Approval – Workforce RTA Team \n \nTo Deny a Leave Retraction Request: \n1.​ Click the \"Deny\" button. \n2.​ A pop-up window will appear, prompting you to enter the reason for denial. \n3.​ Provide a clear reason (e.g., “Employee has already taken leave” or “Workforce coverage \nimpact”). \n4.​ Click \"Confirm\" to finalize the denial. \n5.​ The employee will receive an automated notification with the reason for denial. \n \nStep 4: Alternative Method to Retract Leave via the View Attendance Page \n1.​ Inside Zoho, navigate to the View Attendance Page. \n2.​ Locate the leave request entry that needs to be retracted. \n3.​ Click the \"Retract\" button beside the leave request. \n4.​ The system will automatically remove the leave request without needing further \napproval.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nRetraction Approval – Workforce RTA Team \n \nStep 5: Ensuring Compliance and Accuracy \n1.​ Verify all leave requests and retractions before approval to prevent workforce \nscheduling conflicts. \n2.​ If a leave retraction is unclear, coordinate with the employee’s immediate supervisor for \nclarification. \n3.​ Update workforce tracking tools accordingly to reflect the most recent leave approvals \nand retractions. \n4.​ Ensure the Zoho Leave Approval Page is updated regularly to maintain real-time \naccuracy. \n \nFinal Notes: \n●​ Employees must submit leave retraction requests properly to avoid unauthorized \nschedule changes. \n●​ If any technical issues occur while accessing Zoho, report them to the IT Support Team \nimmediately. \n●​ Always validate retraction requests to ensure they align with workforce policies and \navoid staffing issues.\n✅ Reprocessing Previously Denied \nLeave Requests\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nReprocessing Previously Denied Leave Requests \n \nReprocessing Previously Denied Leave Requests \n \nListen to this tab\nContext: \nThere might be some leave requests that have been denied by the Workforce RTA team due to \nslot limitations or business need restrictions. In such cases, the employee must reach out to the \nWFM RTA team to receive further instructions and guidance on the next steps. \n \nStep-by-Step Process for Reprocessing Previously Denied Leave Requests \nStep 1: Log In to ECETrack \n●​ Open your preferred browser (Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, or Microsoft Edge).​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nReprocessing Previously Denied Leave Requests \n \n●​ Go to the ECETrack website using the designated company URL.​\n \n●​ Enter your login credentials (username and password).​\n \n●​ Click on the “Sign In” button to access your dashboard.​\n \n \nStep 2: Access the Disapproved Leave Requests \n●​ On the ECETrack homepage, navigate to the top menu bar.​\n \n●​ Click on “Leave Request.”​\n \n●​ Click Disapproved Requests or alternatively, go directly to this link:​\n \nhttps://creatorapp.zoho.com/ececonsultinggroup/ece-attendance-manila/#Report:Disappro\nved_Requests\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nReprocessing Previously Denied Leave Requests \n \n​\n \n●​ Filter the page using the employee’s name and date of the leave application to locate \nthe denied request.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nReprocessing Previously Denied Leave Requests \n \n​\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nStep 3: Determine the Appropriate Action Based on Scenario \n✅ Scenario 1: Additional Documentation Is Required (e.g., SL, SPL, BL, etc.)\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nReprocessing Previously Denied Leave Requests \n \n●​ If the denied leave was due to missing or incomplete documents:​\n \n○​ Click the “Update to Retracted” button.​\n \n○​ Advise the employee to refile the leave request with complete documentation (e.g., \nmedcert, SPL proof, etc.).​\n \n○​ Once resubmitted, follow the standard Leave Request Approval process to \nreview and approve the request.​\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n✅ Scenario 2: No Additional Documentation Needed (e.g., VL or previously verified SL)\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nReprocessing Previously Denied Leave Requests \n \n●​ If the request was denied due to slot limitations or business needs, and now there’s \navailable allocation:​\n \n○​ Click the “Approve” button directly from the Disapproved Requests page.​\n \n○​ No need for the employee to refile.​\n \n○​ This is commonly applied for Vacation Leave (VL) or previously reviewed SLs \nwithout documentation issues. \n \n \n \n \n✅ Scenario 3: RTA Error in Disapproval\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nReprocessing Previously Denied Leave Requests \n \n●​ If the denial was done by mistake or clerical error by the RTA:​\n \n○​ Re-validate the request.​\n \n○​ Click the “Approve” button directly without requiring the employee to refile.​\n \n \nStep 4: Notify the Employee \n●​ Regardless of the scenario, notify the employee via Cliq or email of the action taken:​\n \n○​ Whether the leave was retracted for refiling.​\n \n○​ Or if the request has been reapproved.​\n \n●​ This ensures proper documentation and alignment with the employee’s schedule and \npayroll.​\n \n \nImportant Reminders \n●​ RTAs should monitor these reprocessed requests to ensure they reflect in Zoho Time \nLogs.​\n \n●​ All reapproved leaves should match the leave type policy and should not exceed \nallocated limits.​\n \n●​ Any discrepancy must be flagged to the Workforce RTA SME or RTA Leads.\n✅ Attendance Notification\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nAttendance Notification Process – Workforce RTA Team \nAttendance Notification Process \n \nListen to this tab\n1. Purpose​\nThis SOP establishes a standardized process for attendance notifications to ensure proper \ndocumentation and tracking of employee absences and tardiness. It aims to maintain workforce \nefficiency by ensuring RTAs and immediate supervisors are informed and aligned. \n \n2. Scope​\nThis SOP applies to all Workforce Real-Time Analysts (RTA), employees, and operations team \nmembers responsible for tracking attendance notifications. It covers all instances of employee \ntardiness and absences, whether reported through Batphone, immediate supervisors, or other \napproved channels. \n \n3. Responsibilities \n●​ Employees must notify their immediate supervisor or the designated attendance reporting \nchannel (Batphone, email, or any approved method). \n●​ Immediate supervisors/RTAs must log any reported attendance notifications into the Zoho \nAttendance Notification Form if the employee fails to do so. \n●​ RTAs are responsible for monitoring, verifying, and ensuring all attendance notifications \nare logged correctly. \n●​ The Workforce team must track attendance trends and escalate recurring violations when \nnecessary. \n \n4. Attendance Notification Process \nStep 1: Employee Notification \n●​ Employees must inform their immediate supervisor/RTA or use Batphone as soon as they \nbecome aware that they will be late or absent. \n●​ Notifications must be made at least at least 2 hours before and 2 hours after that s/he \nwill be out of work.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nAttendance Notification Process – Workforce RTA Team \n●​ If an employee fails to notify, it will be considered a No Call No Show (NCNS), which may \nlead to disciplinary action. \n \nStep 2: Logging Attendance Notification in Zoho \n●​ All notifications must be recorded in the Zoho Attendance Notification Form by either \nthe employee or the immediate supervisor/RTA if the employee is unable to do so.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nAttendance Notification Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n●​ Required details to be filled out: \n○​ Employee Name \n○​ Channel of Notification (Batphone, Direct Message, Email, Supervisor Report, etc.) \n○​ Type of Notification (Absent or Late) \n○​ Reason for Absence or Tardiness (Personal, Medical, Emergency, etc.) \n○​ Expected Return Date (if applicable) \n○​ Additional Notes (Supporting Documents, Expected Delays, etc.)\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nAttendance Notification Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \nStep 3: RTA Review and Confirmation \n●​ The assigned RTA must review attendance notifications in Zoho and ensure that they are \nupdated correctly. \n●​ Any inconsistencies or missing details should be clarified with the immediate supervisor \nbefore finalizing the log. \n●​ RTAs should acknowledge the receipt of the attendance notification in Zoho to confirm that \nthe absence or tardiness has been properly recorded. \nStep 4: Updating Workforce Tools & Staffing Impact Assessment \n●​ RTAs should update workforce tools to reflect attendance notifications in real-time. \n●​ If an employee’s absence affects staffing levels, RTAs should notify operations to \ncoordinate any necessary adjustments such as reassigning workload or securing \nadditional support. \n \n \n5. Situational Events & Mitigations\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nAttendance Notification Process – Workforce RTA Team \nScenario 1: Employee Fails to Notify \n●​ If an employee does not inform their absence or tardiness within the required time frame, \nthe immediate supervisor should attempt to contact them. \n●​ If no response is received within the first two hours of the shift, it will be marked as NCNS, \nand the case should be escalated to HR. \n●​ RTA should log the incident for documentation purposes. \nScenario 2: Employee Notifies Outside Zoho \n●​ If an employee notifies via personal message, chat, or other informal methods, the \nimmediate supervisor must log it into Zoho on their behalf. \n●​ RTA should cross-check and confirm the accuracy of manually entered attendance \nnotifications. \nScenario 3: Repeated Attendance Violations \n●​ If an employee exhibits repeated tardiness or absences, RTAs should track the pattern \nand notify operations. \n●​ If needed, operations should initiate an investigation and issue a Notice to Explain (NTE) \nbased on the company’s attendance policy. \nScenario 4: Late Submission of Notification \n●​ If an employee submits a late notification (after the shift has started), it will still be logged, \nbut a note should be added indicating the late report. \n●​ The employee’s immediate supervisor should follow up with the employee to remind them \nof the proper notification timeframes. \nScenario 5: Absence Due to Emergency \n●​ If an employee is unable to notify due to an emergency, they must provide a valid \nexplanation upon returning to work. \n●​ Supporting documents (such as medical certificates) should be submitted if applicable. \n●​ The RTA should coordinate with HR if further documentation is needed. \n \n6. Compliance & Monitoring\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nAttendance Notification Process – Workforce RTA Team \n●​ RTAs must ensure that the Zoho Attendance Notification Form is regularly updated and \nreviewed. \n●​ Attendance trends should be analyzed monthly to identify recurring attendance issues and \npotential corrective actions. \n●​ HR and Operations should work together to reinforce the attendance policy and take \nnecessary disciplinary actions when required. \n \n7. Review & Updates \n●​ This SOP should be reviewed periodically to incorporate improvements in the attendance \nnotification process. \n●​ Any updates or revisions should be communicated to all relevant stakeholders. \n \n \n \nUpdates \nNCNS Policy Clarification - 2/13/2025 \n●​ Clarification on tagging of No Call, No Show (NCNS). \n●​ Employees who report to work despite not notifying within the required attendance \nnotification window: \n○​ Will NOT be tagged as NCNS. \n○​ Will accrue APS based on: \n■​ Late arrival, or \n■​ Half-Day (HD) Absence. \n●​ NCNS tagging is automated. \n●​ If incorrectly tagged: \n○​ Employee must file a dispute via the WFM ticketing system for correction.\n✅ Suspension Process\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nEmployee Suspension Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nSuspension Process \nEffective Date: February 24, 2025 \n \nListen to this tab\nOverview \nAll employee suspensions must be properly documented and plotted by the Workforce RTA team \nto ensure accurate staffing projections and prevent unauthorized logins or leave filings during the \nsuspension period. Suspensions may occur due to the following reasons: \n●​ Suspension due to Attendance Pointing System (APS) \n●​ Suspension due to other handbook violations \n●​ Preventive Suspension \nProcess Flow \n1. Suspension Due to Handbook Violations and Preventive Suspension \n1.​ Operations Team Request: \n○​ The Operations team must file a Workforce Management (WFM) ticket selecting \nthe category “Suspension Request” and indicating: \n■​ Employee name and details \n■​ Violation or reason for suspension \n■​ Recommended suspension dates \n■​ Any relevant documentation (e.g., NOD, screenshots, or case notes)\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nEmployee Suspension Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n2.​ Coordination with RTA: \n○​ The Operations team must coordinate with the assigned RTA for the program. \n○​ The RTA will evaluate the proposed suspension dates based on staffing availability. \n○​ Adjustments may be recommended to minimize staffing impact while ensuring \ncompliance with disciplinary action timelines. \n3.​ Filing the Suspension in Zoho: \n○​ The RTA will input the approved suspension details into the Zoho Leave Request \n(Suspension) Form (NOT the standard Leave Request Form). \n○​ The suspension must be properly labeled with the reason and approved dates. \n○​ The RTA must confirm the final plotted suspension with the Operations team.​\n​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nEmployee Suspension Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n​\n \n \n4.​ Preventing Unauthorized Work During Suspension: \n○​ Once filed, the suspension details must be logged to prevent the affected \nemployee from: \n■​ Logging into the system on the suspension date \n■​ Filing Sick Leave (SL) or Vacation Leave (VL) during the suspension \nperiod \n○​ If the employee attempts to log in or file leave, the RTA must notify the Operations \nteam immediately for corrective action. \n2. Suspension Due to Attendance Pointing System (APS)\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nEmployee Suspension Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n1.​ Receipt of NOD for Suspension: \n○​ The APS Point of Contact (POC) creates the Notice of Decision (NOD) for \nSuspension for an employee due to APS violations. \n○​ The APS POC must coordinate with the assigned RTA and Operations team to \ndetermine the appropriate suspension date. \n2.​ Scheduling Suspension Dates: \n○​ The RTA will evaluate the staffing impact and work with the Operations team to \nselect the best dates for suspension. \n○​ Adjustments can be made to avoid staffing shortages while ensuring compliance \nwith the APS disciplinary process. \n3.​ Filing in Zoho: \n○​ Once a date is confirmed, the APS POC must log the suspension in the Zoho \nLeave Request (Suspension) Form, ensuring all required details are included. \n○​ The reason for suspension must be clearly labeled as APS Suspension to \ndifferentiate it from other disciplinary suspensions. \n4.​ Ensuring Compliance: \n○​ The RTA must cross-check employee schedules to ensure no conflicting leave \nrequests exist. \n○​ If an employee attempts to file leave or log in on their suspension date, the RTA \nmust escalate the issue to the Operations team for intervention. \n \n \n \nSituational Events and Mitigation Plans \nScenario 1: Employee Attempts to Work on a Suspension Date \nIssue: The suspended employee logs in despite the recorded suspension.  \nMitigation: \n●​ The RTA must immediately flag the login activity and notify the Operations team. \n●​ If the login was intentional, HR should be informed for further disciplinary action.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nEmployee Suspension Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n●​ If the login was due to a system error, IT/Programming team should be engaged to correct \naccess permissions. \nScenario 2: Employee Files Leave on a Suspension Date \nIssue: The employee submits a leave request (SL/VL) to avoid suspension.  \nMitigation: \n●​ The RTA must reject any leave request that overlaps with the scheduled suspension. \n●​ The Operations team must remind the employee that suspension days are mandatory and \ncannot be converted into leave days. \nScenario 3: Conflict Between Suspension Date and Critical Staffing Needs \nIssue: The proposed suspension date coincides with a period of high call volume.  \nMitigation: \n●​ The RTA should recommend adjusting the suspension date to a period of lower demand \nwhile still ensuring compliance with disciplinary timelines. \n●​ If the adjustment is not possible, additional temporary staffing measures should be \nconsidered to maintain service levels. \nScenario 4: Operations Team Fails to Submit Suspension Request \nIssue: An employee’s violation has been confirmed, but the Operations team has not submitted \nthe required WFM ticket.  \nMitigation: \n●​ The RTA must proactively follow up with the Operations team if a suspension request is \nexpected but not received. \n●​ If the delay is critical, escalate the issue to the Workforce Manager or HR Director to \nensure compliance with disciplinary procedures. \nFinal Confirmation and Documentation\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nEmployee Suspension Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n●​ Once a suspension is plotted, the RTA must confirm the final suspension dates with the \nOperations team. \n●​ A record of all suspensions must be kept for audit purposes, and HR should be informed if \nany discrepancies arise. \nThis SOP ensures that all suspension processes are executed smoothly, minimizing operational \ndisruptions while maintaining compliance with company policies.\n✅ WFM Ticketing\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nWFM Ticketing System \nWFM Ticketing System \n \nListen to this tab\nObjective \nThis Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) outlines the guidelines for the RTA/WFM \n(Real-Time Analytics/Workforce Management) Ticketing System, with a focus on the \nbenefits, processes, and best practices for streamlining RTA-related requests, managing, \nand prioritizing them effectively.​\n \nIntroduction \nThe RTA/WFM Ticketing System is a crucial component of our organization\'s workflow, \ndesigned to streamline the process of handling RTA-related requests, ensuring efficient \nmanagement and prioritization. This system helps us maintain operational excellence by \naddressing issues promptly, optimizing resources, and enhancing customer satisfaction. \n \nBenefits of the RTA/WFM Ticketing System: \n○​ Efficiency and Organization: The ticketing system centralizes all RTA-related \nrequests, creating an organized repository. This streamlines communication and \nensures that no request is overlooked. \n○​ Prioritization: Tickets allow us to prioritize requests based on their urgency and \nimpact on operations, ensuring that critical issues receive immediate attention. \n○​ Accountability: By assigning tickets to responsible individuals or teams, the \nsystem promotes accountability and transparency in managing RTA requests. \n○​ Historical Data: The system archives all ticket data, providing a valuable resource \nfor analysis, trend identification, and process improvement. \n \nTicket Creation Process: \n○​ Request Submission:RTA-related requests can be submitted using the \ndesignated RTA/WFM Ticketing System Form available at the following link: \nRTA/WFM Ticketing System Form. This online form serves as the primary channel \nfor submitting RTA-related requests. The ticket should include: \n➔​ Requester\'s information \n◆​ Employee name, ECE ID, Site and Account. \n➔​ Category \n \nCategory \nDescription\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nWFM Ticketing System \nProductivity Report \nReports focusing on measuring and analyzing productivity levels \nwithin the program \nAttendance-Related Report \nReports detailing attendance records and related metrics, such as \nadherence and absenteeism. \nClient Request \nRequests from clients for specific services, information, or assistance \nfrom the program. \nStaffing Analysis \nReports analyzing staffing levels, including workforce optimization, \nstaffing gaps, and resource allocation. \nBillable Hours \nRecords and reports tracking the number of hours worked by \nemployees. \nWBR or MBR Report-Related \nReports related to Weekly Business Reviews (WBR) or Monthly \nBusiness Reviews (MBR), providing insights on performance and \ngoals. \nShift Bid \nRequests or processes related to employees bidding for shifts. \nIncentive Report \nReports detailing incentives or rewards earned by employees based \non performance, sales, or other criteria. \nOffline Activity Request \nRequests or CRM plotting of event related to activities conducted \noffline requests such as coaching, meeting, training etc. \nSchedule Change \nRequests for changes to employee schedules, including shift swaps, \ntime off requests, or adjustments in working hours. \nAPS-Related Requests \nRequests or inquiries related to the Attendance Pointing System \n(APS) \nMovement Form \nRequests for changes in position, account, promotion, or transition \nfrom non-billable to billable roles and change of team leaders or \nsenior team leaders. \nClient Offboarding Request \n \nRequests for deactivation of client-provided tools and system access \nwhen offboarding employees. \nSuspension Request \nRequests for temporary suspension of an employee \n \n➔​ Detailed description of the request \n➔​ Due Date \n➔​ Attachments such as image or relevant documents.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nWFM Ticketing System \n \n○​ Ticket Creation: Upon receipt, a designated team member will accept the ticket in \nthe RTA/WFM Ticketing System.  \n○​ Initial Assessment: The ticket assignee reviews the request and assesses its \nurgency and impact on operations. They may request additional information if \nneeded.​\n \nTicket Management: \n○​ Assigning Tickets: Tickets are assigned to the appropriate RTA or team based on \ntheir nature. This ensures that the right expertise is applied to each request. ​\n \nFernando Gadong II Suzanne Arnelyn Funelas Jhon Patrick Aseñas Joriz Cruz\nwill assign the tickets to the right RTA based on expertise through this view or the \nRTA can accept the ticket on their own.  \nTicket Assignment Criteria \n1.​ Program Support \n○​ Each RTA is assigned to a specific program or set of accounts.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nWFM Ticketing System \n○​ Tickets will first be routed to RTAs supporting the relevant program based on their \nlogin time and number of pending tickets on their bucket (Maximum pending tickets \nis 6) \n2.​ Category-Based Assignment \n○​ Different types of requests require specific expertise. \n○​ Tickets will be assigned based on the request category and the skill set needed to \nprocess them. \n3.​ Skill-Based Assignment \n○​ RTAs have designated skills and areas of expertise. \n○​ Tickets will be handled by the appropriate personnel according to the category of \nrequest. \n​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nWFM Ticketing System \nRTA Action Item​\nRTA Accepting the ticket:  \n❖​ Once the ticket has been submitted, a bot message from Cliq will be \nreceived indicating a new request has been submitted. All RTAs will be \nnotified. \n❖​ Upon receiving the bot notification, RTAs & RTA leads will review the newly \nsubmitted ticket in the RTA/WFM Ticketing System. RTAs can accept the \nticket from their assigned account requestor within the system.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nWFM Ticketing System \n \n❖​ RTA leads have the authority to reassign or assign the ticket to any RTA \nbased on their expertise or workload considerations. ​\n​\n \n○​ Monitoring Progress: The ticketing system allows for real-time monitoring of ticket \nstatus. Regular updates must be provided to keep stakeholders informed. The \nrequestor can also monitor the progress of their request via the following report \nlink: My RTA/WFM Ticketing Report. Additionally, the requestor will receive bot \nmessages indicating the progress of their request, ensuring transparency and \nkeeping them informed throughout the ticket lifecycle.​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nWFM Ticketing System \n​\n \n4.3. Communication: RTAs handling the ticket should maintain clear and consistent \ncommunication with the requester regarding progress, expected resolution time, and any \nchanges in status. \n \nTicket Resolution: \n○​ Resolution and Documentation: Upon resolution of the request, the ticket \nassignee documents the solution, actions taken, and any relevant notes within the \nticket.​\n​\nRTA Action Item: My RTA/WFM Ticketing Report​\n​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nWFM Ticketing System \n​\n \n○​  Verification: A verification step may be necessary to ensure that the request has \nbeen successfully addressed and meets the requester\'s requirements. \n \nTicket Closure: \n○​ Final Review: A final review of the ticket ensures that all aspects of the request \nhave been addressed, and the resolution is satisfactory. \n○​ Closure Confirmation: Once confirmed, the ticket is marked as \"Done\" and \nclosed. \n○​ Post-Resolution Survey: After the ticket is marked as \"Done\" and closed, the \nsystem will automatically trigger a post-resolution survey. This survey will be sent \nto the requestor through bot chat in Cliq, inviting them to provide feedback on their \nexperience with our RTA/WFM services. The feedback collected from these \nsurveys will be used to assess customer satisfaction and identify areas for \nimprovement in our services.​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nWFM Ticketing System \n \n \n \n \n \nTicket Reporting and Analysis: \n○​ Regular Reporting: Periodic reports are generated from the ticketing system data \nto analyze trends, identify recurring issues, and measure performance against \nservice level agreements (SLAs). \n○​ Continuous Improvement: Insights from ticket data should be used to drive \nprocess improvements and enhance the RTA/WFM operations continually.​\n \n2.​\nEscalation Process: \nIn case of unresolved issues or requests requiring higher-level attention, an escalation \nprocess should be in place, clearly defining the steps and individuals involved in escalating \na ticket.​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nWFM Ticketing System \nTraining and Documentation: \nEnsure that all RTA involved in the RTA/WFM Ticketing System are adequately trained in \nits use and that documentation is readily available for reference.​\n \nConclusion:  \nThe RTA/WFM Ticketing System is a critical tool for managing RTA-related requests \nefficiently. By following this SOP and leveraging the system effectively, we can streamline \nprocesses, prioritize tasks, and maintain a high level of customer satisfaction while \ncontinuously improving our operations.\n✅ RTA Task Tracker\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nRTA Task Tracker Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nRTA - Task Tracker and Correct Tracking \n \nListen to this tab\n \nPurpose: The RTA Task Tracker serves as a guide for RTAs by outlining their daily tasks and \nenabling measurement as a quantifiable metric. \n \n1.​ Working on the task tracker \nThe task tracker will be viewable as a table with interactive buttons on the left side, [Track] \nand [Not Applicable]. \n \n2.​ Each task has a corresponding Expected Hour, and RTAs are required to track each line \nitem accordingly. To allow flexibility, tasks can be tracked up to 59 minutes before or 59 \nminutes after the target Expected Hour. \nEg. Expected Hour = 10:00. You can track the said task between 09:01 to 10:59 \nand still be in adherence. \n3.​ When the task is tracked within the expected time, the [In Adherence] and [Tracked] \ncolumns will be highlighted green. Tracking regardless of expected hours will highlight \n[Tracked] as green and [In Adherence] in red. \n4.​ If a task is no longer applicable to your workload (stopped report, old process) then you \ncan click on the [Not Applicable] button and inform your team lead so they can have it \nremoved on succeeding tasks.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nRTA Task Tracker Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \n \nTask Tracking Impact \n \n​\nTask Tracking is one of the major metrics in the RTA Scorecard - along with Attendance \nand QA. Incorrect Tracking can cause low Task Tracker Adherence and can cost you the \nscorecard incentive. This also greatly affects the team scorecard.  \n \nFAQs: \n \nQ: I accidentally tracked the task outside the allowable hours, can I have this \nedited?  \nA: RTAs cannot edit the task tracker. As such, take extra care with tracking the right task at \nthe right time. \n \nQ: I was on SL/VL on a specific day and my tasks were left untracked. What can I do \nabout this? \nA: You can reach out to your Team Lead so that the said day can be removed from your \ntracker. Only days with no expected productivity can be removed so be very careful with \ntracking on time. \n \nQ: I forgot to track my task since I was preoccupied with an urgent task. I tracked \nthe task as soon as I remembered but I am already out of adherence. \nA: While the tracking itself is not flexible to your task at hand, the task assignment is \nnegotiable with your team lead. If you have a repeating task that you are expecting would \ntake most of your time on specific days, you can reach out to your lead and request to \nhave those hours vacated and move your tracker to another hour instead. \n \nQ: I didn’t have any tasks to track on specific hours of the day but when I refreshed \nthe tracker at a later time, I suddenly saw tasks pop up on those lapsed hours. What \ncan I do? \nA: Reach out to your lead so they can investigate and modify the task accordingly. \n \nQ: I have a task that I am not familiar with or I have a task on a day where I am \nsupposedly on rest day.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nRTA Task Tracker Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \nA: If you are unfamiliar with the task, communicate this with your lead so they can cascade \nor explain the task to you. They can either teach you the task or remove it altogether. If \nyou have a task on your rest day, inform your lead so it can be removed. \n \nRTA LEAD - Assigning Tasks and Troubleshooting \n \n \nTools needed: \n \n​\nTemplate Sheet: RTA Task Tracker - Template Walmart - Google Sheets \n \nOverview: \n \n \n \nThe Template Workbook contains 6 core sheets: \n1.​ Auto-Template: This sheet is the generated template for uploading to Zoho Task Tracker. \nOnce this sheet has been filled up, download it as CSV and upload to Zoho Task Tracker - \nBulk upload.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nRTA Task Tracker Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n2.​ Raw: This sheet contains the raw data that will be used by the auto-template. All \nmodification will be done here. \n \n3.​ Manual Template: On instances where Auto-Template is not working, we can use the \nManual Template to generate the CSV file for uploading in Zoho. \n \n4.​ RTA Roster: This sheet is run by an importrange formula linked to the primary roster sheet \nof the RTA team. Do not modify this sheet. \n \n5.​ Accounts: This sheet lists all accounts that the RTAs are handling. Add any account that is \nneeded for the tracker on this sheet.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nRTA Task Tracker Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n6.​ Category: This sheet lists all the tasks that the RTAs are handling. It also has an adjacent \ncolumns that identifies the category of the task and description of the task. Modify this \nsheet to suit the tasks that the RTA is handling.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nRTA Task Tracker Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \nAssigning Tasks: \n \n1.​ Checking the basics: \na.​ First ensure that the RTA we’re assigning the task to is already in the roster \n​\n \n \nb.​ Ensure that the Account is also listed under the accounts sheet \n \n \nc.​ Finally, ensure that the tasks, its categories and descriptions are encoded correctly \nin the Category Sheet\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nRTA Task Tracker Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \n2.​ In the Raw Sheet, Modify columns B, C, E and F to M to assign tasks \n \na.​ Column B: The Task to be assigned to the agent. This automatically updates \ncolumn A with the corresponding category as well. \nb.​ Column C: The time (24 hours time format) that the task should be tracked. This \nwill automatically convert column D into a 24 hour time value format. \nc.​ Column E: The redundancy of the task. Should be set to Weekly if task is done \nonce a week, Monthly if one a Month, etc. \nd.​ Columns F to L: These fields have a header with the days of the week. Assign an \nRTA Name on these columns to designate which RTA works on the task on which \nday of the week. \ne.​ Column M: Account Name. This ensures that the RTA is aware that the task their \nworking on is for this specific account. \n \n3.​ Once the Raw Sheet has been completed, the Auto-Template sheet will be automatically \nfilled up. Make sure that the Column J (Date Range Confirmation) is set to Indefinite so \nthat the task will repeat continuously. Also modify the From and To date ranges to set \nwhen the task will be running (Columns H:I)\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nRTA Task Tracker Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \n4.​ Once the Auto-Template sheet is confirmed to be accurate, download the file as CSV by \nclicking on the File Menu > Download > Comma Separated Values (.csv) \n \n \n5.​ Once downloaded, we are ready to upload this to Zoho. Go to Zoho Task Tracker > Import \nTasks.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nRTA Task Tracker Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \n6.​ On the Import Task Page, click on the Sandwich menu (top right) and click on Import. \n \n \nDrag and Drop the downloaded CSV file on this page.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nRTA Task Tracker Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \n \n7.​ Once the file has been imported, click on the warning icon beside the From field at the \nright side menu (Columns Mapping).\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nRTA Task Tracker Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \nA warning will pop-up. Click “Standardize”. You will have to repeat this process for the “To” \ncolumn field. \n \n \n8.​ In the Refine Data Menu, make sure that the Date format is correct. Standard is MDY.Click \nApply.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nRTA Task Tracker Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n         \n \n \n9.​ Repeat the same with the “To” Column. \n \n10.​Next click on the close button [x] at the top right of the Columns Mapping Menu and click \non the Settings Button.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nRTA Task Tracker Process – Workforce RTA Team\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nRTA Task Tracker Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n11.​Always ensure that the “Execute Scripts” tickbox is checked on the settings page.\n \n \n12.​Click “Import” and wait for it to finish. Confirm that the tasks are assigned by checking on \nthe RTA Daily Task Tracker page of Zoho Task Tracker and have your RTA check on their \nend as well.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \nhttps://www.ececonsultinggroup.com/ \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Workforce Real-Time Analyst (RTA) \nRTA Task Tracker Process – Workforce RTA Team \n \n \nTroubleshooting & FAQs: \n \nQ: I uploaded the tasks and checked the Daily Task Tracker page but couldn’t find \nthe uploaded task. \nA: Make sure that when you uploaded the task, you ticked the “Execute Scripts” tick box in \nthe settings page. \n \nQ: An RTA reported to me that there was a task that they are not supposed to do on \ntheir tracker. How do I remove it? \nA: On the Daily Task Tracker Page, you can tick the box on the left side of the task and \nclick on Discontinue. This stops the tracker from generating the same task on the \nsucceeding days/months. After Discontinuing the task, click delete. \n \n \nFor any other questions that are not in this FAQs, please reach out to any Team leads or \ndirectly to Ms. Imee Compra for assistance.",
  Support: "💬 Crucial Conversation Prep Worksheet \nScenario Example: A team member has been spreading rumors or gossip, creating confusion, \ntension, or negativity within the team. \n  \n1. Lead with the Heart \nPurpose: Approach this conversation with empathy and clarity. Rumors often come from \nmisunderstanding or frustration, not intentional harm. Your goal is to protect trust, reinforce \nprofessionalism, and offer support — not blame. \nExample Reflection: “I want to approach this believing there may be confusion or emotions \ndriving this behavior. My goal is to help them understand the impact on trust and team unity \nwhile offering support.” \nPrompts: \n• How do I stay neutral and avoid sounding confrontational? \n• How can I make it clear my goal is support, not punishment? \n• How can I connect their behavior to the impact on culture and morale? \nExample Opening Line: “I wanted to talk with you about something that may feel uncomfortable, \nbut it’s important for maintaining trust and unity on the team.” \n__________________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________________ \n🎯 2. Define the Ideal Outcome \nExample: “By the end of the conversation, I want the team member to understand the impact of \nspreading unverified information and commit to using appropriate communication channels \ngoing forward.” \n__________________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________________\n⚠️ 3. Define the Negative Outcome to Avoid \nExample: “Avoid making them feel accused, embarrassed, or attacked. Avoid leaving any \nambiguity about expectations related to professionalism and communication.” \n__________________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________________ \n📊 4. Gather the Facts \nExample Notes: \n• Teammates reported hearing the employee repeat unverified information. \n• Gossip created worry, confusion, or tension within the team. \n• Leadership had to clarify misinformation. \n• No previous discussions on this issue are documented. \n__________________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________________ \n🤝 5. Gain Buy-In After Presenting the Facts \nPurpose: Invite their perspective. Gossip often stems from assumptions or feeling out of the \nloop. This is an opportunity to build trust. \nSample Questions: \n• Can you share what you heard and why you decided to share it? \n• Where did that information come from? \n• Were you aware of the impact it had on the team? \n• How can we prevent misunderstandings like this in the future? \n__________________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________________ \n🧭 6. Conversation Plan (Outline)\n1. Open with empathy and purpose. \n“I appreciate you taking time to talk with me. I want to address something that’s affecting team \ntrust and clarity.” \n 2. Acknowledge their intentions. \n“I don’t believe you intended to create harm. Sometimes we share information thinking we’re \nhelping others.” \n 3. Present the facts calmly. \n“There have been instances where unverified information was shared, and it caused confusion \nand concern.” \n4. Gain buy-in after presenting the facts. \n“Can you help me understand what led to sharing that information?” \n 5. Collaborate on the way forward. \n“Going forward, let’s keep communication direct. Bring questions or unclear topics to leadership \nfirst so we can help clarify.” \n 6. Reaffirm trust and partnership. \n“I appreciate your openness. You’re an important part of this team, and I want to support you in \nbeing a positive influence here.” \n__________________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________________ \n📝 7. Reflection (After the Meeting) \n• Did I stay neutral and compassionate? \n• Did I clearly explain the impact of gossip on trust and morale? \n• Did the team member feel heard and respected? \n• Did I reinforce expectations without being harsh? \n• What went well? What could improve next time? \n__________________________________________________________________\n__________________________________________________________________\n\n💬 Crucial Conversation Prep Worksheet \nScenario Example: Team member expresses resistance, frustration, or negativity toward \nschedule changes  \n  \n1. Lead with the Heart \nPurpose: Approach this conversation with empathy and understanding. Schedule changes affect \nroutines, families, and daily life. Acknowledge their feelings while providing clear context for why \nthe change is needed. \nExample Reflection: “Schedule changes can be frustrating and disruptive. I want to approach \nthis conversation with care while explaining the ‘why’ behind the change and supporting them \nthrough the transition.” \nPrompts: \n• How can I acknowledge their feelings without over-promising? \n• How do I explain the business need clearly and transparently? \n• How can I reinforce fairness and consistency? \nExample Opening Line: “I understand that this schedule change may impact you. I’d like to talk \nthrough why it’s needed and how I can support you through it.” \n____________________________________________________________________________\n__ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n__ \n🎯 2. Define the Ideal Outcome \nExample: “By the end of the discussion, the team member understands why the schedule \nchange is needed, feels heard about their concerns, and is willing to adapt with the assurance \nof leadership support.” \n____________________________________________________________________________\n__\n____________________________________________________________________________\n__ \n⚠️ 3. Define the Negative Outcome to Avoid \nExample: “Avoid creating the impression that schedules change randomly or unfairly. Avoid \ntriggering defensiveness or discouragement. Avoid leaving the employee feeling unheard or \nunimportant.” \n____________________________________________________________________________\n__ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n__ \n📊 4. Gather the Facts \nExample Notes: \n• Schedule changes are driven by arrival patterns, absenteeism, attrition, and client service level \nrequirements. \n• Leadership avoids making changes more than quarterly unless necessary. \n• Schedule bids reward top performers with the first choice of preferred shifts. We operate on \nmeritocracy.  \n• The new schedule was applied consistently and communicated in advance. \n____________________________________________________________________________\n__ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n__ \n🤝 5. Gain Buy-In After Presenting the Facts \nPurpose: Provide space for the team member to express concerns, challenges, or personal \nimpacts. Listening builds trust and partnership. \nSample Questions: \n• What part of this change is most challenging for you?\n• Does this shift affect responsibilities outside work? \n• Is there anything we can adjust or support during this transition? \n• Have you experienced schedule changes before? What helped you adapt? \n____________________________________________________________________________\n__ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n__ \n🧭 6. Conversation Plan (Outline) \n1. Open with empathy and purpose. \n“I know schedule changes can be disruptive. I want to walk through why this change is \nnecessary and support you through it.” \n 2. Acknowledge their feelings. \n“Your reaction makes sense — routines matter, and changes can be difficult.” \n 3. Present the facts calmly. \n“Based on arrival patterns, absenteeism/attrition, and client needs, we must realign schedules to \nmaintain service levels. We avoid frequent changes, but this one is necessary.” \n“Our schedule bid process rewards performance by allowing top performers first choice, \nensuring fairness and consistency.” \n 4. Gain buy-in. \n“Tell me which parts of this shift concern you the most. I want to understand your situation fully.” \n 5. Collaborate on the way forward. \n“Let’s talk about how we can support you in adapting to this schedule.” \n 6. Reaffirm trust and partnership. \n“I appreciate your willingness to adapt. You’re a valued part of the team, and we’ll support you \nthrough this change.” \n____________________________________________________________________________\n__\n____________________________________________________________________________\n__ \n📝 7. Reflection (After the Meeting) \n• Did I acknowledge their feelings respectfully? \n• Did I clearly explain the business reasons for the change? \n• Did they leave feeling heard and supported? \n• Did I avoid making commitments I cannot deliver? \n• What follow-up actions are needed? \n____________________________________________________________________________\n__ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n__\n\n💬 Crucial Conversation Prep Worksheet \nScenario Example: Personal hygiene concern impacting team comfort or workplace \nprofessionalism. \n  \n1. Lead with the Heart \nPurpose: Approach this deeply sensitive topic with compassion, discretion, and respect. The \ngoal is to address the issue privately while preserving dignity and offering support. \nExample Reflection: “This conversation could be embarrassing for them, so I need to approach \nit with care and empathy.” \nPrompts: \n• How do I preserve their dignity? \n• How do I remain compassionate while being clear? \n• How do I ensure privacy and protect trust? \nExample Opening Line: “I want to talk with you privately about something sensitive, and I’m \nsharing it because I want you to feel supported and successful here.” \n__________________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________________ \n🎯 2. Define the Ideal Outcome \nExample: “They understand the concern without feeling embarrassed, know I’m here to support \nthem, and feel empowered to take steps to address the issue.” \n__________________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________________ \n⚠️ 3. Define the Negative Outcome to Avoid\nExample: “Avoid shame, embarrassment, defensiveness, or damaging trust. Avoid allowing the \nissue to persist and further impact team morale.” \n__________________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________________ \n📊 4. Gather the Facts \nExample Notes: \n• Multiple discreet concerns raised by peers. \n• Noticeable odor on multiple occasions. \n• Issue appears consistent, not a single occurrence. \n• No prior conversations documented. \n__________________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________________ \n🤝 5. Gain Buy-In After Presenting the Facts \nPurpose: Allow them space to respond. They may be unaware, embarrassed, or dealing with \npersonal/medical issues. \nSample Questions: \n• Has anything changed recently that I should be aware of? \n• Is there anything you need from us to help you address this? \n• Are you aware this has been an issue? \n• How can I support you moving forward? \n__________________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________________ \n🧭 6. Conversation Plan (Outline) \n1. Open with empathy and privacy.\n“I appreciate you taking a moment to talk privately. I want to discuss something sensitive \nbecause I care about your comfort and success.” \n2. Acknowledge their feelings. \n“I know this may feel uncomfortable, and I want you to know I’m sharing this with full respect.” \n 3. Present the facts gently. \n“There have been a few occasions where there has been a noticeable odor. I’m not sure if \nyou’ve been aware, so I wanted to share this privately.” \n 4. Gain buy-in after presenting the facts. \n“Is this something you’ve noticed? I want to understand from your perspective.” \n 5. Collaborate on the way forward. \n“Let’s talk about what support you might need and the steps we can take together.” \n 6. Reaffirm trust and partnership. \n“Thank you for being open. I’m here to support you, and I appreciate your professionalism.” \n__________________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________________ \n📝 7. Reflection (After the Meeting) \n• Did I preserve their dignity and privacy? \n• Did I remain compassionate and non-judgmental? \n• Did I clearly communicate the concern without exaggeration? \n• Did I allow space for their perspective? \n• What went well? What can improve next time? \n__________________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________________\n\n💬 Crucial Conversation Prep Worksheet \nScenario Example: Public displays of affection (PDA) or romantic relationships between peers \nwithin the team. \n1. Lead with the Heart \nPurpose: Approach this conversation with care, discretion, and professionalism. The goal is to \nmaintain a comfortable and respectful environment for everyone while protecting the reputation \nof those involved. \nExample Reflection: “I want to handle this with empathy and respect. I care about both \nindividuals and the team’s comfort, and I want to reinforce professionalism without judgment or \nembarrassment.” \nPrompts: \n• How can I make sure the tone is caring and neutral? \n• How can I protect the dignity of those involved while maintaining standards? \n• How can I frame the conversation around team impact rather than personal behavior? \nExample Opening Line: “I wanted to speak with you about something that’s sensitive but \nimportant. My intent is to make sure we maintain a positive, professional environment for \neveryone.” \n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________ \n🎯 2. Define the Ideal Outcome \nPurpose: Visualize what success looks like after the conversation. \nExample: “By the end of this discussion, I want both team members to understand that while \npersonal relationships aren’t prohibited, public displays of affection can make others \nuncomfortable and distract from the professional atmosphere we’re all responsible for \nmaintaining.” \n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________ \n⚠️ 3. Define the Negative Outcome to Avoid \nPurpose: Identify what could go wrong if the situation isn’t addressed properly.\nExample: “I want to avoid embarrassment or feelings of being singled out. I also want to avoid \nletting the issue go unaddressed, which could lead to gossip, tension, or a perception that \nunprofessional behavior is tolerated.” \n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________ \n📊 4. Gather the Facts \nPurpose: Ensure that your feedback is factual, not based on rumor or bias. \nExample Notes: \n• PDA observed during breaks or in the production area (e.g., hugging, hand-holding, extended \nclose contact). \n• Team members have appeared visibly uncomfortable or distracted. \n• No prior discussions or warnings have been issued. \n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________ \n🤝 5. Gain Buy-In After Presenting the Facts \nPurpose: Allow peers to share their perspective and show understanding of the impact. This \nbuilds trust and cooperation instead of defensiveness. \nExample Approach: “Now that I’ve shared what’s been noticed, I’d like to hear how you see it. \nMy intent isn’t to judge — it’s to make sure we all understand how this might affect others on \nthe team.” \nSample Questions to Ask: \n• Do you understand how this could make others uncomfortable? \n• What are your thoughts on how we can keep things professional at work? \n• Would you be willing to be more mindful about how things appear to others? \n• Is there anything that could make this easier for you both to manage? \n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________ \n🧭 6. Conversation Plan (Outline) \nExample Flow and Dialogue: \n1. Open with empathy and purpose\n“I wanted to talk about something that may feel awkward, but it’s important for maintaining our \nteam’s comfort and professionalism.” \n2. Acknowledge their feelings \n“I understand that relationships can naturally develop at work — that’s not unusual. I just want \nto ensure that what happens in the workplace remains professional and comfortable for \neveryone.” \n3. Present the facts calmly \n“There have been a few observations of physical affection between you two in shared \nworkspaces, and a few team members have mentioned it makes them uncomfortable.” \n4. Gain buy-in after presenting the facts \n“Do you see how this might come across to others, even if the intent isn’t negative?” \n5. Collaborate on the way forward \n“Let’s agree to keep personal interactions appropriate for the workplace. That protects both of \nyou and the comfort of everyone around.” \n6. Reaffirm trust and partnership \n“I appreciate your understanding and maturity in addressing this. I trust that you’ll handle it with \ndiscretion and professionalism moving forward.” \n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________\n____________________________________________________________________________ \n📝 7. Reflection (After the Meeting) \n• Did I approach the conversation respectfully and neutrally? \n• Did I avoid language that sounded judgmental or personal? \n• Did both individuals leave feeling respected and clear on expectations? \n• Did I ensure confidentiality and privacy were maintained? \n• What went well? What could I improve next time? \n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________\n____________________________________________________________________________\n\n💬 Crucial Conversation Prep Worksheet \nScenario Example: Frontline team member escalates a concern directly to the client partner \nwithout informing internal support staff or giving leadership a chance to address it first. \n 1. Lead with the Heart \nPurpose: Begin from a place of understanding and shared accountability. The goal is not to \nreprimand but to rebuild trust, reinforce communication channels, and demonstrate that \nconcerns are heard and acted upon internally. \nExample Reflection: “I want to use this as an opportunity to strengthen trust and communication \nwith this team member. If they felt the need to go directly to the client, that’s a signal that I may \nnot have made it clear that their voice matters and that we act on feedback.” \nPrompts: \n• What might have led them to feel unheard or unsure of our follow-through? \n• How can I show that I’m more interested in understanding than correcting? \n• How can this conversation demonstrate that leadership is listening and accountable? \nExample Opening Line: “I wanted to talk about how the recent concern was raised. My goal isn’t \nto point fingers — it’s to understand what led to that and make sure you feel confident bringing \nissues directly to us in the future.” \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n🎯 2. Define the Ideal Outcome \nPurpose: Clarify what a positive result looks like for both the team member and the organization. \nExample: “By the end of this conversation, I want the team member to feel heard, understood, \nand assured that leadership values transparency and action. They should leave knowing that \nraising concerns internally first is the best way to resolve issues quickly and effectively.” \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________\n⚠️ 3. Define the Negative Outcome to Avoid \nPurpose: Identify what could go wrong if the issue is mishandled. \nExample: “I want to avoid making the employee feel defensive or silenced. I also want to avoid \nreinforcing the idea that going directly to the client is acceptable. Finally, I want to prevent \ndamage to our client relationship or the perception that our internal communication is weak.” \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n📊 4. Gather the Facts \nPurpose: Base the conversation on verified details, not assumptions. Know exactly what was \nsaid, to whom, and what led up to it. \nExample Notes: \n• Team member directly emailed or messaged the client partner regarding an internal process or \nconcern. \n• The issue could have been resolved internally through the immediate supervisor or support \nteam. \n• The client expressed surprise or concern that internal leadership wasn’t aware of the issue. \n• There’s no prior history of similar behavior from this team member. \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n🤝 5. Gain Buy-In After Presenting the Facts \nPurpose: Give the team member space to share their side. Seek understanding before reinforcing \nboundaries. \nExample Approach: “I can imagine you had good intentions in wanting the issue resolved quickly. \nCan you walk me through what led you to bring it directly to the client?” \nSample Questions to Ask: \n• What made you feel like bringing this up to the client was the best option? \n• Have there been times when you felt your concerns weren’t heard internally?\n• What could I or the team have done differently to make it easier for you to come to us first? \n• How can we rebuild that trust so you feel confident raising things internally next time? \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n🧭 6. Conversation Plan (Outline) \nExample Flow and Dialogue: \n1. Open with empathy and purpose \n“I wanted to talk about the recent situation where a concern was brought directly to the client. \nMy goal is to understand what led to it and make sure you always feel comfortable bringing \nthings to us first.” \n2. Acknowledge their intentions \n“I know you likely did this because you care about the customer experience and wanted to get \nthings fixed quickly. I appreciate that initiative — and I also want to help channel that through \nthe right process.” \n3. Present the facts calmly \n“The concern you raised was valid, but it caught the client off guard because our internal team \nwasn’t aware. When issues go straight to them, it can make it seem like we’re not aligned or \nresponsive as a team.” \n4. Gain buy-in after presenting the facts \n“Can you share what led you to take that route? Was there a point where you felt your concern \nwasn’t being heard?” \n5. Collaborate on the way forward \n“Going forward, I’d like us to re-establish that trust. If something feels urgent, bring it to me \ndirectly. My commitment is to ensure your feedback is acted on swiftly and communicated \nclearly to you.” \n6. Reaffirm trust and partnership \n“You’re a valued part of this team, and your voice matters. I appreciate your honesty, and I want \nyou to feel confident that leadership has your back and will advocate for you with the client.” \n__________________________________________________________\n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n📝 7. Reflection (After the Meeting) \n• Did I take ownership for potential communication or trust gaps? \n• Did the team member feel heard and supported? \n• Did I reinforce proper communication channels without blame? \n• Did I clearly convey how their actions affected the client relationship? \n• What went well? What could I improve next time? \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________\n\n💬 Crucial Conversation Prep Worksheet \nScenario Example: Personal conflict within the team. This may involve peer-to-peer conflict, \nteam leaders with peers, frontline agents with peers, or team leaders with frontline agents. \n \n1. Lead with the Heart \nPurpose: Address personal conflict with empathy, neutrality, and professionalism. The goal is \nnot to assign blame, but to restore trust, clarify expectations, and help all parties move forward \nproductively. \nExample Reflection: “Conflict often comes from miscommunication or unmet expectations. I \nwant to approach this conversation calmly, fairly, and with the intent to repair working \nrelationships.” \nPrompts: \n• How do I remain neutral and avoid taking sides? \n• How can I validate emotions without validating harmful behavior? \n• How can I reinforce respect, accountability, and professionalism? \nExample Opening Line: “I want to talk about a situation that’s creating tension. My goal is to \nunderstand perspectives and help everyone move forward constructively.” \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n🎯 2. Define the Ideal Outcome \nExample: “By the end of the conversation, those involved feel heard, understand expectations \nfor professional behavior, and commit to working together respectfully.” \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________\n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n⚠️ 3. Define the Negative Outcome to Avoid \nExample: “Avoid escalating emotions, assigning blame, or allowing the conflict to become \npersonal. Avoid minimizing the issue or letting unresolved tension impact the team.” \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n📊 4. Gather the Facts \nExample Notes: \n• Repeated tension, arguments, or avoidance observed. \n• Impact to collaboration, morale, or performance. \n• Specific behaviors, incidents, or dates documented. \n• Facts verified with those involved; no assumptions. \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n🤝 5. Gain Buy-In After Presenting the Facts \nPurpose: Allow each person to share their perspective. Buy-in is achieved when individuals feel \nheard and understand their role in resolving the conflict. \nSample Questions: \n• Can you walk me through how you see the situation? \n• What do you feel has been misunderstood? \n• How has this situation affected your work?\n• What would resolution look like from your perspective? \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n🧭 6. Conversation Plan (Outline) \n1. Open with empathy and purpose. \n“I want to talk about something that’s been creating tension. My goal is to understand what’s \nhappening and help restore a productive working relationship.” \n  \n2. Acknowledge emotions without judgment. \n“I can see this has been frustrating. It’s important everyone feels heard while we stay \nprofessional.” \n  \n3. Present the facts calmly. \n“Here’s what I’ve observed and what’s been shared. I want to focus on behaviors and impact, \nnot intent.” \n  \n4. Gain buy-in after presenting the facts. \n“Help me understand your perspective so we can identify a path forward.” \n  \n5. Collaborate on the way forward. \n“Let’s agree on how we’ll communicate and work together going forward to avoid similar issues.” \n  \n6. Reaffirm expectations and partnership. \n“We don’t have to agree on everything, but we do need to work together respectfully. I \nappreciate your willingness to address this.”\n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n📝 7. Reflection (After the Meeting) \n• Did I remain neutral and fair? \n• Did all parties feel heard and respected? \n• Did I clearly reinforce expectations for professionalism? \n• Did we agree on specific next steps or boundaries? \n• What follow-up is needed to ensure resolution? \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________\n\n💬 Crucial Conversation Prep Worksheet \nScenario Example: A team member posts work-related issues or frustrations on social media. \nWhile the concern may be legitimate, it was shared publicly and with an inappropriate tone \ninstead of being raised through internal channels. \n \n1. Lead with the Heart \nPurpose: Approach this conversation with empathy and professionalism. The goal is not to \ndismiss the concern, but to redirect how and where concerns are raised, protecting the \nemployee, the team, and the client relationship. \nExample Reflection: “I want to acknowledge that the concern may be valid, while clearly \nexplaining that social media is not the appropriate forum. My role is to guide them toward safer, \nmore effective communication.” \nPrompts: \n• How do I validate the concern without validating the behavior? \n• How do I explain the impact of public posts on trust, clients, and the organization? \n• How do I reinforce internal channels as safe and effective? \nExample Opening Line: “I want to talk about a recent post you shared. My goal isn’t to shut \ndown concerns, but to make sure we address them in the right way and in the right place.” \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________ \n🎯 2. Define the Ideal Outcome \nExample: “By the end of this conversation, the team member understands why public posts are \nnot appropriate, feels heard regarding the underlying issue, and commits to using internal \nchannels going forward.”\n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n⚠️ 3. Define the Negative Outcome to Avoid \nExample: “Avoid appearing dismissive or defensive. Avoid sending the message that concerns \nshould be suppressed. Avoid escalating the situation by focusing on punishment instead of \neducation and redirection.” \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________ \n📊 4. Gather the Facts \nExample Notes: \n• Specific social media post(s) referencing work, leadership, clients, or policies. \n• Tone or language that could be perceived as unprofessional or damaging. \n• Legitimate concern embedded within the post. \n• Relevant company social media or conduct policy. \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________ \n🤝 5. Gain Buy-In After Presenting the Facts \nPurpose: Allow the team member to explain their intent and feelings. Buy-in comes from \nacknowledging the concern while resetting expectations for communication. \nSample Questions: \n• What led you to share this publicly?\n• What outcome were you hoping for by posting it? \n• Were you aware of how this could be perceived by clients or teammates? \n• How comfortable do you feel raising concerns internally? \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n🧭 6. Conversation Plan (Outline) \n1. Open with empathy and intent. \n“I want to start by acknowledging that the concern you raised may be valid, and I appreciate you \ncaring enough to speak up.” \n  \n2. Acknowledge emotions. \n“It sounds like you were frustrated, and that’s understandable.” \n  \n3. Present the facts and impact. \n“When concerns are shared publicly on social media, it can impact trust with clients and create \nmisunderstandings, even when the issue itself is real.” \n  \n4. Gain buy-in after presenting the facts. \n“Can you help me understand what you hoped would happen by posting this?” \n  \n5. Redirect to the right channels. \n“I want to make sure you know that bringing concerns directly to leadership is the safest and \nmost effective way to get them addressed.”\n6. Reaffirm trust and expectations. \n“Your voice matters. We just need to make sure it’s shared in a way that protects you, the team, \nand our clients.” \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n📝 7. Reflection (After the Meeting) \n• Did I validate the concern without validating the behavior? \n• Did I clearly explain the risks of public posting? \n• Did the team member feel heard and supported? \n• Did I reinforce internal channels clearly? \n• What follow-up or monitoring is needed? \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________\n\n💬 Crucial Conversation Prep Worksheet \nScenario Example: A team member requests to change accounts due to dissatisfaction with the \naccount, its complexity, or leadership. Transfers are rare and reserved for specific \ncircumstances. \n \n1. Lead with the Heart \nPurpose: Approach this conversation with empathy and curiosity. A request to transfer is often a \nsignal of frustration, being overwhelmed, or unmet expectations. The goal is to understand the \nconcern, explore solutions within the current account, and reinforce that transfers are not the \ndefault solution. \nExample Reflection: “I want to understand what’s driving this request and explore how we can \naddress those concerns here first, while being honest about how rarely transfers occur.” \nPrompts: \n• How do I validate their experience without promising a transfer? \n• How do I frame this as problem-solving, not avoidance? \n• How do I reinforce accountability while remaining supportive? \nExample Opening Line: “I want to understand what’s behind your request and talk through \nwhether we can address those concerns within your current account.” \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n🎯 2. Define the Ideal Outcome \nExample: “By the end of the conversation, the team member feels heard, understands why \ntransfers are rare, and is open to addressing their concerns within the current account with \nsupport.”\n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n⚠️ 3. Define the Negative Outcome to Avoid \nExample: “Avoid reinforcing the idea that discomfort leads to reassignment. Avoid appearing \ndismissive of their concerns. Avoid setting expectations that cannot be fulfilled.” \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n📊 4. Gather the Facts \nExample Notes: \n• Specific concerns raised about the account, workload, or leadership. \n• Performance history on the account (quality, productivity, attendance, coachability). \n• Support already provided (coaching, nesting, shadowing, schedule adjustments). \n• Whether challenges appear skill-based, effort-based, or situational. \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n🤝 5. Gain Buy-In After Presenting the Facts \nPurpose: Invite the team member to reflect on their experience and participate in \nproblem-solving, rather than positioning a transfer as the solution. \nSample Questions: \n• What specifically feels most challenging about this account?\n• Is it the work itself, the pace, or how support shows up? \n• What support do you feel would help you be successful here? \n• If those supports were in place, would you be open to continuing on this account? \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n🧭 6. Conversation Plan (Outline) \n1. Open with empathy and intent. \n“I appreciate you being honest about how you’re feeling. Let’s talk through what’s driving this \nrequest.” \n  \n2. Acknowledge concerns as valid feedback. \n“Concerns about an account or leadership are important feedback, and I want to understand \nthem fully.” \n  \n3. Present expectations and context. \n“Our preference is always to resolve concerns within the account. Transfers are rare because \nthey can have significant operational and performance impacts.” \n  \n4. Gain buy-in after presenting the facts. \n“Given that context, let’s explore what would need to change for you to be successful here.” \n  \n5. Collaborate on next steps. \n“We can work on targeted coaching, added support, or leadership alignment to address what’s \nnot working.”\n6. Reaffirm role and transparency. \n“I don’t approve transfers, but I do escalate recommendations when appropriate. My \nresponsibility is to ensure we’ve explored all options first.” \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n📝 7. Reflection (After the Meeting) \n• Did I avoid over-promising or positioning a transfer as likely? \n• Did I treat their concerns as valid feedback? \n• Did I reinforce accountability and problem-solving? \n• Did I clearly explain my role in the process? \n• What follow-up actions are needed? \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________ \n____________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________\n\n💬 Crucial Conversation Prep Worksheet \nScenario Example: Team members express frustration or perceived unfairness about workload \ndistribution, comparing their volume or task difficulty to other groups. \n \n1. Lead with the Heart \nPurpose: Start with empathy and understanding. Acknowledge that perceptions of fairness are \npowerful and can affect motivation, teamwork, and trust. Approach this conversation with care, \naiming to bring clarity and reinforce transparency rather than defensiveness. \nExample Reflection: “I want to approach this conversation recognizing that feeling overworked or \nunfairly treated can be discouraging. My goal is to help them see the bigger picture, understand \nhow distribution decisions are made, and feel confident that their contributions are recognized \nand valued.” \nPrompts: \n• How can I validate their feelings without agreeing with inaccurate comparisons? \n• What information or context can I share to increase understanding and trust? \n• How can I shift the focus from comparison to collaboration and shared goals? \nExample Opening Line: “I can understand how it might feel if it seems like one group has a lighter \nload. Let’s talk through what’s behind the distribution and how we can make sure things feel \nbalanced and fair moving forward.” \n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________ \n🎯 2. Define the Ideal Outcome \nPurpose: Clarify what success looks like — not just ending the conversation peacefully, but \nrestoring trust and focus. \nExample: “By the end of the discussion, I want the agent to better understand how workload \ndecisions are made, feel heard about their concerns, and leave the conversation reassured that \nleadership is monitoring balance and fairness across teams.” \n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________\n⚠️ 3. Define the Negative Outcome to Avoid \nPurpose: Identify what could go wrong if the discussion is mishandled. \nExample: “I want to avoid dismissing their concerns or creating a defensive tone. I also want to \nprevent the discussion from turning into blame or comparisons that divide the team rather than \nstrengthen it.” \n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________ \n📊 4. Gather the Facts \nPurpose: Enter the discussion with data and context, not assumptions. Facts help clarify why \ndifferences exist and show transparency in decision-making. \nExample Notes: \n• Task allocation is based on client priorities, skill specialization, and business needs. \n• Current distribution: Group A handles higher complexity but fewer cases; Group B handles \nhigher volume but lower complexity. \n• Scheduling and skill alignment may cause temporary differences in workload. \n• No intentional favoritism — adjustments are regularly reviewed. \n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________ \n🤝 5. Gain Buy-In After Presenting the Facts \nPurpose: After explaining the data, invite them to share their perspective. The goal is not to \nprove them wrong but to ensure they feel seen and valued. \nExample Approach: “I want to make sure I’m seeing this from your point of view, too. Can you \ntell me what makes it feel unbalanced from where you sit?” \nSample Questions to Ask: \n• What part of the workload feels unfair to you right now? \n• Are there certain types of tasks or schedules that feel heavier? \n• Have you noticed any recent changes that might be driving that perception? \n• What would help you feel the distribution is more balanced or manageable? \n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________\n🧭 6. Conversation Plan (Outline) \nExample Flow and Dialogue: \n1. Open with empathy and purpose \n“I know workload balance can be a sensitive topic, and I want to talk about it openly. My goal is \nto make sure you understand how work is distributed and to hear any feedback you have.” \n2. Acknowledge their feelings \n“I understand it can feel unfair when others seem to have fewer or easier tasks. I appreciate you \nbeing honest about how that feels.” \n3. Present the facts calmly \n“Different teams have different types of work based on client needs, skills, and schedules. We \nlook at fairness from both quantity and complexity. For example, one team might have fewer \ntasks, but those tasks take more time to complete.” \n4. Gain buy-in after presenting the facts \n“From your perspective, does that explanation make sense? Are there parts of your workload \nthat still feel unbalanced?” \n5. Collaborate on the way forward \n“If we notice ongoing gaps, I’ll work with the team to see where we can rebalance or provide \nadditional support. I also want to keep communication open — if something feels off, let’s talk \nsooner.” \n6. Reaffirm trust and partnership \n“You’re a key part of this team, and I value how much you contribute. We’ll continue reviewing \nworkloads to make sure we’re supporting everyone fairly.” \n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________\n____________________________________________________________________________ \n📝 7. Reflection (After the Meeting) \n• Did I validate their feelings while reinforcing transparency? \n• Did I clearly explain the reasons behind workload distribution? \n• Did I avoid comparisons or defensiveness? \n• Did I reaffirm the team’s shared goals and trust?\n• What went well? What could I improve next time? \n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________\n____________________________________________________________________________\n\n💬 Crucial Conversation Prep Worksheet \nScenario Example: An employee has been making inappropriate sexual jokes. \n1. Lead with the Heart \nPurpose: Begin with empathy and the genuine intent to help the employee understand the \nseriousness of the issue while protecting a respectful, safe workplace for everyone. \nExample Reflection: “I want to handle this conversation in a way that preserves the individual’s \ndignity while making it clear that this behavior isn’t acceptable. My intent is to correct the \nbehavior, not to shame the person.” \nPrompts: \n• What might be motivating this behavior — humor, attention, habit, or lack of awareness? \n• How can I show care for the person while maintaining professionalism? \n• How can I ensure the conversation stays calm, respectful, and focused on accountability? \nExample Opening Line: “I wanted to have a quick but important conversation with you. I know \nyou may not have meant harm, but some of your recent comments have made others \nuncomfortable, and I want to talk about that directly and respectfully.” \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n🎯 2. Define the Ideal Outcome \nPurpose: Clearly visualize what “success” looks like after the conversation. \nExample: “By the end of this discussion, I want the employee to understand why sexual jokes \nare inappropriate in the workplace, acknowledge the impact, and commit to changing the \nbehavior moving forward.” \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________\n__________________________________________________________ \n⚠️ 3. Define the Negative Outcome to Avoid \nPurpose: Identify what could go wrong if emotions or defensiveness take over. \nExample: “I want to avoid the employee feeling personally attacked or embarrassed in a way \nthat prevents learning. I also want to avoid minimizing the issue, which could undermine the \nimportance of maintaining a respectful environment.” \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n📊 4. Gather the Facts \nPurpose: Enter the conversation equipped with objective information. Stick to specific examples, \ndates, and any feedback or complaints received — avoid hearsay or assumptions. \nExample Notes: \n• Several teammates reported discomfort after hearing sexual jokes made during team huddles. \n• The comments were made on [specific date(s)] and included remarks about coworkers’ \nappearance. \n• No formal complaint yet, but HR has been informed. \n• The employee has not had prior disciplinary issues but has a generally casual communication \nstyle. \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n🤝 5. Gain Buy-In After Presenting the Facts \nPurpose: Invite the employee to share their perspective after explaining the concern. This builds \nunderstanding and encourages ownership.\nExample Approach: “Now that I’ve explained what was shared with me, I’d like to hear your \nthoughts. Can you help me understand your perspective?” \nSample Questions to Ask: \n• How do you feel hearing this feedback? \n• Do you understand why this type of comment is inappropriate in the workplace? \n• Do you see how this could make others uncomfortable? \n• What can you do differently moving forward to help maintain a respectful environment? \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n🧭 6. Conversation Plan (Outline) \nExample Flow and Dialogue: \n1. Open with empathy and purpose \n“I wanted to talk about something that’s come to my attention. My goal is to have an open, \nrespectful discussion and make sure we’re aligned on our workplace expectations.” \n2. Acknowledge their feelings \n“I understand you may not have intended harm or offense. I appreciate your willingness to hear \nthis feedback.” \n3. Present the facts calmly \n“There have been a few instances where you made sexual jokes in team settings, which made \nothers uncomfortable. Those types of comments are not appropriate in a professional \nenvironment.” \n4. Gain buy-in after presenting the facts \n“Now that I’ve shared what’s been observed, how do you feel about this feedback? Do you \nunderstand why this behavior needs to stop?” \n5. Collaborate on the way forward\n“Let’s agree that this kind of humor won’t be used moving forward. I’m here to support you in \nmaking that change, but I also need to be clear that continuing this behavior could lead to \ndisciplinary action.” \n6. Reaffirm trust and partnership \n“I believe this can be a learning moment, not a defining one. I know you’re capable of creating a \npositive, respectful environment, and I want to help you succeed in doing that.” \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n📝 7. Reflection (After the Meeting) \n• Did I lead with empathy and respect while remaining firm? \n• Did I clearly communicate expectations and potential consequences? \n• Did I allow space for the employee to share their point of view? \n• Did I document the discussion for HR follow-up if needed? \n• What went well? What could I improve next time? \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________\n\n💬 Crucial Conversation Prep Worksheet \nScenario Example: “The performance improvement plan is not fair.” \n1. Lead with the Heart \nPurpose: Begin from a place of empathy, genuine care, and shared goals. Reflect on your intent \n— the goal is to help the person grow and succeed. \nExample Reflection: “I want to help the agent see that the PIP is not a punishment but a support \nplan to help them succeed.” \nPrompts: \n• What do I appreciate about this person or their effort recently? \n• How might they be feeling about this situation? \n• How can I show that I’m here to help, not to blame? \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n🎯 2. Define the Ideal Outcome \nPurpose: Clearly visualize what “success” looks like after the conversation. \nExample: “By the end of the discussion, I want them to feel heard, understand the reasons \nbehind the PIP, and be open to collaborating on a plan that helps them meet expectations.” \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n⚠️ 3. Define the Negative Outcome to Avoid \nPurpose: Identify what could go wrong if emotions or defensiveness take over. \nExample: “I want to avoid the conversation turning into blame or arguments about fairness.”\n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n📊 4. Gather the Facts \nPurpose: Enter the conversation equipped with accurate, objective information. \nExample Notes: \n• Attendance: 4 tardies, 2 unapproved absences (30 days). \n• QA average: 70% vs. team 85% (2 weeks). \n• Coaching sessions on June 12, 18, and 25 addressed the same areas. \n• The agent acknowledged understanding previously. \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n🤝 5. Gain Buy-In After Presenting the Facts \nPurpose: Invite the team member into the dialogue after sharing the facts to ensure mutual \nunderstanding and shared ownership. \nSample Questions: \n• What are your thoughts about what I’ve shared? \n• Do you understand why we are implementing a PIP now? \n• From your point of view, what has contributed to the recent challenges? \n• Does any part of this feel unclear or unfair to you? \n• What support do you feel would make the biggest difference? \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________\n🧭 6. Conversation Plan (Outline) \nExample Flow and Dialogue: \n1. Open with empathy and purpose \n“I wanted to talk about your feedback that the PIP feels unfair. I understand this is stressful, and \nI want us to look at it together.” \n2. Acknowledge their feelings \n“I appreciate you being honest — that tells me you care about your performance.” \n3. Present the facts calmly \n“Here’s what led to the PIP: a few missed attendance targets and a QA drop that we had \ncoached on before.” \n4. Gain buy-in after presenting the facts \n“Now that you’ve seen where the data comes from, how does that sit with you?” \n5. Collaborate on the way forward \n“Let’s identify what support you need from me to meet your targets. The goal is not to penalize, \nbut to help you succeed.” \n6. Reaffirm trust and partnership \n“I believe in your ability to turn this around. Let’s treat this plan as our shared roadmap to your \nsuccess.” \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n📝 7. Reflection (After the Meeting) \n• Did I lead with empathy and clarity? \n• Did the conversation stay focused on growth and support? \n• Did I invite the team member to share their perspective after presenting the facts? \n• What went well?\n• What could I improve next time? \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________ \n__________________________________________________________\n\nWalmart Support SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \nWalmart Support SOP: Table of contents \nSubtab 1: Leaves SOP \nGuidelines and filing procedures for planned time off, medical leaves, and emergency absences \nto ensure proper coverage. \nSubtab 2: Overtime SOP \nThe end-to-end process for requesting, authorizing, and documenting additional work hours \nbeyond the standard shift. \nSubtab 3: Schedule Change SOP \nProcedures for shift swaps, permanent schedule adjustments, and aligning Zoho schedules with \nthe Master Transition Sheet. \nSubtab 4: Attendance Notification SOP \nCommunication protocols for reporting late logins, early departures, or unscheduled absences \nto RTA and Management. \nSubtab 5: Workforce Ticketing SOP \nInstructions for submitting formal tickets to the WFM team regarding profile updates, headcount \nchanges, or data corrections. \nSubtab 6: Disputes SOP \nThe formal method for agents to contest errors in attendance logs, payroll hours, or \nperformance metrics. \nSubtab 7: Issue Logs SOP \nStandardized steps for documenting daily operational challenges, workflow blockers, and \nreal-time agent concerns. \nSubtab 8: Suspension SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \nThe administrative workflow for managing temporary suspension of duties or system access due \nto disciplinary or security reasons. \nSubtab 9: REST SOP \nCompliance guidelines regarding mandatory break intervals, rest periods, and maintaining login \nadherence during downtime. \nSubtab 10: Client Tool SOP \nA technical overview and troubleshooting guide for specific client-side platforms such as \nServiceNow and the LATTE tool. \nSubtab 11: System Issue Reporting \nCritical escalation paths for reporting technical outages, hardware malfunctions, or software \nbugs that prevent production.\nLeaves SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Walmart Support \n| Employee Leave Management \nObjective: To define the responsibilities of Support Staff in managing leave requests for their \ndirect reports and themselves, ensuring operational stability and policy compliance. \n \n \nClick the “Leave Request” tab on the upper right tab to access the general view of the \nleave request dashboard.\n \n1. Vacation Leave (VL) Process: \nSupport focuses on workload balancing and advanced scheduling. \n●​ Lead Time: Must be filed at least 7 days in advance with a maximum range of 30 days \nbefore the target date. To remain equal on slot application, leave requests beyond 30 \ndays of creation date shall be disapproved by the RTA’s. \n●​ Support Action:\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n1.​ Quota Check: Open the Walmart VL Dashboard on Zoho and fill in the dates \nthat are planned for Vacation Leave. If the date is at capacity, Support must \nsuggest alternative dates to the agent before filing. \n2.​ Performance Review: Support checks if the agent has any pending deliverables \nor \"Critical Support\" shifts during the requested period. \n3.​ Submission: Support informs the agent that he/she may now apply for vacation \nleave. \n4.​ RTA Sync: Once Support \"Endorses,\" RTA will now review leave applications. ​\nNote: After pre-qualifying for leaves with support, approval still remains on the decision \nwith the RTA if slots are available on the date of request.  \n \n2. Sick Leave (SL) Process: \nSupport focuses on immediate communication and documentation. \n●​ Notification: Agent must notify Support and/or via attendance notification form on Zoho \nat least 2 hours before shift start or 2 hours after the scheduled start shift. This is to\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \navoid being marked as NCNS which would prevent additional points on APS. \n \n●​ Support Action: \n1.​ Acknowledge: Confirm receipt and tell the agent to \"Rest well\" (Empathy first). \n2.​ The \"2-Day Rule\": If the SL exceeds 2 or more days, Support must remind the \nagent that a Medical Certificate is required upon return. \n3.​ System Entry: After recovery of the agent, Support may advise the agent to file \nSick Leave via leave requests form. Medical documents shall be required for SL \napplication.  \n \n3. Bereavement Leave Process:  \nSupport focuses on sensitivity and long-term coverage. \n●​ Eligibility: Standard is 3–5 days (depending on company policy) for immediate family. \n●​ Support Action: \n1.​ Immediate Leave: If the agent is currently on shift, Support should advise the \nagent to communicate with RTA and/or higher operations for an immediate \n\"Emergency leave” via Email. \n2.​ Document Grace Period: Inform the agent that the Death Certificate should be \nsubmitted on Zoho for approval of bereavement leave by HR. Agent may also \napply for BL on Zoho via the Leave request page.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \n3.​ Team Handoff: Support reassigns the agent’s tasks to other team members if \nthere are any, to ensure no \"pings\" reach the agent during their mourning. \n \n4. Solo Parent Leave Process:  \nSupport focuses on legal entitlement and validity. \n●​ Eligibility: Usually 7 days per year, provided the agent has served for at least 6 months  \n●​ Support Action: \n1.​ ID Validation: Before filing, Support must verify the Solo Parent ID is not \nexpired.  \n2.​ System Tagging: Ensure the leave is tagged specifically as \"Solo Parent Leave\" \nand not \"VL,\" as these are separate credits. \n3.​ Planning: Since these can be used for school events or child medical needs, \nSupport should encourage agents to provide at least 48 hours notice when \npossible, though RTA must prioritize these requests as a legal right. \nIf qualifications are valid, assist the agent in filing the leave via Zoho on the leave request page. \nSelect SPL on the dropdown as follows and file the remaining information needed on the leave\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \nrequest. RTA may now review and be subject for approval. ​\n \n5. Maternity Leave Process:  \nSupport focuses on long-term resource planning and the \"Return-to-Work\" plan. \n●​ Duration: Usually 60–105 days (depending on local law). \n●​ Support Action: \n1.​ Advance Notice: Support should be notified at least 30–60 days before the \nexpected delivery date (EDD) to allow RTA to move resources. \n2.​ HR Liaison: Support ensures the agent has submitted the \"Notification of \nPregnancy\" to HR via email for government/insurance benefit processing. \n3.​ The \"Hard Stop\": Coordinate with RTA for the agent’s last day. Ensure login \ncredentials are set to \"Inactive\" or “Maternity Leave” status during the leave. \n4.​ Filing: Agent must file the maternity leave notification form to proceed with the \nmaternity leave. The form can be found under HR>Maternity Leave Notification\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \non zoho. \n6. Paternity Leave Process: \nSupport focuses on flexibility, as the exact start date is often unpredictable. \n●​ Duration: Usually 7–14 days. \n●​ Support Action: \n1.​ The \"Window\" Period: Support notes the spouse\'s due date. Unlike VL, RTA \nmust allow for a \"floating start date.\" This would mean that there would be an \nestimation of the start date of leave.  \n2.​ Activation: Once the child is born, the agent notifies Support and proceeds to \nfile Paternity leave via Leave request form on Zoho. ​\nDocumentation: Support ensures the agent provides a copy of the Birth \nCertificate upon return to validate the paid leave.​\n \nProcess Breakdown by Category \nLeave Type \nSupport Action \nRTA Visibility \nVL \nCheck RTA View for slots. \nHigh (Planning required) \nSL \nNotify RTA via \"Urgent\" chat immediately. \nImmediate (Intraday)\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n​\n7. Leave Retraction Process: \nSupport focuses on restoring headcount and updating the RTA Real-Time View. \nA retraction occurs when a leave that was already approved needs to be cancelled. This is \ndifferent from a \"denial\" because the schedule has already been adjusted. \nAgent-Initiated Retraction \n1.​ Notification: Agent must notify TL and RTA at least 24 hours before the leave start \ndate. Same day retraction is not allowed and is subject to disapproval.  \n2.​ Support Action: \n○​ Verify Intent: Confirm if the agent wants to retract the entire leave or just specific \ndays. \n○​ System Reversal: Agent must go into the Leave Request System > My Records \n>  For Approval and select \"Retract\" . \n3.​ RTA Action: RTA approves retraction in a condition where the application of retraction is \nbefore the date of the requested date of leave.\nOvertime SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Walmart Support  \n| Client-Mandated Overtime (OT) Recruitment and Cascade \n \n1. Purpose/ Scope \nThis procedure outlines the process for managing Overtime (OT) requests triggered by \na Client Mandate. It is designed to address spikes in volume by ensuring a seamless \nflow of information from the Client to the Real-Time Analysts (RTA), through the Team \nLeads (TL), and finally to the Agents (OT Takers). This applies to all Lines of Business \n(LOB) under the Operations. \n2. Roles & Responsibilities \nRole: \nResponsibility: \nClient \nInitiates the mandate based on volume spikes and SLA \nrequirements. \nRTA \n(Real-Time \nAnalyst) \nMonitors volume, validates the need for OT. \nTeam Lead (TL) \nCascades the OT requirement to their specific LOB and \nrecruits \"OT Takers.\" \nSupport Agent \nVolunteers as an \"OT Taker\" and executes the additional \nhours. \n \n3. Tools / Requirements \n●​ Workforce Management (WFM) Tool: Incontact tool, ECE IRR Realtime \nDashboard.  \n●​ Internal Communication Tool: Cliq, Email, MS Teams.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n4. Procedure (Steps) \nStep 1: Receipt of Client Mandate \n●​ The Client or LOB Manager identifies a spike in volume that cannot be handled \nby the current headcount. \n●​ They issue a mandate for OT. \n●​ Action: RTA and higher Operations receives the notification and assesses the \nrequired headcount to stabilize the queue. \nStep 2: Cascade to Team Leads (TLs) \n●​ Higher operations send a priority notification to all TLs within the affected LOB. \n●​ Action: The message must include the reason (Volume Spike) and the number \nof agents needed. \nStep 3: Internal Team Cascade and Recruitment \n●​ TLs immediately post the OT requirement in their respective team channels. \n●​ Action: TLs solicit \"OT Takers.\" \nStep 4: Roster Collection and Validation \n●​ TLs gather the names of the \"OT Takers\" and submit the list back to the RTA. \n●​ Action: RTA and/or Operations updates the roster and informs the Client that the \nadditional headcount is being deployed. \nStep 5: Execution and Monitoring \n●​ Agents begin their OT. RTA monitors Adherence to ensure OT takers are actually \nin production using the InContact Tool.  \n●​ Action: If the volume spike subsides, RTA notifies TLs to cease OT recruitment. \nNotes / Tips: \n●​ Always prioritize agents with high proficiency in the specific queue experiencing \nthe spike. \n●​ Ensure agents take their mandatory rest breaks if the OT extends their shift \nsignificantly.​\n​\n​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n​\n \n5. Exceptions & Escalation \n​\nVolume Spike persists: OT is insufficient to clear the queue. Inform RTA to request \n\"Cross-LOB\" support.  \nQueue Monitoring: Monitor Agents that are still on shift and the OT takers to ensure \n100% productivity within the day. ​\n​\n6. Records / Outputs \n●​ OT Tracker: A log of all agents who worked the mandate. \n●​ Post-Shift Report: A brief summary showing how the OT impacted the volume \nspike. \n●​ Payroll Export: Adjusted timesheets reflecting the approved OT hours. \n7. References \n●​ Employee Handbook (Overtime Pay Policies). \n●​ Client Service Level Agreement (SLA) Documentation.\nSchedule Change SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Walmart Support  \n| Operations Schedule Synchronization & Agent Assistance (Schedule \nChanges) \n \n1. Purpose/ Scope \nThis procedure ensures that all schedule updates made in the Transition Scheduling \nSheet are correctly updated in Zoho. Because the scheduling sheet does not \nautomatically sync with Zoho, Team Leads must monitor for changes and prompt agents \nto manually request the shift update in Zoho to ensure time-tracking and payroll \naccuracy. \n2. Roles & Responsibilities \nWorkforce/RTA: Updates the Master Transition Scheduling Sheet with new \nevent-based shifts, meetings, or coverage requirements. \nTeam Lead (TL): Regularly monitors the Transition Scheduling Sheet for any updates \nand informs the affected agents to initiate the change in Zoho. \nAgent: Responsible for submitting the \"Shift Change Request\" in Zoho once notified by \ntheir TL to ensure their check-in/out aligns with the new schedule. \n \n3. Tools / Requirements \n●​ Transition Scheduling Sheet: The source of truth for Schedules and shift view. \n●​ Zoho: The official tool for time-tracking and attendance. \n●​ Communication Tool: (Email/Cliq) For supervisory notifications. \n4. Procedure (Steps) \nStep 1: Monitor the Transition Scheduling Sheet \n●​ Daily/Weekly, TLs must review the \"Monthly Events/Meetings\" tab in the \nTransition Scheduling Sheet.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \n●​ Action: Identify any agents under your supervision whose shift has been moved \nto cover for event attendees. \nStep 2: Validate Against Zoho \n●​ Open Zoho and check the agent’s current assigned shift. \n●​ Action: Confirm if the change from the Transition Sheet is reflected. If not (which \nis typical), take note of the variance to prevent \"Late\" or \"Absent\" tags in the \nsystem. \nStep 3: Direct Agent Notification (The Cascade) \n●​ Contact the affected agent immediately via cliq or any communication platform.  \n●​ Action: Inform them of the specific change (e.g., \"Shift moved from 8 AM to 10 \nAM to cover\"). \n●​ Note: This must be done as soon as the change is spotted to ensure the agent is \naware before their next login. \nStep 4: Manual Zoho Assistance \n●​Pre-Shift Action: Assist the agent in updating their schedule following “Zoho> \nDaily Schedule> My Daily Schedule” Click “Request Sched change” on the \nmismatched date to match the Transition Sheet before the start of their shift. \nThis pop up should show and TL must assist the agent to avoid further complications on review.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \n●​Agent Action: Instruct the agent to log their time based specifically on the \nTransition Sheet schedule. \n●​RTA review: RTA may review the schedule request and is subject for approval. ​\nTL’s are not authorized to approve schedule changes from their cluster. \nStep 5: Confirmation and Log \n●​ Action: Ensure the agent reacts or responds to the notification to confirm they \nhave seen the new schedule. \n5. Exceptions & Escalation \n \nIssue: Zoho Schedule does not match transition schedule which causes confusion on \nthe agent side. Due to this, agent may adhere to the official schedule without updating \nthe zoho schedule causing undertime, login errors, RDOT pop ups on regular shift, and \nlate logins. ​\n​\nEscalate: TL and RTA \n \n6. Quality Check / Expected Result​\n \nMinimize Discrepancies: Minimal discrepancies during payroll, and lessen possible \ndisputes. ​\n​\nAttendance notification SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Walmart Support  \n| Operations Schedule Synchronization & Agent Assistance (Schedule \nChanges) \n \n1. Purpose/ Scope \nThe purpose of this SOP is to establish a standardized process for documenting agent \nabsences. Ensuring every absence is logged in Zoho allows for real-time visibility for the \nReal-Time Analysts (RTA) and accurate record-keeping for Operations. This procedure \napplies to all Team Leaders (TLs), Senior Team Leaders (STLs), and Operations \nManagers (OMs) when assisting agents with attendance reporting.​\n​\n2. Roles & Responsibilities \n●​ Agent: Responsible for notifying their TL immediately and filing the attendance \nnotification in Zoho. If unable to file, they must provide all necessary details and \ndocumentation to their supervisor. \n●​ Team Leader (TL): Responsible for guiding the agent through the process or \nfiling on the agent\'s behalf if they face technical or personal issues. Ensures all \ndata is accurate and visible to Ops. \n●​ STL / OM: Responsible for overseeing compliance and stepping in if the TL is \nunavailable to ensure no gaps in attendance reporting. \n●​ RTA Team: Responsible for monitoring Zoho entries to adjust staffing levels and \nmanage real-time service levels. \n3. Tools / Requirements \n●​ Zoho \n●​ Internal Communication Tool: Zoho Cliq,Batphone via Messenger, Email. \n●​ Supporting Documentation (e.g., Medical Certificates, if applicable) \n4. Procedure (Steps) \nStep 1: Receipt of Absence Notification The process begins when an agent contacts \nthe TL via any communication channel to report an absence. \n●​ Action: Acknowledge the message and confirm the reason for the absence.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \nStep 2: Verification of Agent Access The TL must instruct the agent to log into Zoho \nto plot their attendance notification. \n●​ Action: Ask the agent: \"Are you able to access Zoho right now to file your \nnotification?\" \n●​ If Yes: Provide the agent with the internal link and monitor for the entry. \n●​ If No: Proceed to Step 3. \nStep 3: Support Intervention (Filing on Behalf) If the agent cannot file due to \ntechnical issues, lack of internet, or medical emergency, the TL must take over. \n●​ Action: Collect necessary details from the agent (Reason, Duration, \nDocumentation/Screenshots). \n●​ Action: Navigate to the Zoho>Attendance Notification>Attendance \nNotification Form and find the name of the agent on the list, and fill out the form \nfor attendance notification. This serves as a visual for the absence of any \nemployee.  \n \nStep 4: Confirmation and Visibility Once submitted, the notification is now visual for \nthe Operations and Workforce Team. ​\n​\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \nNotes / Tips: > * Documentation: Always encourage agents to provide a Medical \nCertificate (MC) or screenshots of technical errors to strengthen the filing. \n●​ Timeliness: Aim to have the Zoho notification completed within 2 \nhours before or after the expected shift of the agent\'s initial \nnotification to avoid being tagged as NCNS.  \n5. Exception and Escalation  \nIssue \nAction \nEscalate To \nZoho System is down \nfor everyone. \nLog the absence in a manual tracker \n(Excel/Google Sheets). \nRTA \nAgent is \nunreachable/AWOL. \nAttempt contact; if no response by \nafter 2 hours of schedule shift, file as \nNCNS. \nRTA \n6. Quality Check / Expected Result \n●​ Expected Result: Every absent agent has a corresponding entry in Zoho for their \nabsence.  \n●​ Quality: lessen NCNS tagging for agents.  \n7. Records / Outputs \n●​ Zoho Attendance Logs: The primary source of truth for RTA and Payroll. \n●​ Support Documentation: Digital copies of MCs or error screenshots attached to the \nZoho entry.\nWorkforce Ticketing SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Walmart Support \n| Workforce Ticketing & Offline Activity Requests \n​\n​\n1. Purpose/ Scope \nThis procedure outlines the process for Support Staff (TL/STL/OM) to request schedule \nadjustments and data reports from the Workforce/RTA team. This ensures all offline activities \nand capacity changes are documented, approved, and tracked to maintain service level targets. \n2. Roles & Responsibilities \n●​ Team Leader (TL) / Support: Responsible for submitting tickets at least one hour in \nadvance and informing agents once approvals are received. \n●​ RTA Team: Responsible for reviewing tickets, checking service level impact, and \nproviding an \"Approved\" or \"Denied\" response. \n●​ Agent: Responsible for plotting the approved activity in their schedule only after being \nnotified by their TL. \n3. Tools / Requirements \n●​ Zoho / WFM Ticketing System \n●​ Workforce Rest \n●​ Other reporting tools, etc.  \n \n4. Procedure (Steps) \nStep 1: Determine Requirement & Timeline Identify the need for an offline activity (Coaching, \nHuddle, Training) or a data request (FTE Capacity/Reports). \n●​ Action: Ensure the request is being made at least 60 minutes (1 hour) before the \nintended start time. \nStep 2: Create a Workforce Ticket Access the Zoho Ticketing module to file the formal \nrequest.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \n●​ Action: Fill in the mandatory fields:\n \n○​ Topic: (e.g., Offline Activity, Data Request, Break Adjustment). \n○​ Reason: Specify the activity (e.g., \"Weekly Coaching,\" \"Team Huddle,\" \"System \nIssue\"). \n○​ Affected Staff: List the Name(s) of the Agent(s). \n○​ Timeframe: Start and End time of the activity. Using Military time is \nrecommended in time format \n○​ Duration: Total Minutes of the activity (for Offline Activity) \n○​ Additional Documents to support requests but not mandatory. \n \nStep 3: Await RTA Review The RTA assigned to the ticket will analyze the current floor volume \nand FTE capacity. \n●​ Action: Wait for a notification or comment on the ticket. Do not pull the agent offline until \nthe ticket status is marked as \"Approved.\" \nStep 4: Agent Notification & Plotting Once the ticket is approved, the TL must close the loop \nwith the agent. \n●​ Action: Inform the agent that the request is granted and prepare for the planned offline \nactivity.  \nStep 5: Ticket Closure \n●​ Action: Ensure the ticket is closed or updated according to the final outcome for \nhistorical data tracking.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \nNotes / Tips \n●​ Advance Notice: Tickets filed less than an hour before the activity are \nsubject to immediate denial if the volume is high. \n●​ Specifics Matter: Avoid vague reasons like \"Meeting\"; use \"Q3 \nPerformance Review\" or \"Process Update Huddle.\" \n5. Exceptions & Escalation \nIssue \nAction \nEscalate To \nTicket not responded to within 30 \nmins \nSend a follow-up ping in the RTA \nChannel. \nAvailable RTA \nTicket Denied but activity is critical \nProvide business justification for \nthe exception. \nRTA Lead \n6. Quality Check / Expected Result \n●​ Expected Result: All offline activities are visible in the RTA dashboard with 0% \n\"Unaccounted\" time for agents. \n●​ Compliance: Tickets are consistently filed 1 hour in advance. \n7. Records / Outputs \n●​ Zoho Support Tickets: Historical log of all offline requests. \n●​ Adherence Reports: Generated by RTA to show scheduled vs. actual activity.\nDisputes SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Walmart Support  \n| Attendance and Log Dispute Management \n \n1. Purpose/ Scope \nThis SOP defines the process for handling agent disputes regarding attendance and shift logs. \nThe goal is to ensure that all disputes filed in Zoho are verified through a hierarchy of evidence. \nThis procedure clarifies when an agent can file independently and when a TL’s \"vouch\" or \nmandatory approval is required to validate a claim. \n2. Roles & Responsibilities \n●​ Agent: Responsible for identifying discrepancies and filing the dispute in Zoho with the \nhighest priority evidence available. \n●​ Team Leader (TL): Responsible for reviewing and \"vouching\" for disputes only when the \nagent lacks primary visual evidence (Screenshots/Docs). \n●​ RTA Lead: Acts as an escalation point for complex disputes or cases where evidence \nand TL vouching are both missing. \n●​ RTA / HR Team: Responsible for the final processing of the Zoho dispute based on the \nevidence hierarchy provided. \n3. Tools / Requirements \n●​ Zoho (Dispute logs) \n●​ Evidence Hierarchy: \n1.​ IC Timestamp (Primary Priority) \n2.​ Work Stamp (Activity logs/System docs) \n3.​ Attendance Thread (SOS/EOS posts) \n4.​ TL Vouch (Required only if options 1-3 are unavailable) \n4. Procedure (Steps) \nStep 1: Discrepancy Identification The agent identifies an error in their logs and prepares the \nnecessary evidence for filing. \nStep 2: Determining Evidence Priority (The \"Vouch\" Rule) Before filing in Zoho, the \nagent/TL must check for evidence in the following order of priority:\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \n●​ Priority 1: IC Timestamp – A screenshot showing the Incontact timestamp of activity. \nRequest for IC stamps: Navigate on Cliq and find the specific thread for IC screenshot \nrequest.​\n​\n​\nFormat for request:​\n​\nRequesting IC Stamp:​\n​\nDate:​\n​\nShift:​\n​\nReason: \n●​ Priority 2: Work Stamp – Any digital document or log validating the employee was \nactive/working during the disputed time. \n●​ Priority 3: SOS/EOS Thread – A screenshot of the agent’s Start of Shift (SOS) or End \nof Shift (EOS) post in the official attendance thread. \n●​ Priority 4: TL Vouch – Mandatory only if Priorities 1, 2, and 3 are missing. The TL must \nreview the claim and provide a formal approval/statement to act as the validation. \nStep 3: Filing the Dispute in Zoho \n●​ If Priority 1, 2, or 3 exists: The agent may proceed with filing the dispute in Zoho, \nattaching the screenshot as proof. TL review is encouraged for the filing to proceed. \n●​ If No Evidence exists: The agent must present the case to the TL. The TL must audit \nthe shift and, if satisfied, provide a \"vouch.\" \nStep 4: Support Documentation Entry \n●​ Action: When filing in Zoho, the evidence must be attached to the ticket. \n●​ Action: If it is a \"TL Vouch\" case, the TL must add a comment or note in the filing: \"No \nvisual evidence available; claim verified and vouched by TL [Name] based on \n[Reason/Internal Audit].\" \nStep 5: Monitoring & Verification The TL monitors the Zoho dashboard to ensure the dispute \nis picked up by RTA. \n●​ Action: Confirm that the dispute status moves from \"Pending\" to \"Approved.\" \nNotes / Tips \n●​ Screenshot Quality: Ensure the full desktop clock/date is visible in IC or \nWork Stamp screenshots to avoid rejection.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \n●​ The Vouch Limit: A TL Vouch should be used as a last resort. Frequent \n\"vouching\" for the same agent without screenshots may be flagged for \noperational review and be subject to fraud.  \n5. Exceptions & Escalation \nIssue \nAction \nEscalate To \nAgent has no evidence and TL \nrefuses to vouch. \nReview shift activity logs via RTA tools for \na final decision. \nRTA \nRTA rejects a \"Vouched\" dispute. \nTL must provide further context on why \nthe vouch was granted. \nRTA Lead \nEvidence is blurry or cropped. \nRequest a new, full-screen capture from \nthe agent. \nAgent \n6. Quality Check / Expected Result \n●​ Expected Result: Every dispute in Zoho contains either a high-priority screenshot \n(Priority 1-3) or a documented TL Vouch (Priority 4). \n●​ Outcome: Accurate payroll and attendance records with minimal rejections and \ndiscrepancies \n7. Records / Outputs \n●​ Zoho Dispute Entries: The official audit trail for the adjustment. \n●​ Attendance Threads: Secondary proof stored in communication channels (Cliq/Email).\nIssue Logs SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Walmart Support  \n| Incident Logging (System, Power, & Internet) \n \n1. Purpose/ Scope \nThis SOP provides guidelines for Team Leaders (TLs) and Support Staff on how to manage and \ndocument agent downtime caused by Power Outages, Internet Connectivity issues, or System \nErrors. It ensures that agents are moved to the correct Aux status in Zoho and that payroll \nimpact is correctly applied based on the agent\'s work setup (Office vs. WFH). \n2. Roles & Responsibilities \n●​ Agent: Responsible for reporting the issue immediately to the TL and providing proof \n(ISP outage notice, photos of power loss, or IT ticket numbers). \n●​ Team Leader (TL): Responsible for determining the \"Resolvability\" of the issue, \ndirecting WFH agents to report to the office if needed, and filing/verifying the Zoho Break \nLogs. \n●​ RTA Team: Responsible for monitoring the floor\'s real-time capacity and ensuring the \nbreak logs match the actual downtime. \n3. Tools / Requirements \n●​ Zoho Attendance (Break Logs Module) \n●​ WFM Ticketing System \n●​ Outage Aux Categories: \n○​ System Error: Internal tool/PC issues (Non-deductible). \n○​ Lost Power: Electricity outage. \n○​ Lost Internet: Connectivity outage. \n \n4. Procedure (Steps) \nStep 1: Incident Reporting & Identification \nWhen an agent encounters an issue, the TL must categorize the outage immediately to \ndetermine the next steps.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \n●​ Action: Identify if the issue is System Error, Lost Power, or Lost Internet. \nStep 2: Processing Office-Based Employees \nFor agents working on-site, outages are considered company-controlled. \n●​ Action: File the respective break log in Zoho. \n●​ Mandatory Requirement: You MUST TICK the \"Not Payroll Deductible\" checkbox. \nThe agent should not lose pay for office-wide issues. \nStep 3: Processing WFH Employees (Outage Management) \nIf a WFH agent reports an Internet or Power outage, follow the 1-Hour Rule: \n●​ If resolved within 1 hour: Log the duration in Zoho from start to finish. \n●​ If NOT resolved within 1 hour: Instruct the agent to report to the Office. \n○​ If Agent can report: Log the outage from the start of the issue until they arrive at \nthe office. DO NOT tick \"Not Payroll Deductible\" (this time is docked). \n○​ If Agent cannot report: Logout the agent and log the outage from the start of \nthe issue until the End of Shift (EOS). DO NOT tick \"Not Payroll Deductible.\" \nStep 4: Processing WFH Employees (System Issues) \nIf the issue is a technical/tool error: \n●​ Action: Direct the agent to IT Support. \n●​ If IT Resolvable: Log the \"System Error\" for the duration of the fix. \n●​ If NOT Resolvable: Instruct the agent to report to the office. Follow the same docking \nrules as Step 3 if they cannot/do not report to the office. \nStep 5: Zoho Entry & Documentation \n●​ Action: Navigate to the agent\'s Zoho profile → Add Break Log. \n●​ Action: Select the correct Type. Ensure the \"Not Payroll Deductible\" box is handled \ncorrectly based on the rules above.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \n●​ Action: Add a note: \"WFH Outage - Did not report to office\" or \"Office-wide Power \nOutage.\" Reference below: ​\n \nNotes / Tips \n●​ The 1-Hour Threshold: The decision to send an agent to the office must be \nmade as soon as it is clear the outage is not a \"flicker\" or quick reset. \n●​ Double Check Checkbox: The most common error is forgetting to \nuncheck/check the \"Not Payroll Deductible\" box. Always verify this before \nhitting save. \n5. Exceptions & Escalation \nIssue \nAction \nEscalate To \nWFH agent claims \"Total Area \nBlackout\"  \nVerify via local news/power company \nmaps; file as docked. \nTL\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \nSystem Issue is a \"Global\" Client \noutage. \nTreat as \"Not Payroll Deductible\" for \neveryone. \nRTA Lead \n​\n6. Quality Check / Expected Result \n●​ Expected Result: Break logs are accurately categorized and payroll settings (deductible \nvs. non-deductible) match the agent\'s work location and reporting status. \n●​ Audit: RTA will cross-reference \"Not Deductible\" logs against known office outages. \n7. Records / Outputs \n●​ Zoho Break Logs: Updated daily. \n●​ IT Ticket Logs: Reference for \"System Error\" validity.\nSuspension SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \n​\nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Walmart Support  \n| Employee Suspension Management & Coordination\n​\n \n1. Purpose/ Scope \nThis procedure establishes the workflow for documenting and enforcing employee suspensions. \nIt ensures that the Operations and RTA teams are aligned to maintain accurate staffing levels, \nrestrict system access during disciplinary periods, and prevent the misuse of leave credits \n(SL/VL) to offset suspension dates. \n2. Roles & Responsibilities \n●​ Operations (TL/STL/OM): Files the suspension request via WFM ticket, provides the \nrequired Notice of Decision (NOD), and ensures the agent complies with the \"no-work\" \ndirective. \n●​ APS POC: Coordinates and logs suspensions specifically resulting from Attendance \nPointing System violations. \n●​ RTA Team: Reviews staffing impact, plots the dates in the specialized Zoho Suspension \nform, and monitors for unauthorized login attempts. \n3. Tools / Requirements \n●​ Zoho Desk: For WFM Ticketing and submission of suspension requests. \n●​ Zoho (Suspension Module): The specific form used for plotting (distinct from the \nstandard leave form). \n●​ Notice of Decision (NOD): Documentation required to validate the disciplinary action. \n \n4. Procedure (Steps) \nStep 1: Submission of Request \nOnce a handbook violation or APS threshold is met, the Support Team must initiate the process. \n●​ Action: Create a Workforce Management (WFM) ticket under \"Suspension Request.\" \n●​ Details: Include Agent Name, Reason for Suspension, Proposed Dates, and attach the \nNOD or supporting evidence. \nStep 2: RTA Staffing Assessment \nThe RTA team evaluates the proposed dates against the program’s capacity requirements.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \n●​ Action: RTA will either confirm the dates or suggest a shift in schedule to avoid service \nlevel gaps. Support must coordinate with RTA to find the balance between disciplinary \ntimelines and floor needs. \nStep 3: Formal Plotting in Zoho \nOnce dates are agreed upon, the official entry is made in Zoho. \n●​ Process: * Handbook/General Violations: The RTA logs the entry. \n○​ APS Violations: The APS POC logs the entry. \n●​ Requirement: Entries must be filed using the Zoho Suspension Form, not the \nstandard leave request tool, to ensure system access is restricted.\n \nStep 4: Compliance Enforcement \nSupport must communicate the suspension terms clearly to the agent. \n●​ Action: Ensure the agent understands they are barred from logging in and cannot use \nVL/SL during these dates.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \n●​ Action: RTA monitors real-time logs. If an agent attempts to log in, RTA alerts the \nTL/OM immediately for corrective intervention. \nStep 5: Completion & Archiving \n●​ Action: RTA provides final confirmation to Support once the dates are secured. Support \nensures the WFM ticket is closed and all documentation is retained for audit purposes.​\n \n5. Quality Check / Expected Result \n●​ Result: All suspensions are visible in Zoho through the correct disciplinary category. \n●​ Success Metric: Zero instances of agents bypassings suspensions via leave credits or \nunauthorized system use. \n6. Records / Outputs \n●​ WFM Ticket History: Records of the request and coordination. \n●​ Zoho Suspension Logs: Official record for payroll and 201 file purposes. \n●​ NOD Documentation: Evidence of the disciplinary process.\nREST SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Walmart Support  \n| Workforce Rest Adjustment and Ticket Filing \n​\n1. Purpose/ Scope \nThis SOP ensures that agents who sacrifice their scheduled breaks/rest periods for work-related \nactivities (Coaching, Meetings, Clinic) are properly compensated with an adjusted rest period. \nThis prevents labor violations and maintains accurate Real-Time Adherence (RTA) tracking. \n2. Roles & Responsibilities \n●​ Team Leader (TL): Validates the overlap, creates the Zoho ticket in EST, and assigns \nthe new rest schedule to the agent. \n●​ Workforce (RTA): Audits the request against the agent\'s actual shift and \napproves/denies the adjustment based on documentation. \n●​ Agent: Attends the required activity and ensures they use the correct status/Aux code \nas instructed. \n3. Tools / Requirements \n●​ Zoho WFM Ticketing \n●​ Communication Channels (Cliq/Email)  \n●​ Required Evidence: Calendar invites or Meeting endorsements. \n \n4. Procedure (Steps) \nStep 1: The \"Overlap\" Audit Before filing, the TL must verify that the activity \n(Coaching/Meeting) physically prevents the agent from taking their break at the scheduled time. \n●​ Note: If the meeting ends before the break starts, no ticket is needed. \nStep 2: Ticket Creation & Categorization Log into Zoho Desk → RTA/WFM Ticketing. \n●​ Category: Select Meeting, Coaching, or Clinic. \n●​ Issue Type: Select Rest Adjustment / Rest Ticket.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \nStep 3: Data Entry (The \"Precision\" Step) All times must be logged in EST. Use the following \ntemplate in the description box to ensure 100% approval: \nREST ADJUSTMENT REQUEST \n●​ Agent Name: [Name] \n●​ Activity Type: [Coaching/Meeting/Clinic] \n●​ Activity Time: [Start Time] - [End Time] EST Military Time  \n●​ Affected Break: [e.g., 1st 15-min Break] \n●​ Requested New Break Time: [Start Time] - [End Time] EST Military Time  \nStep 4: Evidence Attachment You must attach proof to the ticket based on the following \npriority: \n1.​ Calendar Invite: Screenshot of the Outlook/Zoho calendar invite showing the time. \n2.​ Meeting Endorsement: Screenshot of the Slack/Teams message from the OM or Client. \n3.​ Clinic Slip: Digital endorsement from the Medical Team.​\n​\nEvidences are required but not mandatory \nStep 5: RTA Approval & Real-Time Tracking Once the ticket is submitted, the TL must \nmonitor the status. \n●​ Approved: Notify the agent of their new time schedules.  \n●​ Action: Instruct the agent to remain on the \"Coaching\" or \"Meeting\" Aux during the \nactivity, then move to \"Break\" Aux during their new adjusted time. \n5. Exceptions & Escalation \nIssue \nAction \nEscalate To \nLate Filing \nIf filed <30 mins before the activity, add a \"Reason for \nDelay\" in the description. \nRTA  \nOverlap Conflict If a meeting is extended, update the existing ticket \ninstead of creating a new one. \nRTA\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \nSystem \nDowntime \nSend the Rest Adjustment details via the Cliq/Email \nchannel. \nRTA Lead \n​\n6. Quality Check / Expected Result \n●​ Sync Check: The agent’s Zoho timeline should show the activity Aux (Coaching) \nfollowed by the Break Aux without any \"Unaccounted\" gaps. \n●​ Payroll Accuracy: No deductions should occur for rest periods consumed by work \nactivities. \n7. Records / Outputs \n●​ Zoho Rest Ticket ID: To be kept by the TL for end-of-week reporting. \n●​ RTA Adherence Report: Shows the updated schedule. \n \nTL Pro-Tips for Success: \n●​ EST Conversion: Double-check your time conversion. 90% of rejections are due to \nPST/Local time errors in the description. \n●​ Don\'t Double-Plot: Never tell an agent to plot their own break if a ticket is still \n\"Pending.\" Wait for the RTA \"Approved\" status.\nClient Tool SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Walmart Support  \n| Client Tool Management & Operational Access \n \n​\n1. Purpose/ Scope \nTo establish a standardized process for managing the primary client tools (VDI, IC, and \nServiceNow). This SOP ensures that the Support Team can maintain uninterrupted operations, \nverify accurate agent tracking, and uphold client-specific workload compliance. \n2. Roles & Responsibilities \n●​ Agent: Must utilize VDI as the entry point for all work and report any tool-specific \nblockers immediately via the official tracker. \n●​ Team Leader (TL): Responsible for verifying that agents are logged into all three tools, \nauditing IC status vs. Zoho status, and documenting technical downtime for their team. \n●​ RTA Team: Monitors real-time status in IC and workload volume in ServiceNow; \nperforms forced logouts during technical emergencies. \n●​ Operations Manager (OM): Oversees tool compliance and reviews ServiceNow data to \nensure SLA targets are met. \n3. Tools / Requirements \n●​ VDI (Virtual Desktop Infrastructure) \n●​ InContact (IC) \n●​ ServiceNow (SNow) \n●​ Internal VDI/PC Issue Tracker \n \n4. Procedure (Steps) \nStep 1: Access Verification (VDI) \nSupport must ensure all agents enter the \"Virtual Environment\" first. \n●​ Action: Confirm the agent is logged into the VDI. \n●​ Critical Rule: Without VDI, agents have zero access to the client network. If VDI fails, \nthe agent is effectively \"System Down.\" \nStep 2: Time & Activity Synchronization (InContact)\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \nOnce in VDI, agents must launch InContact (IC). \n●​ Tracking: TLs must check that agents are using the correct Aux codes for productive \nand offline work. \n●​ Comparison: IC is the Client\'s Source of Truth. TLs should spot-check to ensure IC \nstatus matches the agent\'s Zoho status. \n●​ Supervisory Action: If an agent is \"stuck\" in a status due to a crash, the TL must \ncoordinate with the RTA to perform a forced logout. \nStep 3: Workload & SLA Monitoring (ServiceNow) \nSNow is the \"Source of Truth\" for production. Support uses this to monitor output. \n●​ Action: Ensure agents are checking SNow for claim/line assignments. \n●​ Monitoring: Support monitors SNow to track volume inflow and ensure claims are being \nclosed within the agreed-upon SLA. \nStep 4: Technical Issue Documentation (SOI/EOI) \nIn the event of a tool failure (VDI, IC, or SNow), Support must enforce the reporting protocol. \n●​ Action: Direct the agent to log the incident in the VDI/PC Issue Tracker Channel. \n●​ Required Data: \n○​ SOI (Start of Issue): The exact minute the access was lost. \n○​ EOI (End of Issue): The exact minute access was restored. \n●​ Verification: TLs must review these logs daily to protect agent productivity metrics. \n \n5. Exceptions & Escalation \nIssue \nAction \nEscalate To \nTotal VDI Blackout \nMove all affected agents to \"System Down\" in \nZoho. \nClient IT / RTA  \nIC/Zoho Mismatch \nInform TL to issue a corrective reminder to the \nagent to sync statuses. \nTL\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \nSNow Assignment \nErrors \nManually reassign claims or lines if the \nauto-assigner (Latte)  fails. \nRTA  \n \n6. Quality Check / Expected Result \n●​ Data Integrity: All agents should show as \"Active\" in both IC and SNow simultaneously. \n●​ Productivity: ServiceNow \"Closed Claims\" count should align with the agent’s \nproductive hours logged in InContact. \n7. Records / Outputs \n●​ VDI/PC Issue Tracker Logs: Used for uptime reporting. \n●​ InContact Activity Reports: Used for client-side attendance auditing. \n●​ ServiceNow SLA Reports: Used for performance reviews. \n \nSupport Pro-Tips: \n●​ VDI Priority: Always troubleshoot the VDI first. If VDI is broken,agent remains \nunproductive since the majority of the client tools are inside VDI.  \n●​ SOI/EOI Importance: Without a documented SOI/EOI, the client will consider the \ndowntime as \"Unaccounted,\" which negatively impacts the program\'s performance \nscore. \nRemember, No evidence means it never happened.\n👏 System Issue Reporting\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Walmart Support  \n| System Issue Reporting & Item Transfer Protocol \n \n1. Purpose/ Scope \nTo establish a clear workflow for Support staff when agents encounter \"stuck\" or \"erroring\" items \nin ServiceNow (SNW). This SOP ensures that non-actionable items (Ghost items) are properly \ndocumented via Google Forms and transferred to the RTA team to prevent queue inflation and \nmaintain accurate productivity reporting. \n2. Roles & Responsibilities \n●​ Team Leader (TL): Validates the agent\'s report, ensures the Google Form is submitted, \nand oversees the transfer of the item to an on-shift RTA. \n●​ Agent: Identifies the error and provides the necessary item identifiers and screenshots \nto the TL. \n●​ On-shift RTA: Receives the transferred items and validates that the handoff was \nsuccessful in the system. \n●​ RTA Lead: Serves as the escalation point for recurring system bugs or items that refuse \nto transfer. \n3. Tools / Requirements \n●​ SNW (ServiceNow): For item review and the actual transfer action. \n●​ Google Reporting Form: Official Link \n●​ Internal Chat (Cliq/Slack): For immediate coordination with the RTA. \n \n \n4. Procedure (Steps) \nStep 1: Item Identification & Categorization \nSupport must verify if the item reported by the agent meets the \"Non-Actionable\" criteria: \n●​ Ghost Items: Items that stay in the bucket despite being completed. \n●​ Erroring Items: Items displaying system failures or abnormal processing behavior. \n●​ Non-Actionable: Items marked \"Done\" but still appearing in the agent\'s active queue.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \nStep 2: Technical Validation \nBefore escalating, the TL must perform a \"Quick Audit\": \n●​ Action: Refresh the SNW workspace. \n●​ Action: Verify if the item is simply pending a delay from another department. \n●​ Requirement: If the issue persists after a refresh, proceed to Step 3. \nStep 3: Filing the Official Report \nDocumentation must occur before or during the transfer. \n●​ Action: Access the Google Form. \n●​ Required Data: Agent Name, Item ID, Screenshot of the error, and a brief description. \n●​ Critical: Do not skip the form; this is the only way the RTA team can track system bugs \nfor the client. \nStep 4: Item Transfer in SNW \nThe TL or Agent (under TL supervision) must move the item out of the agent\'s queue. \n●​ Action: Open the item in SNW and initiate the Transfer action. \n●​ Target: Select an On-shift RTA. \n●​ Fallback: If no RTA is online, transfer to John Vergara – Vendor (vn5774u). \n●​ Warning: If the system prevents a transfer, contact the RTA channel immediately. \nStep 5: Coordination & Confirmation \n●​ Action: Reach out to the receiving RTA via Cliq. \n●​ Confirmation: \"I have transferred Item ID [Number] to you; please confirm if visible.\" \n●​ Finality: Ensure the agent\'s queue is now clear of the reported item. \n \n \n5. Exceptions & Escalation \nIssue \nSupport Action \nEscalate To\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \nItem cannot be \ntransferred \nAttempt to \"Allow\" or \"Block\" the item; if it fails, \ntake a screenshot of the error. \nRTA  \nGoogle Form is \nDown \nDocument all details (Item ID/Reason) in a manual \nlog/email. \nRTA Team \nRTA cannot see item \nRe-verify the Item ID and ensure the transfer was \n\"Submitted\" in SNW. \nRTA Lead \nRecurring Bug \nFlag the specific Item IDs that repeatedly reappear. RTA Lead \n \n6. Quality Check / Expected Result \n●​ Successful Outcome: The \"Ghost\" item is removed from the agent\'s view, and a \ncorresponding Google Form entry exists for the RTA\'s audit. \n●​ Traceability: Every transferred item has a documented \"Ticket/Form\" trail. \n7. Records / Outputs \n●​ Google Form Responses: Serves as the master log for system issues. \n●​ SNW Audit Trail: Shows the transfer from Agent to RTA.",
  Agent: "ECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n1 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nREVISED COMPANY HANDBOOK \nECEnterprises Group Manila, Inc. \nVersion 1 \nEffective March 1, 2025 \nReviewed January 21, 2026\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n2 \n \n \nOBJECTIVE \n \nECEnterprises Group Manila, Inc. (hereafter ECE, ECE Consulting, or Company) establishes \nthis Company Employee Handbook which aims to provide the standards of discipline and \nprofessionalism necessary for smooth operations and excellent customer service. This serves \nas a guide for the conduct expected of all employees in relation to Company Management, \ncustomers and co-workers. \n \nThe Company Employee Handbook also contains a Code of Conduct to ensure corrective (at \nthe same time, punitive) actions which are consistent, fair and prompt.  \n \nThe Company Employee Handbook applies to all employees regardless of status and position \nand shall cover all violations committed during work hours and/or in the work premises, except \nwhen otherwise indicated herein. \n \n \nECE CORE VALUES \n \nServant Leadership \nWe are not here to be anyone\'s \"boss\", we want to be their mentor, their support, and help \nthem achieve their goals.  We are therefore here to serve our subordinates, not to grow our \nown egos by being \"the person in charge\". \n \nFront-line First Mentality \nOur business is customer service, and our product is the people who directly interact with our \nclients’ customers.  Therefore, the front-line troops must be the focus of everything we do.  The \nsupport team is there for one reason - to help the front-line troops get better.  Our frontliners \nshould feel appreciated, well taken care of, and happy to work for a company that treats them \nlike the professionals they are, vs. like \"agents\".  If we take great care of our frontliners, they \nwill take great care of our client\'s customers. \n \nMeritocracy \nWe must only reward and promote based on performance.  We must never favor anyone, give \npreferential treatment to anyone, or become close personal friends with people we also need \nto manage.  Everyone must believe fully that the company evaluates them strictly on their \ncontribution, and not on any personal preferences by the leaders.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n3 \n \n \nClient Focus \nIf we don\'t have happy clients, we don\'t have a business period.  Knowing that, we need to do \nwhatever it takes to make our clients extremely satisfied.  Clients are the foundation that \neverything else is built on.  We need to appreciate all of our clients, and help our teams do the \nsame.  They are the reason we are in business and thriving. \n \nKaizen \nKaizen is the Japanese term for \"continuous improvement.  Our processes are never \nperfect.  Leaders need to constantly review how we perform the work, looking for incremental \nimprovements that will make us more valuable to our clients. \n \nNever Be Satisfied \nNo matter how well you are performing, and your team is performing, you should never be \nsatisfied.  We can always get better.  Once we think we are where we need to be, that\'s when \ncomplacency sets in.  We need to always strive and work hard to get better every day, as \nindividuals and as a group. \n \nCoach for Improvement, Leaving Dignity Intact \nWe always want to treat our subordinates with respect, even when they have significant areas \nof opportunity.  You can hold someone accountable without making it sound disrespectful or \ncondescending.  We learn how to stick with facts, explain things clearly, and tell them \'why\' \nwe need the behavior change, what\'s in it for them to improve (WIIFM), and why it\'s important \nto the client and our company to maintain excellent performance.  We call this \"explaining the \n\'why\'\". \n \nPromote from Within \nWe want our front-line team members to see that there is significant career growth opportunity \nat ECE.  We need to constantly develop the \"next person up\" so when projects grow, or new \nprojects come in, high performing frontliners can grow their careers.  We need to invest the \ntime in identifying and developing high potential frontliners, so they are ready to grow when \nthe time comes.  \n \nMISSION: \n \nOur mission is to become the world\'s most employee-centric BPO. We take care of our people, \nand they take care of our clients\' customers. We empower them to deliver extraordinary \ncustomer experience, driving mutual success to our clients and our business.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n4 \n \n \nTable of Contents \n \n7 \n \nIntroduction \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n8 \n \nPart 1: General Employment Information \n \n8 \n \nSection 1: Equal Employment Opportunity \n \n8 \n \nSection 2: Harassment Policy  \n12 \n \nSection 3: Employment Classification  \n12 \n \nA. Probationary Employment  \n13 \n \nB.  Regular employment \n13 \n \nC. Project employment \n \n13 \n \nSection 4: Personnel Records  \n13 \n \nSection 5: Employee Solicitation and Distribution \n \n14 \n \nSection 6:  Dress Code, Attire and Grooming Policy \n \n15 \n \nPart 2: Attendance and Timekeeping  \n15 \n \nSection 1: Attendance at Work \n15 \n \nA. Absenteeism \n \n16 \n \nB. Leave / Paid Leave  \n16 \nC. Notification Procedure for Tardiness/Absenteeism Due to Emergency or \nJustifiable Circumstance \n \n16 \n \nD. No-Call, No-Show \n17 \n \nSection 2: Workdays and Hours of Work \n \n17 \n \nA. Workweek and Rest Days \n \n17 \n \nB. Overtime \n \n17 \n \nC. Undertime  \n18 \n \nD. Holidays \n