of Company Property  \n35 \n \nE.  Provisions on Health Safety and Security \n \n37 \n \nF.  Provisions on Confidentiality \n \n38 \n \nSection 3: Kinds of Offenses  \n40 \n \nSection 4:  Kinds of Penalties  \n41 \n \nSection 5: Non-Punitive Measures \n \n41 \n \nSection 6: Effects of Penalties  \n42 \n \nNTE Rollback \n42 \n \nSection 7: Circumstances that Affect Administrative Liability  \n43 \n \nSection 8: Administrative Jurisdiction/Procedures \n \n43 \n \nA.  Administering Officer \n \n43 \n \nB. Procedure  \n44 \n \nC. Implementation of Disciplinary Action \n \n44 \n \nSection 9: Progressive Discipline Categories  \n46 \n \nSection 10:  Table of Offenses and Penalties in Any Given 30-Day Period \n \n46 \n \nA. Provisions on Performance of Duties \n \n51 \n \nB. Provisions on Proper Conduct and Decorum \n \n55 \n \nC. Provisions on Honesty \n \n57 \n \nD. Provisions on Use and Care of Company Property  \n59 \n \nE. Provisions on Health, Safety and Security  \n61 \n \nF. Miscellaneous Provisions \n \n62 \n \nPart 8: Mandatory Workplace Policies  \n62 \n \nSection 1: Drug-Free Workplace Policy \n67 \n \nSection 2: Workplace Policy on Mental Health \n68 \n \nSection 3: Workplace Policy on Safe Spaces \n70 \n \nPart 9: Ethics and Decorum \n70 \n \nSection 1: Conflicts of Interest \n70 \n \nSection 2: Strict prohibition on accepting kickbacks or bribes\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n6 \n \n72 \n \nSection 3: Guidelines on Personal relationship within the Company \n74 \n \nSection 4: Data Privacy \n77 \n \nPart 10: Revisions \n77 \n \nDate of Effectivity, Miscellaneous and Transitory Provisions \n78 \n \nAcknowledgement Receipt\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n7 \n \n \nIntroduction \n \nECEnterprises Group Manila, Inc. (hereafter ECE, ECE Consulting, or Company) operates in \nthe interests of its customers, shareholders, and employees, by providing the best possible \nservices and products. It is the responsibility of all employees to: \n \n \n• \nEnsure the satisfaction of the Company’s customers; \n• \nTreat all individuals with respect; \n• \nBe fair and consistent with all fellow employees; and \n• \nUnderstand the Company’s Employee Handbook. \n \nThis Company Employee Handbook has been designed and developed for all employees of \nthe Company to assist it in carrying out these responsibilities successfully for the interest of \nthe Company. \n \nAll employees are expected to be familiar with and abide by the policies in this Company \nEmployee Handbook. It is made known to all employees upon their employment with the \nCompany. All those with authority to do so have a duty to administer these policies fairly and \nconsistently. It is the responsibility of supervisors / direct managers to communicate and \ndistribute these policies and procedures to all employees. Copies of these policies are also \nmade available and accessible to all employees via computer network.  \nIf you have questions on employment laws or interpretation of any of the policies and \nprocedures in this Company Employee Handbook, you may contact the Human Resources \nDepartment (HRD) or the Administrative Manager for clarification. In rare and exceptional \ncircumstances, exceptions to policies may be made by the Management.   \nFrom time to time, policies and procedures in this Company Employee Handbook may be \nchanged, amended or revoked, at any time, at the discretion of Management. All changes, \namendments, or revocation of the policies and procedures in this Company Employee \nHandbook will be communicated to all employees immediately.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n8 \n \n \nPart 1: General Employment Information \n \nSection 1: \nEqual Employment Opportunity \n \nIt is the policy of the Company to provide equal employment opportunity to all employees and \napplicants for employment and not to discriminate on any basis prohibited by law, including \nrace, color, sex, age, religion, national origin, disability, marital status or veteran status.  It is \nour intent and desire that equal employment opportunities will be provided in employment, \nrecruitment, selection, compensation, benefits, promotion, demotion, layoff, termination and \nall other terms and conditions of employment. The President of the Company and all \nmanagerial personnel are committed to this policy and its enforcement. \n \nAny employee(s) with questions or concerns about any type of discrimination in the workplace \nare encouraged to bring these to the attention of their immediate supervisor or the HRD.  \n \nAny employee who violates this policy or knowingly retaliates against an employee reporting \nor complaining of a violation of this policy shall be subject to immediate disciplinary action, up \nto and including dismissal. Complaints brought under this policy will be promptly investigated \nand handled with due regard for the privacy and respect of all involved. \n \nSection 2: \nHarassment Policy \n \nECE will not tolerate harassment or intimidation of our employees on any basis prohibited by \nlaw, including race, color, sex, age, religion, national origin, handicap, disability, marital status, \nor veteran status. Moreover, any suggestions made to any employee that sexual favors will \naffect any term or condition of employment with the Company will not be tolerated. It is the \npolicy of the Company that any harassment, including acts in creating a hostile work \nenvironment or any other discriminatory acts directed against our employees, will result in \ndiscipline, up to and including termination from employment. ECE also will not tolerate any \nsuch harassment of our employees by our clients or vendors. \nFor purposes of this policy, sexual harassment is defined as any type of sexually oriented \nconduct, whether intentional or not, that is unwelcome and has the purpose or effect of \ncreating a work environment that is hostile, offensive or coercive. The following are examples \nof conduct that, depending upon the circumstances, may constitute sexual harassment: \n \n• \nUnwelcome sexual jokes, language, epithets, advances or propositions; \n• \nWritten or oral abuse of a sexual nature, sexually degrading or vulgar words to describe \nan individual; \n• \nThe display of sexually suggestive objects, pictures, posters or cartoons;\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n9 \n \n• \nUnwelcome comments about an individual’s body; \n• \nAsking questions about sexual conduct; \n• \nUnwelcome touching, leering, whistling, brushing against the body, or suggestive, \ninsulting or obscene comments or gestures; \n• \nDemanding sexual favors in exchange for favorable reviews, assignments, \npromotions, or continued employment, or promises of the same. \n \nEmployees must bring any violation of this policy to the immediate attention of their supervisor \nor the Company President. ECE will thoroughly investigate all such claims with due regard for \nthe privacy of the individuals involved. Any employee who knowingly retaliates against an \nemployee who has reported workplace harassment or discrimination shall be subject to \nimmediate disciplinary action, up to and including termination from employment. \n \nComplaint Procedures \n \na. \nAn employee, who believes that he or she has been a victim of harassment, may \nmake a Complaint to the following, in order of preference: \n \n• \nImmediate Supervisor/Team Leader  \n• \nManager  \n• \nHRD \n \nA prompt investigation of harassment complaints will be conducted. \n \n \nb. The following actions should be taken to the extent possible upon receipt of the \nComplaint, including anonymous complaints, or the management’s knowledge of possible \nharassment, whether a complaint was made: \n \n \n• \nCarefully document the complaint or possible harassment. \n• \nDocument the alleged behavior and resolution desired by the complainant. \no \nThe complainant\'s desired resolution may aid the review process but will not \ndictate the final resolution. \no \nThe complainant\'s request not to investigate cannot be honored by \nmanagement. \n \n• \nRequest the complainant to prepare a written statement describing the incident. \n• \nAdvise the complainant that an immediate investigation will be conducted.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n10 \n \n• \nNotify the HRD to secure advice and to determine whether any immediate steps are \nneeded to alleviate the situation for the complainant.  \n• \nCounsel on investigations may be sought from external counsel \n \nCommittee on Decorum \n  \nCommittee formed by the Company to investigate sexual or gender-based harassment \ncomplaints in the Workplace.   \n• \nProject Managers \n• \nOperations Managers \n• \nAssistant Managers \n• \nHRD \n• \nClinic  \n \nIt is the policy of the Company that: \n• \nHarassment will not be tolerated or condoned under any circumstances  \n• \nEmployees can express concern about harassment to their supervisor or to the HRD. \n• \nAll harassment complaints will be treated seriously, empathically, quickly and privately \nto the extent possible.  All complaints will be investigated fairly and impartially. \n• \nThe determination of whether harassment has occurred will be made after considering \nall the facts involved.  Any employee who is found to have violated this policy will be \nsubject to appropriate corrective action up to and including termination from \nemployment. \n• \nEmployees will not be subjected to any retaliation as a consequence of bringing a \ncomplaint to the attention of management or for acting as a witness in an \ninvestigation.  Any employee found guilty of such actions will face corrective action up \nto and including termination from employment. \n \nA component of this policy is sexual and gender-based sexual harassment. Sexual \nharassment has been defined by law as either Work, Education, or Training-related.  It \ninvolves employees, managers, supervisors, agents of the employer, instructor, trainer, or any \nother person, who have influence or moral ascendancy over another in a work environment, \ndemands, requests or otherwise requires any sexual favor from the other, regardless of \nwhether the demand, request or requirement for submission is accepted by the object of said \nAct.  \nOn the other hand, gender based sexual harassment is committed either on the streets and \npublic places, online, and in the Workplace, educational and training institutions. Gender \nbased sexual harassment is committed regardless of influence or moral ascendency by one\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n11 \n \nover another. Finally, gender based sexual harassment is committed through unwanted or \nuninvited sexual actions or remarks against any person regardless of motive.  \nIt is the policy of the Company that all employees should enjoy good working \nrelationships.  This means that everyone should feel comfortable in the Workplace and that \nindividual differences should be respected. An important part of good working relationships is \nthat everyone must be able to work in an environment free from harassment in any form. \nHarassment in the Workplace can take many forms.  It can be overt or subtle, direct or indirect, \nverbal or nonverbal, and physical or otherwise.  \n \nSome forms of verbal harassment include, but not limited to: \n• \nSexual or suggestive remarks \n• \nMaking fun of someone \n• \nImitating someone’s accent \n• \nPropositions (sexual invitations) \n• \nSpreading rumors \n• \nObscene and/ or unsolicited telephone calls, letters, email messages or faxes \n• \nRepeated unwelcome invitations \n• \nOffensive jokes  \n• \nSuggestive jokes or leers \n• \nRepeated questions about personal life \n• \nThreats or insults \n• \nThe use of language that is not suitable in the Workplace \n• \nName-calling and/or catcalling \n \nSome forms of non-verbal harassment include, but are not limited to: \n• \nPutting sexually suggestive, offensive or degrading/ insulting material on walls, \ncomputer screensavers, wallpapers, emails, communication channels \n• \nUnwelcome practical jokes \n• \nDisplaying or circulating racist, sexist cartoons or literature \n• \nMimicking someone with disability \n• \nFollowing someone home from work without their consent \n• \nOffensive hand or body gestures \n• \nUnnecessarily leaning over someone\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n12 \n \n• \nSending material that could be regarded as offensive through computer, fax, or email, \nand through any business communication channels \n• \nWolf whistling  \n \nSome forms of physical harassment include, but are not limited to: \n• \nUnwelcome physical contact (pinching, patting, brushing up against a person, \ntouching, kissing and hugging) \n• \nIndecent or sexual assault or attempted assault \n• \nPushing, shoving or jostling \n• \nPutting hand or an object into someone’s pocket \n \nSection 3: \nEmployment Classification \n \nThe Company shall maintain a clear set of guidelines on employment classification as a guide \nfor setting security of tenure and formulating compensation packages for everyone. \n \nEach employee will be given an employment status that reflects the impact of several factors \nsuch as length of service, performance, and acquired skills during their progress in the \nCompany. \n \nA. Probationary Employment \n \nAn employee will be required to undergo a probationary period of up to six (6) months, \nequivalent to 180 days, to enable the company to determine their fitness and capacity to work \nand achieve the goals of the organization. It is during this period that the company will \ndetermine whether the employees’ overall performance and work behavior met the criteria to \nmerit a permanent regular position at the end of the probationary period. \nAt the time of an employees’ engagement, the Management shall make known to the \nemployee the standards by which he/she will qualify as a regular employee. The standards \nshall include, but shall not be limited to, work performance, attendance, attitude, and conduct. \nDuring this period, both the employee and the Company will determine the suitability and \ninterest for the work which the employee has been hired to do. At any time during the \nprobationary period, the employee or the Company may terminate employment based on set \nand agreed separation or termination guidelines in accordance with existing laws and \nprevailing jurisprudence. \nTo ensure that the employee fits the requirements for regularization, he/she will be evaluated \non the third and fifth month of the probationary period of employment or as often as necessary \nas the company sees fit. If the performance standards and other factors are met, the regular \nstatus is awarded at the end of the 6-month probationary period. Otherwise, employment will\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n13 \n \nbe terminated accordingly within a reasonable period prior to the expiration of the 6-month or \n180-day probationary period. \nExtension of probationary period will be dealt with according to the labor laws. \n \nB.  Regular employment \n \nEmployee is deemed regular if he or she has satisfactorily met the requirements and standards \nof the Company and has satisfied the requirements for passing the probationary period. \nAn employment shall be deemed to be regular where the employee has been engaged to \nperform activities which are usually necessary or desirable in the usual business or trade of \nthe employer, unless otherwise agreed upon by the parties. \n \nC. Project employment \n \nProject Employment is employment that is fixed for a specific project or undertaking the \ncompletion or termination of which has been determined at the time of the engagement of the \nemployee. Project employment shall be terminated at the completion of the project or the \nphase of the project for which an employee is hired. \n \nSection 4: \nPersonnel Records \n \nIt is the responsibility of every employee to promptly notify the company of any changes in \ntheir personal data. This includes the correct address, telephone number, marital status, \ndependents and educational accomplishments. Without any proper notice, the record on file \nof every employee is deemed correct, current and updated for purposes of serving notices, \namong others. \n \nSection 5: \nEmployee Solicitation and Distribution \n \nThe Company’s official social communication platforms and bulletin boards are used to \nprovide necessary communication and information to our employees. These online platforms \nare only for the Company’s use to provide business related notices. Only designated \nmanagement or HRD representatives are allowed to post items on the bulletin board.  The \nCompany highly discourages postings, of any kind, unrelated to business. \n \nIn order to minimize distractions that could affect the performance of its employees, the \nCompany, as a general rule, does not permit the solicitation or distribution of materials of any \nkind. Solicitation or distribution of materials of any kind is discouraged in the \nWorkplace.  Solicitation includes, but not limited to, the sale of products or raffle tickets,\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n14 \n \nrequests for donations or contributions, solicitation for lotteries or contests, and solicitation for \nmembership in support of any organization or cause.   \n \nSection 6: Dress Code, Attire and Grooming Policy \n \nECE strives to maintain a workplace environment that is well functioning and free from \nunnecessary distractions and annoyances. As part of that effort, the company requires \nemployees to maintain a neat and clean appearance that is appropriate for the workplace \nsetting and for the work being performed.  \n \nTo that end, ECE department heads may determine and enforce guidelines for workplace-\nappropriate attire and grooming for their areas. \n \nInappropriate Clothing Attire: \n \n• \nRevealing and provocative clothing or outfits such as plunging necklines, see-\nthrough clothing, tank tops, spaghetti straps, muscle shirts, midriffs, cropped tops \nand high-slit skirts; \n• \nClothing that has inappropriate messaging; and \n• \nShorts above the knees, sandos/sleeveless, sport jerseys, ripped jeans, slippers, \ncrocs (exemption to the rule shall apply only during typhoons or conditions that \nrequire to wear slippers on the way to the office)\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n15 \n \n \nPart 2: Attendance and Timekeeping \n \nSection 1: \nAttendance at Work \n \nEach employee is important to the overall success of our operation.  Consequently, an \nemployee is expected to report to work on time on the agreed shift, i.e., time and day of work.  \nThe company depends on its employees to be at work during the period and locations \nscheduled. Note that the attendance record, including excessive absenteeism and/or \ntardiness is a part in the assessment of the overall performance rating, may be included during \nthe review and may be considered for disciplinary action up to and including termination, \nespecially if tardiness and/or absenteeism has become gross and habitual. The corresponding \npenalties for absenteeism and/or tardiness are indicated in the Code of Conduct. It must be \nnoted that probationary employees who received any disciplinary action whether relating to \nattendance or performance may not be regularized. \nIf an employee is unable to report for work, it is important that he/she uses the Zoho \nAttendance notification process or, in the absence of internet connection, calls the supervisor \nor immediate superior as soon as possible prior to starting time. State the reason for the \nabsence and disclose the expected date of return. This procedure is intended to enable \nManagement to make the necessary adjustments to assure production continues and the work \nis covered. Absence without notification will incur a negative consequence and may result in \nabsence without official leave.  \n \nIMPORTANT NOTE: It is each employee\'s responsibility to keep the Company informed as to \ntheir current personal email and physical addresses. \n \nThe company created an Attendance Point System (APS) scheme to address all attendance \nissues. Please refer to the memo for all information regarding APS. \n \nA. Absenteeism \n \n• \nExcessive and unreasonable absenteeism in one (1) calendar month shall be a cause \nfor disciplinary action possibly up to dismissal. \n• \nAbsence from work for three days without proper notification shall be tagged as \nAbsence Without Leave (AWOL) and shall be a valid cause for termination of \nemployment. Absence means leave credits are not yet available or leave credits have \nbeen exhausted. \n• \nAn employee who is tagged as AWOL based on the above description shall be sent a \nReturn to Work Order (RTWO) on the third day to his last known recorded residential \naddress, by electronic mail sent through his/her official company email or the \nemployee’s personal email the address reflected on his/her employment record \nwhereby the employee shall be required to report to HRD not later than 24 hours and\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n16 \n \nexplain in writing the reason for going on AWOL. If management finds such an \nexplanation valid, such an employee may be reinstated; otherwise, such an employee \nwill be terminated accordingly. \n• \nIf an employee does not respond to the RTWO within 5 calendar days upon receipt or \nthe latest unsuccessful delivery attempt, then the employee will be considered as \nhaving abandoned his/her work and as such, proper termination procedures will be \nundertaken. \n \nB. Leave / Paid Leave \n \na. All employees are required to submit their vacation leave application seven (7) \ncalendar days prior to the actual leave date. The leave application must be approved \nprior to taking the leave, otherwise leave without express approval from the manager \nwill be considered as unpaid leave. \nb. A sick leave application must be completed on the day an employee returns to work \nand is accompanied by a valid medical certificate. \n \nC. Notification Procedure for Tardiness/Absenteeism Due to Emergency or Justifiable \nCircumstance \n \n• \nAn employee who will unexpectedly be late or absent from work due to emergency or \nunavoidable but justifiable circumstances must notify their supervisor at least two (2) \nhours prior to the start of the work shift and likewise, provide the reason for the \ntardiness or absence. \n• \nEmployees should initiate the notification through Zoho attendance notification or call \ntheir respective “bat phones” at least 2 hours before and 2 hours after that s/he will be \nout of work.    \n• \nFailure to properly communicate tardiness or absence and/or to follow the notification \nprocedure will be considered as an AWOL of which, will be meted out with the \ncorresponding and/or appropriate disciplinary action.  \n• \nAny medical and dental appointments which are not emergency in nature should be \nscheduled outside the assigned work hours or shift; otherwise, they may be considered \nas absences without pay.  \n \nD. No-Call, No-Show \nAn employee who fails to observe the notification procedure in section C above and who fails \nto inform their Supervisor of their tardiness or absence from work at least two (2) hours before \nand two (2) hours after the start of their shift shall be considered to be on No-Call, No-Show \n(NCNS), hence absent, and will be dealt with accordingly.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n17 \n \nSection 2: \nWorkdays and Hours of Work \n \nThe Company generally follows a work schedule of 40 hours per week. All employees must \nclock in a minimum of eight (8) hours in the office daily, excluding the lunch break. The \nCompany also has accounts with different work hours set-up. All employees are required to \nreport for work on time in accordance with each individual\'s schedule and be ready to perform \nthe duties of their positions. \n \nA. Workweek and Rest Days \nThe standard work week schedule is commonly five (5) working days and two (2) rest days. \nHowever, this may differ based on department needs; thus, rest days may change accordingly. \n \nB. Overtime \nBased on normal work schedules, hours during which the employee performs work beyond \nthe minimum forty (40) hours weekly requirement is considered overtime work.  Non-\nproductive hours (vacation, holiday, paid time off, etc) will not count toward overtime \ncompensation.  All requests for overtime work must be authorized in writing by the supervisor \nor department manager. \nEmployees who do overtime work (work rendered in excess of eight (8) hours under normal \nwork schedules) will be compensated in accordance with existing laws. \nEmployees may volunteer for OT by following the procedures established to solicit overtime. \nOvertime will be rewarded based on the needs of the business. It is the employee’s \nresponsibility to ensure that the request for overtime has been approved before they are \nauthorized to work it. \nEmployees working overtime are subject to the same policies and procedures that govern \nregular scheduled hours, regardless of the length of the shift. Employees who are late for the \nbeginning of an approved overtime shift, or employees who defer from working the shift without \napproval, will be marked absent or late and treated in accordance with existing laws. \nIf the need of the business changes prior to the start of an employee’s overtime shift, and the \novertime is no longer required, employees will be released from the shift. Every attempt will \nbe made to notify employees scheduled to work overtime prior to the start of the overtime shift. \nIf the company’s overtime requirements change after the start of the shift, employees will be \npermitted to complete the shift. \n \nC. Undertime \nAn employee who intends to leave his work before the end of his work schedule/shift should \nseek approval from his supervisor/department manager.   The employee may opt to apply for \na half-day leave if he/she was able to complete the first half of his/her work schedule.  \nFailure to file for undertime is enough grounds for corrective action as stated in the Code of \nConduct.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n18 \n \nD. Holidays  \nThe following Philippine Holidays are observed by the Company: \n \nLegal Holidays \nSpecial Holidays \nNew Year’s Day – January 1  \nMaundy Thursday – Movable Date \nGood Friday – Movable Date \nAraw ng Kagitingan – April 9 \nLabor Day – May 1 \nIndependence Day – June 12 \nNational Heroes Day – Last Monday \nof August \nBonifacio Day – November 30 \nChristmas Day – December 25 \nRizal Day – December 30 \nEid al-Fitr – Movable Date \nEidul Adha – Movable Date \nNinoy Aquino Day – August 21 \nAll Saints Day – November 1 \nFeast of the Immaculate Conception of Mary – \nDecember 8 \nLast Day of the Year – December 31 \nOther local holidays announced by The President of \nthe Philippines \n \nNon-Exempt employees who render holiday work will be compensated in accordance with \nexisting laws. \n \nSection 3: \nTimekeeping Procedure \nIt is important for all employees to log-in and to log-out using their designated devices \naccordingly. Altering, falsifying or tampering with records, or recording time on another \nemployee\'s time record, shall constitute time fraud and is prohibited and may result in \ndisciplinary action up to and including termination, and the Company may avail itself of any \nand all applicable legal actions. \nAny errors in the attendance should be reported immediately to management, who will attempt \nto correct legitimate errors.  \n \nSection 4: \nPerfect Attendance \nEmployees with perfect attendance, zero tardiness, no undertime, overbreak and no filed \nsick leave for the entire year will receive an additional 20 hours leave credits as an \nincentive for being punctual. The additional leave will add up to his/her leave credit the \nfollowing year. The same will not be convertible to cash.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n19 \n \nSection 5: \nCritical Workdays  \nCritical workdays are defined as days where the clients expect the agents to come to work \ndespite the declaration of holidays both in the Philippines or the client’s location.  \n \n For purposes of US-based clients, the following are the critical workdays:  \n1. Memorial Day \n2. Independence Day (July 4th) \n3. Labor Day \n4. Veteran’s Day \n5. Thanksgiving Day \n6. Christmas Eve Day (December 24th) \n7. Christmas Day (December 25th) \n8. New Year\'s Eve Day (December 31st) \n9. New Year\'s Day (January 1st) \n10. Any other day as declared by the Clients per business requirement \n \nThe Philippine based holidays are governed by all the applicable sections of the Philippine \nLabor Code and the Company will compensate employees as required by such applicable \nlaws. \nThe Philippine holidays depend on the government’s yearly declaration, as enumerated under \nparagraph F of this section.  \n \nCritical workdays may also include the following: \n1. 1 Day before and after the actual pay roll out. \n2. Actual pay roll-out.   \n3. The day before and after a regular or special holiday.  \n4. The day before and after the agents rest days.   \n5. The day before or after the agents approved Vacation leave\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n20 \n \n \nPart 3: Statutory and Mandatory Benefits \n \nThe Company grants all leaves and benefits as required by law, including but not limited to \nmaternity leave, paternity leave, solo parent leave, battered woman leave, special leave \nbenefits for women, and any additional applicable mandatory leaves and benefits. \n \nSection 1: \n13th Month Pay \nRegardless of employment status, the employee will be entitled to the 13th month pay which \nwill be paid no later than December 24 of each year. This will be given on a pro-rata basis if \nthe employee has been with the Company for at least one month. \nThe “basic salary” of an employee for the purpose of computing the 13th month pay shall \ninclude all remuneration or earnings paid by his employer for services rendered. \nThe 13th month pay shall NOT include allowances and monetary benefits which are not \nconsidered or integrated as part of the regular or basic salary, such as: \n• \ncash equivalent of unused vacation and sick leave credit \n• \novertime premium \n• \nnight differential \n• \nholiday pay  \n \nFor employees who have worked for the Company for less than one year, the following formula \nshould be used: \n \nBasic Monthly Salary x No. of Months of Service \n_____________________________________ \n12 \n \nSection 2: \nSocial Security System (SSS) \n \nSocial security benefits are composed of a package of benefits under the Social Security \nProgram in the event of death, disability, sickness, maternity, old age and unemployment. \nWork-related death, disability and sickness also receive benefits under the Employees\' \nCompensation (EC) Programs. Basically, social security provides replacement for income lost \non account of the aforementioned contingencies. \nIn accordance with the law, all employees are registered with the SSS. Both the employee \nand the Company will pay a monthly premium contribution to the SSS, the rate of which will\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n21 \n \ndepend on the employee’s “gross”pay. The employee’s contributions are paid through salary \ndeductions.  \n \nSection 3: \nPhilHealth \n \nThe National Health Insurance Program (NHIP) is a compulsory health insurance program of \nthe government established to provide universal health insurance coverage and ensure \naffordable, acceptable, available and accessible health care services for all citizens of the \nPhilippines. Every month, employees are required to pay a certain amount, which is deducted \nfrom the employee’s salary. Likewise, the company pays an equal contribution on the \nemployee’s behalf. \nPhilHealth is implementing a unified benefit package for all PhilHealth members, which \nincludes the following categories of personal health services:  \n \n \nInpatient care \n• \nRoom and board;  \n• \nServices of health care professionals;  \n• \nDiagnostic, laboratory, and other medical examination services;  \n• \nUse of surgical or medical equipment and facilities;  \n• \nPrescription drugs and biologicals, subject to the limitations stated in Section 37 of RA \n7875;  \n• \nInpatient education packages.  \n \nOutpatient care \n• \nServices of health care professionals;  \n• \nDiagnostic, laboratory, and other medical examination services; \n• \nPersonal preventive services;  \n• \nPrescription drugs and biologicals, subject to the limitations described in Section 37 of \nRA 7875; \n• \nEmergency and transfer services \n \nSection 4: \nPAG-IBIG \n \nIn accordance with the law, all employers and their employees covered by the SSS shall pay \na monthly contribution to the Pag-IBIG Fund. As a member of Pag-IBIG, the employee is\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n22 \n \nentitled to avail of basic housing and emergency loans if the employee meets the required \nnumber of monthly contributions: \n \nMultipurpose Loan \n• \nMinor Home Improvement  \n• \nLivelihood  \n• \nMedical  \n• \nEducational  \n• \nPurchase of appliance and furniture  \n• \nOther immediate needs  \n \nCalamity Loan  \n• \nThe Program provides financial assistance to Pag-IBIG member-victims in calamity-\nstricken areas. \n• \n \nPag-IBIG Housing Loan \n• \nHome Improvement \n• \nConstruction \n• \nHouse & Lot \n \nProvident Savings \n• \nPag-IBIG Regular Savings \n• \nModified Pag-IBIG 2 (MP2) Savings\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n23 \n \n \nPart 4: Company Benefits \n \nSection 1: \nVacation Leave (VL)  \n \nA. \nDefinition of Terms \n \nVacation Leave is a company-initiated benefit. \n \nB. \nVacation Leave eligibility \n \n• \nA permanent regular employee who has worked for at least twelve months \ncontinuously from their initial date of employment is entitled to one hundred \nhours (100 hrs) of vacation leave annually with pay. \n• \nFor new hires, VL will accrue starting on hiring date and can start using it upon \nregularization. \n• \nVL applications must be filed at least seven (7) calendar days prior to the actual \nleave date, using the Zoho Leave Application Form. \n• \nAll VL credits for the current year must be availed on or before the end of the \nfirst quarter of the succeeding year. \n• \nThe Management reserves the right to schedule or reschedule VL in \naccordance with the operational requirements. \n• \nEmployees with perfect attendance and zero tardiness for the entire year shall \nreceive an additional twenty-hour (20 hrs) VL credits for the next year. This will \nnot be converted to cash, if unused. \n \nC. \nSpecial rules on Vacation Leave \n \n• \nApplication for Leave should be properly signed / approved by the Immediate \nSupervisor. Otherwise, the said leave shall be considered disapproved / \nunauthorized / AWOL. \n• \nManagerial/Supervisorial Employees should secure approval from Top \nManagement. \n• \nTerminal Leaves (using VL credits) is subject to immediate supervisor’s \napproval, otherwise this will be considered disapproved / unauthorized / AWOL. \n• \nEmployees under suspension are not allowed to use / apply their VL credits. \nFurther, for separated or terminated employees for just or authorized cause, \nVL is not convertible to cash.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n24 \n \n• \nEmployees required to go on extended vacation leave must present signed \ndocumentation/proof indicating their reason and expected length of absence. \nFollowing the full usage of all leave credits for any current year, the employee \nwill incur appropriate salary deductions without any bearing on performance. \n• \nVacation leaves accrual for all outgoing employees will be prorated based on \nactive employment and supersede the advance crediting of leaves awarded \nevery January for eligible employees. There will be a deduction from their final \npay for the leaves taken in excess of the pro-rated credits. \n \nSection 2: \nSick Leave (SL)  \n \nA. Definition of Terms \n \nSick Leave (SL) is a company-initiated benefit wherein an employee is allowed to be absent \nfrom work (with pay) on account of sickness or involvement in an accident resulting in injuries \nto such an employee. \n \nB. \nSick leave eligibility \n \n• \nA permanent regular employee who has worked for at least twelve months \ncontinuously from their initial date of employment is entitled to fifty hours (50-\nhours) annual sick leave credits with pay  \n• \nEmployees shall be required to submit a medical certificate for at least two (2) \nconsecutive days for any health-related leave or based on the management’s \ndiscretion/needs.  \n• \nAny sick days in excess of the allowable leave credits will not be compensated \nand shall be treated as unpaid leave.  \n• \nAny unused Sick Leave credits shall be converted to cash. \n• \nThe Management reserves the right to terminate employee’s services due to \nexcessive absences.  \n \nC. \nSpecial Rules on Sick leave  \n• \nEmployees who went on leave due to sickness should submit Zoho Attendance \nNotification, call/advise (text messages are allowed provided the immediate \nsupervisor confirms receipt) their Bat phones or respective immediate supervisor \nwithin the day of their absence; otherwise, they will be considered AWOL on the \nsaid date. \n• \nEmployees with no SL credits are also covered by the previous policy resulting in \ncorresponding unpaid leaves.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n25 \n \n• \nSL due to emergency cases such as accidents, fire, sudden death of immediate \nrelatives (i.e. parents, siblings, or grandparents), and similar circumstances will be \nconsidered upon presentation of necessary documents or proof justifying the said \nemergency upon reporting to work. \n• \nEmployees who will present false or tampered documents will be subject to \ndisciplinary action and may be meted with a penalty including dismissal from \nemployment. \n• \nDepartment heads or immediate superiors who will tolerate or approve \n“emergency” leave applications without necessary evidence pursuant to paragraph \n3 above will be sanctioned accordingly. \n• \nEmployees under suspension are not allowed to use or apply for SL credits. \n• \nEmployees required to go on extended medical leave must present signed \ndocumentation or proof indicating the reason and expected length of absence. \nFollowing full usage of all leave credits for any current year, the employee will incur \nappropriate salary deductions. Please refer to the Clinic MEDLOA Guidelines for \ncomplete details \n• \nThe employee who avails of the extended medical leave shall be required to submit \na valid Fit-to-Work certificate upon his/her return from his/her absences signed by \nhis/her attending physician. The employee must also undergo reassessment by \nthe Company physician to determine physical fitness to work. Said requisites must \nbe done or submitted prior to logging in for work. \n• \nThe utilization of sick leaves for recreation or vacation purposes shall not be \nallowed. If such misrepresentation becomes known to the Company or later \nrevealed to the Company, the employee shall be subject to disciplinary measures, \nor such misrepresentation may be a cause for termination of the employee.  \n• \nEmployees should seek medical attention from doctors and clinics accredited by \nthe company’s HMO or government hospitals.  \n• \nThe company reserves the right to verify the authenticity of the medical certificates \nor fit-to-work certificates presented by the employees.  \n• \nUpon submission of the medical certificate, the company shall verify such \ndocuments from the HMO accredited hospital or government hospital.   \n• \nSick leave accrual for all outgoing employees will be prorated based on active \nemployment and supersede the advance crediting of leaves awarded every \nJanuary for eligible employees. There will be a deduction from their final pay for \nthe leaves taken in excess of the prorated credits. \n \nSection 3: \nHalf-Day Leave \n \nA. \nDefinition of Terms \nHalf-Day Leave is a company-initiated benefit wherein time off equivalent to four (4) or five (5) \ncontinuous hours depending on the work schedule shall be considered as paid leave. These \nhalf-day leaves will be applied against any available sick leave or vacation leave, if any. If no \nsick leave or vacation leave is available, the half-day leave will be unpaid time off.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n26 \n \nB. \nSpecial Rules on Half-day leave \n \n• \nEmployees under suspension are not allowed to use / apply their VL credits \n• \nThe Management has the right and/or discretion to reschedule one’s leave. \n \nSection 4: \nBereavement Leave \n \nA. \nDefinition of Terms \n \nBereavement Leave is a company-initiated benefit wherein an employee is entitled to an \nadditional three (3) days paid leave in addition to their existing and current vacation and sick \nleave credits.  \n \nB. \nEligibility of Bereavement leave \n \nBereavement leave can be availed by employees regardless of employment status if an \nimmediate family member (as defined in section C.2. below) passes away. \n \nC. \nSpecial Rules on Bereavement leave  \n \n \n• \nEmployees shall inform HRD of the bereavement and shall present a copy of death \ncertificate to HRD in order to be credited as paid leave. \n• \nAn employee’s immediate family includes his/her spouse, children, parents, \nsiblings, parents-in-law, grandparents, and guardian/s, provided that a supporting \ndocument is submitted showing the employee’s relationship with the deceased. \n• \nTime taken for the purpose of bereavement will not be counted against the \nemployee when determining Attendance Incentive eligibility. Unused Bereavement \nLeave credits are not convertible to cash. \n \nSection 5: \nSolo Parent Leave Benefits Policy \n \nParental Leave of not more than seven (7) working days per year shall be granted to any solo \nparent employee who has rendered service for at least six (6) months. Parental Leave shall \nmean leave benefits granted to a solo parent to enable him/ her to perform parental duties and \nresponsibilities where physical presence is required.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n27 \n \n \nA solo parent is any individual who falls under the following categories: \n• \nA woman who gives birth as a result of rape and other crimes against chastity even \nwithout a final conviction of the offender: Provided, That the mother keeps and raises \nthe child; \n• \nParent who was left solo or alone with the responsibility of parenthood due to death of \nspouse; \n• \nParent who was left solo or alone with the responsibility of parenthood while the spouse \nis detained or is serving sentence for a criminal conviction for at least one (1) year; \n• \nParent who was left solo or alone with the responsibility of parenthood due to physical \nand/or mental incapacity of spouse as certified by a public medical practitioner; \n• \nParent who was left solo or alone with the responsibility of parenthood due to legal \nseparation or de facto separation from spouse for at least one (1) year, as long as \nhe/she is entrusted with the custody of the children;  \n• \nParent who was left solo or alone with the responsibility of parenthood due to \ndeclaration of nullity or annulment of marriage as decreed by a court or by a church as \nlong as he/she is entrusted with the custody of the children;  \n• \nParent who was left solo or alone with the responsibility of parenthood due to \nabandonment of spouse for at least one (1) year;  \n• \nUnmarried mother/father who preferred to keep and rear her/his child/children instead \nof having others care for them or give them up to a welfare institution;  \n• \nAny other person who solely provides parental care and support to a child or children, \nprovided said person is duly licensed as a foster parent by the DSWD or duly appointed \nlegal guardian by the Court; \n• \nAny family member who assumes the responsibility of head of family as a result of the \ndeath, abandonment, disappearance or prolonged absence of the parents or solo \nparent. \n \nEmployees who are eligible to the Solo Parent Leave Benefits Policy are those who have been \nissued an Eligibility identification card by the Department of Social Welfare and \nDevelopment.  The Human Resources Department should be informed by the employees if \nsuch eligibility has been granted. \nA change in the status or circumstance of the parent claiming benefits under this law, such \nthat he/she is no longer left alone with the responsibility of parenthood, shall terminate his/her \neligibility for these benefits. Failure to do so shall merit corrective action up, including \ntermination from employment as may be warranted under the circumstances. \nSolo Parent leave is not convertible to its cash equivalent. \n \nSection 6:  \nMaternity Leave Benefits Policy \n \nAll covered female workers, regardless of civil status or the legitimacy of her child, shall be \ngranted one hundred five (105) days maternity leave with full pay and an option to extend for \nan additional thirty (30) days without pay. Provided that in case the worker qualifies as a solo \nparent under the Solo Parents’ Welfare Act, the worker shall be granted an additional fifteen \n(15) days maternity leave with full pay.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n28 \n \nThe maternity leave is taken with pay as a Social Security benefit administered by the \nCompany. The Company advances the employee’s maternity benefit and recovers the said \nbenefit from the SSS. The daily maternity benefit is equivalent to one hundred percent (100%) \nof the employee’s average salary credit (SS Standards) for one hundred five (105) days \nregardless of whether she gave birth via cesarean section or natural delivery, while maternity \nleave of sixty (60) days with full pay shall be granted for miscarriage or emergency termination \nof pregnancy. An additional maternity leave of thirty (30) days, without pay, can be availed of, \nat the option of the female worker, subject to the following conditions: \n• \nThat the employer shall be given due notice, in writing, at least forty-five (45) days \nbefore the end of her maternity leave \n• \nThat no prior notice shall be necessary in the event of a medical emergency, but \nsubsequent notice shall be given to the immediate supervisor. \n \nSection 7: \nPaternity Leave Benefits Policy \n \nPaternity Leave of seven (7) working days with full pay is granted to a married male employee, \nregardless of his status in the Company, for the first four (4) deliveries of the legitimate spouse \nwith whom he is cohabiting.  The employee may avail themselves of this leave under the \nfollowing conditions: \n• \nHe is an employee at the time of the delivery of his child. \n• \nHe is cohabiting with his legitimate spouse at the time she gives birth or suffers a \nmiscarriage. \n• \nHe has applied for paternity leave; and \n• \nHis wife has given birth or suffered a miscarriage \nPaternity leave is not convertible to its cash equivalent. \n \nSection 8: \nLeave for Victims of Violence Against Women and Their Children (VAWC) \n \nLeave for victims of violence against women and their children refers to the paid leave granted \nto a female employee who is a victim of violence against women and their children allowing \nher to attend to medical and legal concerns at any time during the application of any protection \norder, investigation, prosecution and/or trial of the criminal case. \n \nViolence Against Women and Their Children (VAWC) \nVAWC  to any act or a series of acts committed by any person against a woman who is his \nwife, former wife, or against a woman with whom the person has or had a sexual or dating \nrelationship, or with whom he has a common child, or against her child whether legitimate or \nillegitimate, within or without the family abode, which result in or is likely to result in physical, \nsexual, psychological harm or suffering, or economic abuse including threats of such acts, \nbattery, assault, coercion, harassment or arbitrary deprivation of liberty.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n29 \n \nVictims of VAWC are entitled to take a paid leave of absence up to ten (10) days in addition \nto other paid leaves provided in this Handbook. Such leave is extendible when the necessity \narises as specified in their barangay protection order, temporary protection order and/or \npermanent protection order. \n \nTo be entitled to this benefit, the victim-employee only needs to present a certification from \nthe barangay chairman/punong barangay, barangay councilor/barangay kagawad, \nprosecutor, or Clerk of Court, as the case may be, that an action relative to the matter is \npending. \nVAWC leave is not convertible to cash and shall not be cumulative. \n \nSection 9: \nSpecial Leave for Women  \nSpecial leave for women refers to the paid leave granted to a female employee allowing her \nto recuperate following surgery caused by gynecological disorders for a period of up to two (2) \nmonths. \nThe special leave may be available for every instance of surgery due to gynecological disorder \nfor a maximum total period of two (2) months or sixty (60) calendar days per year. \n \nConditions for Entitlement: \n• \nShe has rendered at least six (6) months continuous aggregate employment \nservice. \n• \nShe has filed an application for special leave with the Company within seven (7) \ncalendar days from the expected date of surgery or within such period as may be \nprovided by company rules and regulations or collective bargaining agreement; \nand \n• \nShe has undergone surgery due to gynecological disorders as certified by a \nlicensed physician. \nThe special leave shall be non-cumulative and non-convertible to cash. \n \nSection 10: \nMedical Benefits \nAll employees shall be entitled to the Company’s Medical and Health Insurance upon \nregularization, subject to the guidelines provided by the appointed service provider. \n \nSection 11: \nNight Differential Pay  \nNight shifts pay is given as a premium for working at a time when the employee is supposed \nto sleep and rest in accordance with the law of nature.  \nNight Shift Differential is equivalent to 10% of an employee\'s regular wage for each hour of \nwork performed between 10:00 pm and 6:00am of the following day in accordance with \nexisting laws.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n30 \n \n \nPart 5: Policy on Home-Based Work \n \nThe Company provides a home-based work setup to protect the rights of workers and promote \ntheir welfare following technological development that has opened up alternative avenues for \nemployees to carry out their work such as telecommuting, and other flexible work \narrangements. \nThe Company ensures to create measures in order to prevent home-based employees from \nbeing isolated from the rest of the working community by giving the former the opportunity to \nmeet with colleagues on a regular basis, and by allowing access to relevant company \ninformation in relation to his/her duties and responsibilities. \n \nSection 1: \nDefinition of Terms \n \n• \nHome-based site - a facility designed for the purpose of performing the duties \nagreed between the Company and the employee located in a private dwelling or \npart thereof. \n• \nHome-based employee - an employee who works at the home-based site. \n• \nHome-based work - regular performance of duties at the home-based site as \nagreed between the Company and employee conducted within the specified work \nhours, unless otherwise approved. \n \nSection 2: \nGuidelines (Refer to WFH Guidelines Memorandum) \n \n• \nHome-based employees are hired on a voluntary basis upon such terms and \nconditions as the employer and employee mutually agreed upon, and as the \nemployee explicitly consent as set forth within their employment agreement. \n \n• \nEmployees must acknowledge the Zoho Item Accountability Form for the PC \nissued by ECE\'s Information Technology (IT) specialist. \n \n \n• \nHome-based employees must have appropriate internet connectivity at all times. \n35 mbps (both download and upload) is the minimum requirement for non-voice \naccounts, while 50  (both download and upload) is the minimum requirement for \nvoice accounts. Without the proper internet connection at home, an employee will \nbe required to work in the office or find an alternative work location. \n \n• \nHome-based \nemployees \nmay \nbe \nremotely \nmonitored \nthrough \nonline \nteleconference, video or audio calls, and/or through any messaging applications \nusing his/her laptop, computer, mobile or any other gadget. A home-based \nemployee may be remotely monitored through their computers and will be required \nto undergo the monthly remote PC scan. The employee is prohibited from using\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n31 \n \nthe office-issued PC for non-work-related matters, activities and undertakings that \nwould require the storage of personal files and documents in the office-issued PC.  \n \n \n• \nA home-based working arrangement will only be entered into on a voluntary basis \nas initiated by the Company. An employee cannot be directed to work from home \nexcept in emergency cases (i.e. power outage) and the Company is under no \nobligation to approve a request from an employee to enter into a working from \nhome arrangement. \n \n• \nA home-based working arrangement will only be considered valid upon formal \napproval by the Management. In the absence of authorization from the supervisor \nto work from home, employees are required to report to work at the office at all \ntimes. Home-based employees must also consent to the work arrangement before \nstarting home-based work. \n \n \n• \nHome-based employees must be available for contact by the client, supervisor and \nco-employees directly involved in the performance of his/her duties during the \nspecified hours of home-based work. \n \n• \nHome-based employees are required to advise their supervisor/team leader, \nthrough Attendance Cliq channel and/or the Client’s systems if required by the \nClient, if they are going to take a break/lunch, or at any time the employee will be \nout of contact for more than 15 minutes. Home-based employees may notify their \ndesignated point of contact (POC), Real-Time Analyst (RTA), Team Leader or \nQuality Assurance manager ONLY IF the immediate supervisor/team leader is \nunavailable or on leave. \n \n \n• \nIn the event of power outages, connectivity issues or any downtime, home-based \nemployees should notify (text messages are allowed provided the immediate \nsupervisor confirms receipt) their respective immediate supervisors of the situation \nand when resumption of work has taken place. A maximum of one (1) hour \ndowntime is allowed for home-based employees to resume normal operations \nbased on client’s discretion. Otherwise, home-based employees are expected to \ngo to the office or find another work location where they can resume work. \nDowntime and travel time will be considered outside of rendered work hours. \n \n• \nHome-based employees must use their company-issued desktop at all times \nexcept during these instances and when they got approval from IT and their \nImmediate Supervisor: \n \n \n• \nAll WFH employees must comply with IT’s directive regarding the monthly \nmaintenance of the company-issued device. \no All home-based devices are subject to proper usage by the home-based \nemployee as prescribed by the company handbook. \no Home-based employees are accountable to the functionality and care for \nthe home-based devices\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n32 \n \no Damages to home-based devices, whether intentional or involuntary, shall \nbe the accountability of the home-based employee. Normal wear and tear \nshall be the exception upon the examination of IT Specialists. \no Damages and issues must be reported to any IT specialist immediately for \nexamination.  \no Software settings shall be maintained to the defaults set by the IT \nDepartment, unless otherwise instructed to be set to another value by either \nthe management or the client. \no Software installations must be authorized by the management. \nUnauthorized software installations are prohibited and sanctionable.  \n \n• \nTroubleshooting \no Troubleshooting of home-based devices shall be done by IT provided the \nhome-based employee has reported the issue and a Zoho IT Ticket has \nbeen filed. \no Troubleshooting shall be exhaustively done remotely  \no Upon arriving at the conclusion that the devices cannot be fixed remotely, \nIT Specialists must update the supervising IT and provide a course of action \nto the home-based employee as prescribed by the Provisions on the \nLimitations of Home-based work devices. \n• \n \nHome-based employees must report to the office as required by their supervisor \nfor client site visits, training, work transition or any activity where the employees’ \nphysical attendance is necessary, unless the period of such activity conflicts with \nthe employees’ working hours. \n \n• \nA home-based-working arrangement may be discontinued or altered by giving a \nminimum of 14-day notice by the company in response to a change in duties \nrequired pursuant to business needs.  Home-based to office-based work transition \nshould be communicated to the RTA and IT specialists.  \n \n \n• \nHome-based work is a privilege extended to well performing employees and \nManagement reserves its right to revoke the said privilege upon the failure of the \nemployee to satisfy the minimum requirement of the home-based program.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n33 \n \n \nPart 6: Performance Appraisal \n \nEach employee is evaluated on a monthly basis through their monthly account business \nscorecard and a yearly evaluation for annualization increase. The review process will measure \nhow an employee assigned to a particular position performs following the basic standard \nperformance set by the Company.  \nThe review process will give an opportunity for the manager and the employee to discuss the \nnecessity to improve one’s performance in relation to the employee’s assigned tasks, duties \nand responsibilities. The employee will be appraised by their immediate supervisor who is \naware of the job description and daily performance that the employee’s role requires. \nAll employees are expected to pass the monthly evaluation with the specific criteria provided \nby the account or department. The employee is given a determined time frame to improve \nhis/her performance based on the accounts performance policy.  \nSerious and repeated unsatisfactory performance will be considered as “gross inefficiency” or \n“gross and habitual neglect of duty” on the part of the employee. Continuous poor performance \nratings or gross inefficiency may lead to an employee’s dismissal analogous to gross and \nhabitual neglect of duty which is a just cause for termination of employment.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n34 \n \n \nPart 7: Code of Conduct \n \nSection 1: Rationale \n \nThe company is entering into a new age where it is required not only to keep pace with the \nrapid developments in the industry but also to do business in a more competitive environment \nif it is to survive and grow. To be able to do so, it must be able to continue to always provide \nexcellent service to its customers. The company recognizes that such a goal is possible only \nwith the continuous and unrelenting support of a highly professionalized and disciplined \nworkforce. \nIt is for the purpose of creating and maintaining a highly professionalized and disciplined \nworkforce that this Code of Conduct (Code) is adopted. It is meant to prescribe work ethics \nand standards for the employees of the Company not only to promote higher productivity and \nenhance and safeguard the corporate image of the Company but more importantly, to create \na safer and more orderly workplace. \nWhile the Code defines the offenses as well as the corresponding penalties thereto, its overall \nintent is designed to prevent infractions rather than penalize the employees. \n \nSection 2: \nStandard of Conduct \nIn any established organization, there is a need for a system of rules, regulations, policies, \nstandards and procedures to regulate the conduct of employees based on accepted norms of \nhonesty, integrity, efficiency and morality.  In enumerating the prohibited acts and omissions, \nthis code attempts to set only the minimum acceptable standard of conduct expected of all \ncompany officers and employees. The company reserves the right to sanction such acts and \nomissions not enumerated herein but considered prejudicial to the interest of the Company \nand other employees.  \nThe provisions of this Code of Conduct are divided into the following major groups: \n \nA. Provisions on Performance of Duties \n \nEmployees are expected to do their assigned work with utmost efficiency and dedication to \nduty, and with the highest degree of excellence, professionalism and skill.  In this connection, \nall employees, except when directed otherwise, are required to report for work at the office \nand be punctual and regular in their attendance. Excessive absenteeism and tardiness shall \nconstitute gross and habitual neglect of duties.  \nIn accordance with existing laws and regulations, the company may implement changes in the \nwork schedule, including but not limited to work/account assignment, work schedule and \nworkload to carry out its business objectives.  In this regard, all employees are expected to \ncomply with company directives in the implementation of these changes.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n35 \n \nIn keeping with its mandate to continuously develop and improve its collective and individual \nperformances, the company shall evaluate the performance of the employees on a regular \nbasis.  The company shall act in accordance with the results of such evaluations. \n \nB.  Provisions on Proper Conduct and Decorum  \n \nEmployees should always conduct themselves with proper decorum within or outside the \ncompany premises in order to protect the good name of the Company as well as to merit and \nmaintain the confidence not only of Company’s clients or customers but the public in general. \nWhile no uniform is prescribed for security and identification purposes, all employees may be \nrequired to wear their company-issued identification cards at all times. \n \n \nC.  Provisions on Honesty \nIn the Company’s business, honesty is of paramount importance.  Hence, all employees are \nexpected to be honest, truthful and upright in the performance of their work, in the handling of \nCompany funds and property, and in their dealings with their fellow employees, the Company, \nCompany clients and suppliers. \nEmployees shall not engage directly or indirectly in any form of dishonesty or fraud nor conceal \nany acts nor facilitate or aid in the commission thereof. Employees are expected to always act \nprofessionally. Employees may not solicit Company clients at any time.  Employees may not \naccept any offers made directly by clients in circumvention of the client\'s agreement with the \nCompany. If an offer is made to an Employee by a client, the Employee is expected to notify \ntheir manager of such an offer immediately. \nViolation of this provision is a very serious offense and may lead to employee’s dismissal due \nto insubordination or willful disobedience of the Company’s lawful orders. \n \nD.  Provisions on use and Care of Company Property \n \n \nEmployees are expected to consider the interest of the Company and take good care of its \ngoods and properties. Employees are expected to use and handle Company property and \nequipment with care and prevent their loss and pilferage. \n \nE.  Provisions on Health Safety and Security \n \nHealth, safety and security are priorities of the Company. Employees are expected to keep \ntheir working places clean and safe. In this connection, all employees are expected to comply \nwith all housekeeping and safety rules, regulations and procedures. Employees are also \nrequired to abide by the security regulations of the Company.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n36 \n \nOccupational Health and Safety \nThe Company is committed to ensuring a safe and healthy work environment for all \nemployees. Management achieves this through the following ways: \n \n• \nProviding and maintaining equipment and systems which are safe and without risks \nto health; \n• \nSupplying the information and instruction, training and supervision necessary to \nensure the health and safety at work of all employees;   \n• \nManaging a Workplace in a safe condition, which includes maintaining safe \nentrances and exits; \n• \nEnsuring the existence of the appropriate worker’s insurance coverage; \n• \nMaintaining a safety program conforming to the highest safety practices; \n• \nEnsuring that when a question arises about productivity and safety, safety will have \nfirst priority; and \n• \nBeing responsible and accountable for the safety of each employee under its \ndirection. \nEmployees have a responsibility to take reasonable care for the health and safety of \nthemselves and others in the Workplace. Employees ought to recognize that injuries are \npreventable. Employees must take reasonable care to prevent them.  All employees are \nexpected to: \n \n• \nMaintain a safe work environment. All employees are expected to follow the \nguidelines of the Occupational Health and Safety Policy as well as comply with all \nsigns, instructions, and safety standards; \n• \nImmediately report to management all work-related illnesses and injuries, no \nmatter how slight; \n• \nAvoid interfering with or misusing equipment provided for employee’s health, safety \nor welfare; \n• \nComply with a reasonable request to assist in giving aid or preventing a risk to \nhealth and safety.  In addition, employees shall not obstruct attempts to give aid or \nattempts to prevent a serious risk to the health and safety of an employee in the \nWorkplace; \n• \nAct in a professional manner at all times; horseplay of any kind will not be tolerated; \n• \nObserve good housekeeping practices; \n• \nBe properly trained and receive authorization before operating any machines or \nequipment; \n• \nKeep all ingress, aisles, and exits clear and accessible; \n• \nReport any defects or malfunctions in machines or equipment immediately and \nwithout delay to the management; \n• \nFollow proper lifting and handling methods at all times.  When moving very heavy \nor awkward equipment or materials, employees should ask for assistance or use \npower equipment; and \n• \nBe alert to any unsafe condition that could result in injury to employees.  All unsafe \nconditions are to be reported immediately to management.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n37 \n \nThese lists are included for illustration only and are not all-inclusive.  Particularly, this list does \nnot include those safety items which are considered common sense safety issues. \n \nViolence Prevention Policy \n \nThe Company has zero tolerance for violence.  If any employee commits or displays any act \nof violence or makes threats of violence in the Workplace, the penalty of dismissal from \nemployment shall be imposed subject to the requirements specified under the Corrective \nAction Guidelines hereof.   \nThe Company defines Violence to include inflicting physical harm to another, shoving, \npushing, harassment, intimidation, coercion, brandishing weapons, and threats or talks of \nviolence.  \nThe Company is committed to providing a safe environment for employees and visitors.   As \na result, access to the Company’s premises and/or property are limited to those authorized by \nit. \n \nThe Company does not tolerate employees or visitors possessing any weapons inside the \nworkplace, including, but not limited to, weapons transported in employee vehicles.  Weapons \ninclude guns, knives, explosives, and other items that could be used to inflect or cause bodily \nharm.  \nThe Company reserves the right to enter or inspect employee work areas including, but not \nlimited to, desks, filing cabinets, employee storage lockers, and other items brought into or \nkept inside its premises. \n \nF.  Provisions on Confidentiality \n \nConfidentiality is essential in the business of the Company. It is essential for the Company to \nkeep the confidentiality of all information related to its operations and clients. All such \ninformation is therefore expected to be kept in strict confidence.    \nThe Company values and upholds the privacy and confidentiality of all information it obtains \nin order that the company may maintain a competitive position and a good standing in the \ncommunity.   \n“Confidential Information” shall mean information regarded by the Company as such, including \ninformation relating to past, present or future research, development of business affairs, and \nany proprietary products, materials or methodologies of the Company and/or any of its clients.  \nConfidential Information should not be discussed with persons outside of the organization \nunless \nit \nis \nnecessary \nfor \ndaily \noperations \nand \nwith \nprior \napproval \nfrom \nmanagement.  Likewise, information should not be shared with other employees, such as \none’s compensation and benefits package and other similar matters. Clients or officers should \nnot be allowed to look at employee files or request any type of personal information without \nsufficient reason and written authorization to do so.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n38 \n \nGeneral information concerning the company’s pricing, customers and their needs, and the \noperations of the company, shall not be discussed outside the company premises. Many \ntimes, it is necessary for us to do work of a legal or confidential nature. No information \nconcerning any legal or confidential nature may be taken or discussed outside company \npremises by employees.  \nThe copy, art, negatives, plates and files maintained by the company are confidential and such \nmaterial and/or the information they contain is not to be released to anyone without \nManagement approval. Original art, negatives and plates created by or for the company are \nthe sole property of the Company. No employee may take or make duplicate copies of these \nmaterials for use other than those sold by the Company. Rejected materials due to quality \nmust be destroyed or disposed of properly and cannot be taken or used in any manner by \ncompany personnel. In the same context, customer work is private personal property and \nextreme discretion must be utilized in displaying or showing this material even within company \npremises. Any violation of this policy may be reason for legal action and result in termination. \n \nIn addition to disciplinary action, all employees should be aware that breach of this policy may \nalso lead to legal action. For this reason, employees should be aware of the seriousness of \nthis policy. \nInquiries seeking information about current or former employees should be referred to the HR \nManager.  The Company does not provide written references regarding any of its current or \nformer employees. \n \nSection 3: \nKinds of Offenses \nOffenses are classified according to their seriousness. \n \n \nMinor Offenses refer to \n• \nSimple violations of the company’s rules or procedures which do not result in serious \nconsequences such as financial loss, administrative or operational difficulty, damage \nto Company property, corporate embarrassment or physical harm. \n• \nThose which arise from simple negligence without resulting in serious inconvenience \nto the company or any other person. \n \nMajor Offenses are those which indicate a disregard for the Company’s interests through \nrepeated violations within a month’s time (reckoned from date of first violation), of established \ncompany policies, rules and regulations and which otherwise would have been minor offenses, \nor the culpable commission or omission of an act which results in serious embarrassment or \ninconvenience to the Company or any other person. \n \nSerious & Grave Offenses refer to acts or omissions which are characterized by: \n• \nsub-par performance of the work performed which results in negative \nrepercussions from our clients\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n39 \n \n• \ndeliberate / significant and malicious intent to achieve personal gain; or \n• \ngross negligence; or \n• \nhabitual disregard for established procedures; or \n• \nfailure to notify the Company immediately of a solicitation from a client or vendor; \nor \n• \nserious undermining of authority and which involve or result in any of the following: \n \n• \nloss of money; or \n• \nbreach of trust relative to confidential and/or highly-classified information; or \n• \npublic scandal and consequent erosion of confidence due to dishonesty, habitual \ndrunkenness, immorality, etc; or \n• \nfinancial loss or administrative/operational obscurity or corporate embarrassment \nfor the Company or physical harm to its publics, officers or employees. \n• \nLoss of earnings of the Company due to intentional or negligent actions of the \nemployee. \nAny act construed as serious misconduct, willful disobedience of the lawful orders of the \nCompany or representative in connection with one’s work, gross and habitual neglect of duties, \nfraud, willful breach of the trust reposed in him by the Company, and any other analogous \ncause under all applicable sections of the Labor Code  and in reference to Section 9 – Table \nof Offenses and Penalties of this Employee Manual, of which are willful in character and grave \nin nature that it has transgressed an established and definite rule of action in relation to \nemployee’s work shall be an automatic ground for disciplinary action up to and including \ndismissal.  \nJust Causes \nArticle 297 (formerly 282) of the Philippine Labor Code indicates that the company can \nterminate an employment for any of the following just causes: \n \n• \nSerious misconduct or willful disobedience by the employee of the lawful orders of \nhis employer or representative in connection with his work \n• \nGross and habitual neglect by the employee of his duties \n• \nFraud or willful breach by the employee of the trust reposed in him by the employer \nor duly authorized representative \n• \nCommission of a crime or offense by the employee against the person of his \nemployer or any member of his family or his duly authorized representative \n• \nOther causes analogous to the foregoing. \nEmployees terminated for just cause are not entitled to separation benefits. \n \nAuthorized Causes \n \nArticle 299 (formerly 284) of the Philippine Labor Code allows the company to terminate the \nservices of any employee suffering from a disease if “…continued employment is prohibited \nby law or is prejudicial to his health as well as to the health of his co-employees…”\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n40 \n \nThe Company may likewise terminate an employee due to the following authorized causes \nfound in Article 299 (formerly 284) of the Labor Code:  \n• \nThe installation of labor-saving devices (automation) \n• \nRedundancy (superfluity in the performance of a particular work) \n• \nRedundancy to prevent losses (there is an excess of employees and employer \nwants to prevent financial losses) \n• \nThe closing or cessation of operation of the establishment or undertaking unless \nthe closing is for the purpose of circumventing the provisions of the Labor Code. \nIn addition to the thirty (30) day notice to the Department of Labor, the affected employees \nshall likewise be given a thirty (30)-day notice prior to the effectiveness of the separation from \nemployment and the payment of separation or termination pay, as may be provided under \nexisting laws. \nIn such case, the mandatory procedure in investigating cases shall be observed in accordance \nwith the rules implementing the provisions of the Labor Code as follows: \n \n• \nNotice to Explain or Show Cause \n• \nNotice of Hearing or Formal Investigation \n• \nNotice of Decision \n \nMandatory Notices \nFor termination of employment based on just causes as defined in the Labor Code and the \ngrounds enumerated in this Handbook, the following must be observed by the Company: \n \n• \nA written notice should be served on the employee specifying the ground or \ngrounds for termination, and giving to said employee a period of at least five (5) \ncalendar days within which to explain his side; \n• \nA hearing or conference during which the employee concerned, with the assistance \nof counsel if the employee so desires, may be given to provide an additional \nopportunity to respond to the charge/s, present his evidence or rebut the evidence \npresented against him in accordance to prevailing jurisprudence; and \n• \nA written notice of termination served on the employee indicating that upon due \nconsideration of all the circumstances, grounds have been established to justify his \ntermination. \nIn case of termination, the foregoing notices shall be hand delivered to the employee \nconcerned or in the event that personal delivery is not possible, such notices shall be served \nvia personal email and Zoho Sign. \n \nSection 4: Kinds of Penalties \nOffenses are penalized depending on their seriousness and attending mitigating or \naggravating circumstances. The following penalties may be imposed:\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n41 \n \nWritten Reprimand – This is imposed upon an employee who commits an offense for the first \ntime. It is an initial communication by the supervisor with the employee to make the employee \naware of the potential problem.  This is served upon the offender to warn him that he should \nbe careful in the future, otherwise, a repetition of the offense will subject him to a higher \npenalty. \n \nSuspension – This penalty is a physical detachment from service for a specific period of time \nbetween 1 and 30 days without pay depending on the gravity of the offense or violation. \nApplication and duration of Suspensions are at managers\' sole discretion.  \nManagers shall take into consideration the exigencies of the job, the client’s work processes, \nmanpower resources, among others, before such penalty may be imposed and the imposition \nof said penalty shall in no way be construed or taken as a precedence on future penalties.  \n \nDismissal – It is the termination of an employee’s services for just cause. The employee shall \nautomatically forfeit all benefits which would normally accrue to him on retirement or \nseparation for reason/s other than for just cause(s). \n \nSection 5: \nNon-Punitive Measures \n \nRestitution – Restitution for losses is not considered as punishment. In the event that the \nCompany incurs any loss due solely to the fault or negligence of an employee, the Company \nshall pursue restitution or restoration of any such losses and the employee will be expected to \nacquiesce to a fair and reasonable salary deduction and/or payment plan. \n \nPreventive Suspension – This is only an intermediate protective measure, not a penalty. An \nemployee may be placed under preventive suspension if his or her continued employment \nposes a serious and imminent threat to the property of the Company or the life or property of \nhis co-employees. The preventive suspension shall not last longer than thirty (30) days, after \nwhich, the employee is entitled to immediate reinstatement to his/her former or to a \nsubstantially equivalent position or the company may extend the period of suspension, \nprovided that, during the period of extension, the employee is paid the salaries and other \nbenefits due to him/her.  \n \nIn such a case, the employee shall not be bound to reimburse the amount paid to him/her \nduring the extension if the Company decides, after completion of the hearing, to dismiss him. \n \nSection 6: \nEffects of Penalties \n \nThe imposition of penalties shall be considered in the employee’s promotion and in their \nperformance rating, as follows:\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n42 \n \n \nPENALTY \nEFFECT \nON \nPROMOTION \nEFFECTIVITY PERIOD \nFirst \nWritten \nWarning \nno promotion \nfor 6 months from the date of the First \nWritten Warning \nFinal \nWritten \nWarning \n \nno promotion \nfor 12 months from the date of the First \nWritten Warning \nSuspension \nno promotion \nRefer to Section 10 below \nDismissal \nN/A \nPermanent \n \n \n \nNTE Rollback \n \nPENALTY \nEFFECTIVITY PERIOD \nFirst Written Warning \nfor 6 months without any Warning for the same offense \nFinal Written Warning \n \nfor 9 months without any Warning for the same offense \nSuspension \nfor 12 months without any Warning for the same offense \nDismissal \nPermanent \n \n \n \nSection 7: \nCircumstances that Affect Administrative Liability \n \nMitigating circumstances shall mean those circumstances by reason of which the penalty \ncorresponding to an offense committed by an employee is made lighter or less severe than \nwhat is usually imposed for such offense. These include past performance, degree of \nparticipation, lack of prior offenses and, in general, such circumstances which, in \nManagement’s view, justify the imposition of a penalty less severe than what is usually \nprescribed in the schedule of penalties. \n \nAggravating circumstances shall mean those circumstances by reason of which the penalty \ncorresponding to an offense committed by an employee is increased or made more severe \nthan what is usually imposed for such offense. These include the employee’s past record of\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n43 \n \noffenses, habituality in the commission of offenses, the accompanying adverse publicity stirred \nby the offense(s) which tends to erode the Company’s corporate image, and such other \ncircumstances which, in Management’s view, justify the imposition of a penalty more severe \nthan what is usually prescribed in the schedule of penalties. \n \nSection 8: Administrative Jurisdiction/Procedures \n \n \nA. Administering Officer  \n \nAll employees have the duty to report immediately any violation of company rules and \nregulations which come to their knowledge to their human resource manager. The human \nresource manager shall then bring the report to the attention of the human resource \nperson/supervisor as soon as possible.  \nThe human resource manager has the authority to investigate all offenses and penalize minor \nand less serious offenses. He has the power to impose penalties lower than dismissal after \nprior consultation with the company counsel and management. Where the offense calls for the \npenalty of dismissal the legal procedure for dismissal shall be undertaken prior to termination \nof the employee’s employment. \n \nB. Procedure \n \nAdministrative investigations and other proceedings shall be summary and expeditious in \nnature. They shall not be governed by the technical rules of procedure or by the rules on \nevidence applied to judicial proceedings. Due process shall always be observed. \n \nUpon filing the report or complaint, human resource manager may, from an examination \nthereof and such evidence as may be attached thereto, dismiss the same outright for being \npatently without basis or merit. The employee concerned and the complainant shall be duly \ninformed of such action.   \nOtherwise, the Management Panel shall immediately notify in writing the employee concerned \nof the charge(s) against him and require him to explain in writing within 120 hours (5 days) \nwhy no disciplinary action shall be taken against him.  Should the employee fail to answer the \nreport or complaint within the specified period, the employee shall be deemed to have waived \nhis/her right to be heard. \nThereafter, the human resource manager may impose the appropriate disciplinary action as \nmay be warranted by the facts alleged in the report/complaint and the answer and all available \nevidence unless he believes that a more thorough investigation or hearing is required to \nestablish the facts. \nIf a more formal hearing or investigation is necessary or the employee concerned has \nrequested such formal hearing or investigation, the employee shall be given ample opportunity\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n44 \n \nto be heard and to defend themselves with the assistance of their chosen representative or \ncounsel, at their discretion. \n \nC. Implementation of Disciplinary Action \n \nThe decision of the human resource manager shall be final unless appealed within two (2) \ndays to the highest-ranking officer of the Company present in the Philippines upon the filing of \nthe appeal. The decision of said officer shall be final. \nThe human resource manager shall implement the disciplinary action only after the decision \nshall have become final. \nDisciplinary action shall be applied consistently and uniformly regardless of rank and position. \nLike penalties shall be imposed for like offenses. The offense, together with mitigating and/or \naggravating circumstances, shall be the measure of the penalty to be imposed. The imposition \nof penalties shall be the sole prerogative of Management. \nSection 9: \nProgressive Discipline Categories \n \nThis schedule of offenses is not all inclusive and therefore not limited to the offenses \nenumerated hereunder. Thus, in addition to the specific infractions as provided below, the \nCompany is not precluded from imposing appropriate sanctions/penalties for acts or omissions \nconstituting violations of existing policies, procedures, and rules and regulations and those \noffenses constituting acts or omissions punishable by the Revised Penal Code and other laws \nof the Philippines. \n \nThe company reserves the right to amend, change, add or alter the content of the Employee’s \nHandbook from time to time as the circumstances dictate and when particular predicaments \ncall for such amendments or supplementation of the Handbook.   \nAll amendments, changes, alterations, additions or otherwise shall be furnished to all \nemployees through written notice whether it be in the form of an addendum, memorandum or \nother written form to which acknowledgment of its receipt shall be made by the respective \nemployees in writing. \nThe following Progressive Discipline shall be observed as follows: \n \nMinor offenses \n \nFirst offense  \n= \nWritten Warning \nSecond offense \n= \nSuspension \nThird offense  \n= \nDismissal\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n45 \n \nOR  \n \nFirst offense  \n= \nWritten Warning \nSecond offense \n= \nFinal Written Warning \nThird offense  \n= \nSuspension \nFourth offense \n= \nDismissal \n \n \nAt management\'s sole discretion, the offense of Dismissal may be substituted with a 1–30-\nday suspension. \n \n \nMajor offenses \n \nFirst offense  \n= \nWritten Warning \nSecond offense \n= \nDismissal \n \nAt management\'s sole discretion, the offense of Dismissal may be substituted with a 1–30-\nday suspension. \n \n \nOR \n \n \nFirst offense  \n= \nDismissal \n \n \nAt management\'s sole discretion, the offense of Dismissal may be substituted with a 1–30-\nday suspension.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n46 \n \n \nSection 10: Table of Offenses and Penalties in Any Given 30-Day \nPeriod \n \nA. \nProvisions on Performance of Duties \n1st \nOffense \n2nd \nOffense \n3rd \nOffense \n4th  \nOffense \n1. \nAbuse \nof \nauthority \n/responsibility which results in \nfinancial losses and/or court \nsuits and civil liabilities to the \nCompany and/or Abuse of \nauthority \nincluding \nbribery, \ncorruption and/or kickbacks. \nDismissal \n \n \n2. \nInfraction of Company \nrules/ procedures in handling \nany Company transaction or \nwork assignment which results \nin a loss. \n Written \nWarning \nFinal \nWritten \nWarning \nSuspension \n \nDismissal \n3. \nSignificant \ndelay \nin \nrendering \nservice \nto \nclients/customers. \nWritten \nWarning \nFinal \nWritten \nWarning \nSuspension Dismissal \n4. Significant slowdown or \nprotraction \nof \nwork \nwhich \ndirectly \naffects \none’s \nproductivity or that of others. \nWritten \nWarning \nFinal \nWritten \nWarning \nSuspension Dismissal \n5. \nMalingering \nor \nfeigning \nillness to avoid assigned work. \nWritten \nWarning \nSuspension \nDismissal \n6. Negligence or dereliction of \nduty \nwhether \nor \nnot \nthe \ncompany suffers a loss. \nWritten \nWarning \nSuspension \nDismissal \n7. Failing to meet performance \nmetrics and minimum goals. \