smissal \n \n \n16. \nUnauthorized \nacquisition \nof \nany \ncorrespondence, confidential documents pertaining \nto employment, client’s business transactions or \nother papers or files whether in digital or written \nform. The employee introducing such a document, \ncorrespondence or paper to another individual, \nentity, agency, organization or company shall be \ndeemed as the author of such unauthorized \nacquisition. \nDismissal \n \n \n17. Sabotage or deliberate acts which cause or \nresult in damage or loss to Company property, \nreputation, equipment or products. \nDismissal \n \n \n18. Failure to immediately notify the Company of an \noffer or solicitation, or acceptance of an offer or \nsolicitation, from a client or vendor to provide the \nsame or similar services which the client or vendor \nhas contracted with the Company to perform in \norder to circumvent such contract with the \nCompany. \nDismissal \n \n \n19. Willful nondisclosure of material events or \nincidents relating to employees during company \nsanctioned events or within company premises. \nDismissal\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n57 \n \n \nD. Provisions on Use and Care of Company Property \n \n1st \nOffense \n2nd \nOffense \n3rd \nOffense \n1. Significant destruction of or causing \ndamage to the Company property or \nrecords. \n \nDismissal \n \n \n2. \nSignificant \nmisuse \nor \nremoval \nor \nunauthorized use of the Company property or \noperation of company vehicles, machines or \nequipment without authority. \nDismissal \n \n \n3. \nImproper use of machines or equipment \nwhich leads to the damage or potential damage of \nsaid property. \nSuspension \nDismissal \n \n4. \nDefacement, removal or placement of \nnotices, signs, or writing in any form on the \nCompany premises at any time without authority \nor any form of vandalism within the Company \npremises. \nWritten \nWarning \nSuspension Dismissal \n5. \nRequisition and use of Company supplies \nfor personal purposes. \nWritten \nWarning \nSuspension Dismissal \n6. Failure to place in order and safety at the \nclose of the day’s work, office equipment \nand/or machines and the Company \nrecords. \nWritten \nWarning \nSuspension Dismissal \n7. \nUsing company property, equipment or \nmaterials for personal or private advantage, \nbenefit or purpose, without duly written permission \nor authority from the management.  \nWritten \nWarning \nSuspension Dismissal \n8. \nUnauthorized \nlending \nof \ncompany \nproperty. \nWritten \nWarning \nSuspension Dismissal \n9. \nFailure of an operator/user to immediately \nreport any damage to or defect of company \nmachine or equipment, the further use of which \nrequired specialized training or skills. \n \nWritten \nWarning \nSuspension Dismissal \n10. \nOperating or tinkering by any employee, \nwith the company machine or equipment without \nprior notice, the safe operation of which requires \nspecialized training or skills or management \nauthorization. \nSuspension \nDismissal\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n58 \n \n11. \nWithout prior authority, allowing another \nperson to operate or tinker with any company \nmachine or specialized tools and equipment. \nSuspension \nDismissal \n \n12. \nImproper, incorrect and careless use of \ncompany machines, tools and equipment, causing \ndamaged/defect on the machine. \nSuspension \nDismissal \n \n13. \nViolation of the company promulgated \nsafety rules, regulations and general practices. \nSuspension \nDismissal \n \n14. \nBringing out or taking company properties \nor attempting to steal any company’s properties \nsuch as but not limited to PCs/laptops, software, \naudio & video equipment, company records, \ndocuments, manuals, office supplies, among \nother company equipment and properties.  \nDismissal \n \n \n15. Loss, destruction or breakage of any \nCompany property, whether intentional or through \nnegligence, where the value of the property is \nP5,000.00 and above.   \nSuspension \nDismissal\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n59 \n \n \nE. Provisions on Health, Safety and Security \n \n1st \nOffense \n2nd \nOffense \n3rd \nOffense \n1. Failure/refusal to abide by security, \nhealth and safety regulations in the \nperformance of duties or in the use \nof the Company facilities. \nWritten Warning \nSuspension Dismissal \n2. Non-observance of signs in \nrestricted areas. \nWritten Warning \nSuspension Dismissal \n3. Non-observance \nof \nprohibition \nagainst \nsmoking \nwithin \nthe \nCompany’s premises  \n \nWritten Warning \nAnd/or \nFine \nequivalent \nto \namount suffered \nby Company as a \nresult \nof \nsuch \nviolation \nSuspension Dismissal \n4. Unauthorized physical access to the \nCompany’s Data Center, computer \ninstallations, and similarly restricted \nareas. \nDismissal \n \n \n5. Unauthorized logical access to the \nCompany’s \ncomputer \nsystem, \nutilities, applications and database. \nDismissal \n \n \n6. Failure to comply with the prescribed \ndata security control procedures and \npractices \nDismissal \n \n \n7. Unauthorized deletion of computer \nfiles \nDismissal \n \n \n8. Intentional destruction of computer \nresources (hardware) \nDismissal \n \n \n9. Failure to implement prescribed \ncomputer policy/ procedures (job \nruns, \nback-up, \nrecovery, \npassword changes, etc.) \nWritten Warning \nSuspension Dismissal \n10. Use/possession \nof \nprohibited \ndrugs within or outside the \nCompany \npremises \nand/ \nor \ncompany sanctioned activities \n \nDismissal\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n60 \n \n11. Possession of firearms and/or \ndeadly \nweapons \nwithin \nthe \nCompany \npremises \nwithout \nproper authorization from the \ncompany. \nDismissal \n \n \n12. Conviction of a criminal offense \n(while \nin \nthe \ncourse \nof \nemployment) \nDismissal \n \n \n13. Assaulting/inflicting \ninjury \non \nanother within the Company \npremises or company sanctioned \nactivities \nDismissal \n \n \n14. Participating in disorders/illegal \nactivities within the Company \npremises or during company \nsanctioned activities. \nDismissal \n \n \n15. Drunkenness or any alcoholic \nbeverages within the Company \npremises \nduring \nor \nbeyond \nworking hours and company \nsanctioned events except on \ncertain \noccasions \nwhen \npermitted by the President \nDismissal \n \n \n16. Reporting for work while under the \ninfluence of drugs or alcohol. \nDismissal \n \n \n17. Lending or giving one’s company ID \ncard for the use/benefit of an \nunauthorized person to the prejudice \nof the company. \nDismissal \n \n \n18. Assisting/allowing \nany \nnon-\nemployee \nto \nenter \ncompany \npremises specifically restricted areas \nwithout authorization. \nSuspension  \nDismissal \n \n19. Knowingly harboring a contagious \ndisease which may endanger the \nhealth of fellow employees, without \nnotifying management. \nSuspension \nDismissal \n \n20. \nOther \nacts \nanalogous \nto \nthe \nforegoing, as may be determined by \nManagement in the exercise of sound \ndiscretion. \nSuspension \nDismissal\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n61 \n \n \nF. Miscellaneous Provisions \n \n1st  \nOffense \n2nd \nOffense \n3rd \nOffense \n1. Violation of confidentiality of the Company \nrecords. \n \nDismissal  \n \n2. Disclosure of computer security code (password) \non confidential Company matters to unauthorized \npersons/personnel with or without loss to the \nCompany \nDismissal  \n \n3. Disclosure \nof \nCompany \ndocuments \nto \nunauthorized persons/personnel with or without \nloss to the Company \nDismissal  \n \n4. Unauthorized use of the on-line system and other \ntelecommunication facilities of the Company to \nbroadcast/transmit unauthorized messages \n \nDismissal  \n \n5. \nUnauthorized disclosure of passwords/security \ncodes to access the Company’s computer system, \nutilities, applications and database. \n \nDismissal\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n62 \n \n \nPart 8: Mandatory Workplace Policies \n \nSection 1: Drug-Free Workplace Policy \n \nIntroduction \n \nDrug use and abuse represents a threat to the well-being of every employee and could cause \ndamage to the company’s image and puts it in high exposure to risks and liability. \nEvery employee is expected to come to work free from the adverse effects of drugs. \nECEnterprises Group Manila, Inc. will not tolerate any involvement with drugs which adversely \naffect the workplace and the work environment or put at risk the safety of employees, the \npublic, equipment owned by the company or the company’s good standing in the community. \n \nUnder the guidelines of Department of Labor and Employment Order no. 53-03, in support of \nRepublic Act No. 9165 (Dangerous Drugs Act of 2002), we are instituting this Drug Free \nWorkplace Policy. \n \nPolicy \n \nECEnterprises Group Manila, Inc. prohibits any employee from using, possessing, distributing, \npurchasing or selling of illegal drugs or narcotics, inhalants, or any unlawful substances within \nor outside the company premises, whether or not they are on duty. \nOn top of corrective action, the company is obliged to turn over all materials including drug \nsubstances, paraphernalia and records to proper government agencies for filing of appropriate \ncharges pursuant to R.A. 9165. \nAnyone caught using illegal substances in company premises shall be terminated. Any \nemployee found under the influence of drugs for the first time, per confirmatory test result, \nshall not be permitted to work until such time that he submits himself to and recovers from \ntreatment, counseling or rehabilitation. Second time offenders shall be dismissed. \nAny employee showing just cause for suspicion to be under the influence of drugs shall be \nimmediately made to undergo drug screening. Pertinent corrective actions are outlined under \nthe guidelines of this policy. \nThe policy applies to all ranks of employees of ECEnterprises Group Manila, Inc.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n63 \n \nDefinitions \n \nIllegal Drugs – drugs that are classified as illegal under Philippines Laws, specifically R.A. \n9165, examples of which are listed, but not limited to, below: \n• \nMarijuana \n• \nShabu or methamphetamine hydrochloride \n• \nEcstasy pills \n• \nHallucinogens \n• \nDepressants or stimulants not prescribed by physician \n \nPrescribed Drugs – Drugs that are prescribed or administered by licensed physicians to \npatients \n \nRandom Drug Test – refers to the unannounced schedule of testing with each employee \nhaving an equal chance of being selected for testing. The policy on the conduct of random \ndrug tests should be known to both employers and employees. \n \nScreening Test – a rapid test performed by a Drug Testing Center for purposes of establishing \npotential or presumptive positive results and the type of drug used. \n \nConfirmatory Test – is an analytical test to validate and confirm the potential or presumptive \npositive result of the Screening Test using a Gas Chromatography Mass Spectrometry \n(GCMS) machine. \n \nClass A Drug Testing Center – a DOH-accredited drug testing center that has the capability \nto collect samples, do drug screening and confirmatory testing. \n \nAssessment Team – the safety team who shall assess and coordinate with the proper \ngovernment agency for needed actions on any person under the influence of drugs. \n \nGuidelines \n1. Orientation \nECEnterprises Group Manila, Inc. shall conduct an orientation on the Drug Free Workplace \nPolicy, explaining its salient features, the legislative background, procedure and compliance \nrequirements. \n \n2. Drug Screening \na. Random Drug Test\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n64 \n \n• \nAn unannounced drug screening test shall be conducted at schedules to be \ndetermined by management. \n• \nThe tests shall be conducted by a Department of Health accredited Class A drug \nscreening center or laboratory, which shall also take charge of gathering \nspecimens from the employees. \n• \nThe collection of samples shall be done within company premises to ensure that \nemployees are not exposed to hazards or any inconvenience beyond what is \nreasonably needed to complete the process. \n• \nDrug testing includes Screening and Confirmatory tests. Confirmatory test is only \napplied when an employee turns positive in the screening. \n• \nSelection of employees for the screening shall be done randomly. All employees, \nincluding officers and managers, selected to undergo drug screening must submit \nthemselves to such a test. \n• \nFailure or refusal to do so shall be construed as willful disobedience and shall be \njust cause for dismissal, as provided in Art. 282 of the Labor Code. \n• \nOnly expenses from the drug screening and confirmatory tests shall be borne by \nthe company. \n \n3. Right of the Employee and Employees Found Positive \n \n• \nThe employee shall have the right to know the result of his drug screening. \n• \nThe company shall authorize the immediate conduct of a confirmatory test, should any \nemployee’s specimen be found positive with drugs in the first drug screening. \n• \nAn employee has the right to contest the result of his confirmatory test within 15 days \nafter receipt of the result. \nEmployees found under the influence of drugs, as ascertained by a confirmatory drug \ntest shall be dismissed from employment. \n• \nThe assessment team shall refer the offender to the Dangerous Drugs Board (DDB) \nor the local Anti-Drug Abuse Council or to a rehabilitation center for determining the \nspecific course of action.  \n• \nDepending on attendant circumstances, the assessment team may summon the police \nfor any incident related to drug use. \n• \nAny expenses to be incurred after the screening and confirmatory tests as a result of \nrecommended action on an employee found to be under the influence of drugs shall \nbe shouldered by the employee. \n• \nAbsences incurred during treatment shall be charged to SIL. Any absences beyond \nSIL credits shall be counted as unpaid leave. \n \n4. Use of Prescribed Drugs \n \nPrescribed drugs must be administered by a competent medical doctor. Any employee \nundergoing prescribed medication must come forward and inform HRD of such. He must no \nlonger wait to be questioned. Rather, if he is taking prescribed medication, that may be \nconsidered a controlled substance, he is duty-bound to report and present pertinent\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n65 \n \ndocuments as proof, such as, but not limited to, a valid medical certificate and a physician-\nissued prescription. He must also obtain fit-to-work clearance from the company physician. \n \n5. Pre-Employment Drug Testing \nAn applicant must undergo drug screening within 24 hours upon the advice of the recruiter \nand submit the result within the same day of taking the drug screening. Drug screening of \napplicants shall be done at the drug test center selected by ECEnterprises Group Manila, Inc. \nShould the applicant fail to comply, this will be construed as his willful foregoing of his \napplication. \nThe company has the right to turn down an applicant who tests positive in the screening test. \nShould he appeal, he may submit to confirmatory testing in a DOH-accredited drug testing \ncenter at his cost. \n \n6. Formation of an Assessment Team \nAn assessment team for the purpose of this policy shall be composed of: \n• \nCorporate Employee Relations \n• \nBusiness Unit HR \n• \nCompany Retainer Physician or Nurse or any accredited physician \n• \nSecurity Manager \n• \nManager of the Program/Department of the Offender \n \nFunctions \n• \nThe committee shall determine the details of the drug screenings. \n• \nIt shall refer to the appropriate government agency(ies) for proper action of all \nemployees who have tested positive on the drug testing. \n• \nThe committee shall also recommend the mandatory drug testing of any employee \nunder suspicion of drugs for any or all reasons specified in 2.B of this policy. \n• \nIt recommends if an employee is fit to work after rehabilitation. \n \n7. \nConfidentiality of Results \nAll documents connected with the drug testing performed shall be part of the employee’s \nmedical record and shall be treated as confidential. \n \n \n8. \nRight to Inspect, Search or Examine \nThe company reserves the right to inspect, search or examine all persons entering the \ncompany’s premises for possible possession of illegal drugs. Any person who refuses to \nsubmit to such search or inspection shall be denied access to the premises.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n66 \n \nIf an employee refuses to be inspected, searched or examined, he shall be administratively \ndealt with. \nLockers, drawers and cabinets, though assigned to employees, are still deemed company \nproperty and may be opened upon order, provided it is done in the presence of the person \ninvolved. In case a locker or drawer is shared by more than one person, and an illegal \nsubstance or drug paraphernalia is found inside such locker or drawer, and ownership of such \ncannot be safely determined, both or all employees to whom the locker or drawer is assigned \nshall be made to go through suspicion drug screening. \n \n \n9. \nSummary of Penalties and Offenses \n \nOFFENSE \n1st OFFENSE \n2nd \nOFFENSE \n3rd \nOFFENSE \n1. Possession of illegal drugs or drug \nparaphernalia (If found within company \npremises, \nimmediate \nsuperior \nshall \nconfiscate in front of a witness.) \nDismissal \nand \nturnover to proper \ngovernment \nagency \n \n \n2. Sale or distribution of illegal drugs \nDismissal \nand \nturnover to proper \ngovernment \nagency \n3. Buying or accepting illegal drugs \nDismissal \nand \nturnover to proper \ngovernment \nagency \n4. Unjustified failure or refusal to submit to \nrandom drug testing \nDismissal \n5. Failure or refusal to submit to suspicion \ndrug testing, after the recommendation of \nthe assessment team, based on probable \ncause of illegal drug use \nDismissal \n6. \nReporting \nto \nwork \nwhile \nunder \nmedication or therapeutic drug which \naffects work abilities without clearance \nDismissal\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n67 \n \nfrom the Company. (Employees shall be \nmade to present proof of prescription). \n \n \n \n \n \n7. Reporting for duty while under the \ninfluence of illegal drugs. (Employees shall \nbe \nmade \nto \nundergo \ndrug \ntesting \nimmediately.) \nDismissal \n \n \n \n \n \n8. Proven drug dependency \nDismissal \n \n  \n \n  \n \n  \n \n  \n \n9. Conviction for a crime involving illegal \ndrugs \nDismissal \n \n  \n  \n \n \nSection 2: Workplace Policy on Mental Health \n \nIn conformity with Republic Act No. 11036 otherwise known as the Mental Health Act of 2018 \nwhich affirms the basic right of all Filipinos to mental health as well as the fundamental rights \nof people who require mental health services, this policy is issued for the information and \nguidance of all employees. \nThe purpose of this policy is to establish, promote and maintain the mental health and well-\nbeing of all employees through workplace practices and programs, and encourage employees \nto take responsibility for their own mental health and well-being. \n \nThe Company aims to: \n• \nBuild and maintain a workplace environment and culture that supports mental health \nand well-being and prevents discrimination (including bullying and harassment). \n• \nIncrease employee knowledge and awareness of mental health and well-being issues \nand behaviors.  \n• \nCorrect stigma and discrimination associated with mental health conditions in the \nworkplace. \n \nIn order to do this, the Company shall:\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n68 \n \n• \nTogether with employees, representatives from the Clinic shall develop, implement, \nmonitor and evaluate the workplace policy and program for mental health and well-\nbeing. \n• \nProvide information regarding mental health and well-being. \n• \nFacilitate access of employees with mental health conditions to treatment and \npsychosocial support. \n• \nEnsure non-discriminatory practices in the workplace. \n• \nEnsure confidentiality of the health status and medical records of employees. \n \nIt is also expected that employees support and contribute to the Company’s aim of providing \na mentally healthy and supportive environment for all employees by: \n• \nUnderstanding this policy  \n• \nConsidering this policy while completing work-related duties and at any time while \nrepresenting the Company \n• \nTaking reasonable care of their own mental health and wellbeing, including physical \nhealth \n• \nTaking reasonable care that their actions do not affect the health and safety of other \npeople in the workplace. \n \nThis policy applies to all employees, including contractors, and shall take effect upon signing \nthe Company Handbook. \nNothing in this policy should be construed to limit the rights and prerogatives of the Company \nunder its Handbook, and other relevant policies as well as the provisions of the Labor Code, \nas amended, including its implementing rules and regulations, and pertinent social \nlegislations.  \n \nSection 3: Workplace Policy on Safe Spaces  \n \nThe Company values the dignity of every human person and guarantees full respect for human \nrights. It likewise recognizes the role of women in nation-building and ensures the fundamental \nequality before the law of women and men. The Company assures, through its policies and \nimplementation that both men and women must have equality, security and safety in the \nworkplace. \nTo this end, the Company has the duty to prevent, deter, or punish the performance of acts of \ngender-based sexual harassment in the workplace through the following means:  \n \n• \nDisseminate or post in a conspicuous place a copy of RA 11313 or the Safe Spaces \nAct to all people in the workplace. \n• \nProvide measures to prevent gender-based sexual harassment in the workplace, such \nas the conduct of anti-sexual harassment seminars. \n• \nProvide and disseminate, in consultation with all people in the workplace, a code of \nconduct or workplace policy which shall:\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n69 \n \no Expressly reiterate the prohibition on gender-based sexual harassment. \no Describe the procedures of the internal mechanism created under Section \n17(c) of the Safe Spaces Act or RA 11313; and \no Set administrative penalties. \n \nCommittee on Decorum and Investigation \n \nThis committee is created to investigate and address complaints of gender-based sexual \nharassment. It shall be composed of the following members:  \n• \nProgram Managers \n• \nHR Officers \n• \nSafety and Health Officers \n• \nOperations Managers \n• \nAssistant Managers \n• \nSTLs \n• \nTLs \n• \nTenured agents \n \nThe committee must be headed by a woman and not less than half of its members should be \nwomen. Its members should be impartial and not connected or related to the alleged \nperpetrator. \nThe committee is duty-bound to investigate and decide on the complaints within ten (10) days \nor less upon receipt thereof. Further, the committee must observe due process, protect the \ncomplainant from retaliation and guarantee confidentiality to the greatest extent possible.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n70 \n \n \nPart 9: Ethics and Decorum \n \nSection 1: Conflicts of Interest \n \nAs an employee of ECE, you are expected to act in the best interests of the company and \nmaintain the highest standards of integrity. To prevent any potential conflicts of interest, if your \njob at ECE includes purchasing, procurement, administration, management and/or any other \nposition that requires you to spend company funds for any reason, we prohibit employees from \nmaking personal purchases of goods or services from any entity, individual, or company with \nwhich they have a personal interest or relationship. Specifically, you acknowledge that you \nunderstand the following company rules, and agree to follow them: \n \n \n1. Vendor Relationships: You shall not purchase goods or services from any vendor, \nsupplier, or contractor if you have a direct or indirect personal interest in that entity \n(e.g., ownership, family relationship, close friendship). \n2. Outside Business Interests: If you are involved in any business or financial \nrelationship with an external entity, you must disclose it promptly to your supervisor or \nthe HR department. Such relationships may include ownership, consulting, or \nemployment. \n3. Gifts and Benefits: Accepting gifts, discounts, or other benefits from vendors or \nbusiness partners that could influence your purchasing decisions is strictly prohibited. \n4. Transparency: If you become aware of any potential conflict of interest, you must \nimmediately disclose it to your supervisor or HR. Failure to do so may result in \ndisciplinary action.\" \n \nSection 2: Strict Prohibition on accepting Kickbacks or Bribes \n \nProhibition of Bribes and Kickbacks:  \n \nECE upholds the principles of honesty, integrity, and transparency in conducting its business \nand taking care of its clients. In line with this commitment, ECE strictly prohibits any form of \nbribery and corruption while performing ECE duties, as well as in dealing with its clients, \nbusiness partners, service providers, and governmental agencies and instrumentalities. \n  \nEmployees have the obligation to ensure that any interaction with public officials, clients, \nbusiness partners, and service providers complies with all relevant laws and regulations, as \nwell as with this policy.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n71 \n \n Corruption Prevention \n  \nThis policy is subject to such rules as ECE may prescribe gifts of minimal value such as \npromotional items and minor company collaterals as may be customary in the ordinary course \nof business and as appropriate to the occasion. Nevertheless, employees must exercise \ncaution regarding the giving or receiving of business-related gifts or hospitality/entertainment. \n  \nThe following shall be considered as prohibited acts of improper exchange for compensation \nand corruption: \n  \n• \nDirectly or indirectly offering, giving, requesting, or receiving money, gifts, favors, \nbenefits or anything of value for the purpose of influencing decisions or gaining \nunfair advantage in company transactions. \n• \nUsing or performing official functions for personal gain; \n• \nPerforming or neglecting to perform any official function in exchange for receiving \nmoney, gifts, favors, benefits or anything of value in the course of business or; \n• \nAny actions that are unlawful or illegal in exchange for receiving money, gifts, \nfavors, benefits or anything of value. \n  \nAny act, attempt or allegation of bribery, improper exchange for compensation, and/or \nkickbacks shall be treated as grave offenses that will be handled with dispatch and dealt with \nthe full force of the applicable laws, rules or regulations. \n In handling any case alleging bribery, kickbacks, or corruption, ECE shall be guided by its \nCode of Conduct herein, and the then-applicable laws of the Philippines pertaining to the said \nsubject. \n  \nMisuse of Company Funds: \n \nThe use of ECE funds for personal gain, or any unauthorized purpose, is strictly prohibited. \nAny misuse of company funds is a serious violation of both our ethical standards and legal \nobligations. \n \nReporting Procedures: \n \nEmployees are responsible for reporting any incident regarding an offer of a bribe, kickback \nor any similar act from the moment of its occurrence. Employees must report such an incident \nto any of the following:\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n72 \n \n• \nYour Project Manager (PM) \n• \nA representative from Human Resources Officers (HRD) \n• \nA Senior Manager \n \nConfidentiality will be maintained to the fullest extent, and no retaliatory action will be taken \nagainst employees who report such incidents in good faith. \n \nConsequences: \nViolations of this policy are grounds for immediate termination of employment. In addition, \nindividuals involved may face criminal prosecution under applicable laws. ECE will take all \nnecessary measures to protect its integrity as its operations are paramount. \n \n \nSection 3: Guidelines on Personal Relationships within the Company \n \nAt ECEnterprises Group Manila, Inc., we value a harmonious and productive work \nenvironment. We understand that personal relationships may develop between colleagues. \nWhile we do not wish to interfere with personal matters, it is essential to maintain \nprofessionalism and ensure that such relationships do not affect the workplace dynamics. \n \nDisclosure: \nEmployees with existing or future relationship/s either by consanguinity or affinity with co-\nemployees of competing companies are expected to disclose such relationship/s to the \nManagement. Said disclosure is confidential and will aid the management of any potential \nconflicts of interest. If management perceives a conflict or potential conflict of interest, every \neffort shall be made, together by management and the employee, to arrive at a solution within \nsix (6) months, either by transfer to another department, without diminution in salary and \nbenefits, or by career preparation toward outside employment. \n \nProfessionalism: \n \nEmployees are expected to always maintain a professional demeanor while at work. \n \nPersonal relationships should not interfere with work performance, decision-making, or create \nan uncomfortable environment for other colleagues.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n73 \n \n \nConflicts of Interest: \n \nEmployees should avoid any activity, investment relationship, or interest that may run counter \nto the responsibilities which they owe to the Company. To this, employees are expected: \n• \nTo avoid having personal or family interest, financial or otherwise, in any competitor \nsupplier or other businesses which may consciously or unconsciously influence their \nactions or decisions and thus deprive the Company of legitimate profit. \n• \nTo refrain from using their position in the Company or knowledge of Company plans to \nadvance their outside personal interests, that of their relatives, friends and other \nbusinesses. \n• \nTo avoid outside employment or other interests for income which would impair their \neffective job performance. \n• \nTo consult with Management on such activities or relationships that may lead to conflict \nof interest. \n \nBehavior in the Workplace: \n \nPublic displays of affection are not appropriate in the workplace and should be avoided. \nAny behavior that disrupts the work environment or affects other employees\' performance is \nnot acceptable. \n \nConfidentiality: \n \nEmployees must respect the confidentiality of their colleagues\' personal information. \nGossip or spreading information about personal relationships is not tolerated and will be \nsubject to disciplinary action. \n \nAccount-Specific Guidelines: \nEmployees within the same account should be particularly mindful of maintaining \nprofessionalism to avoid any potential conflicts or perceptions of favoritism. \nPlease also refer to the Conflict-of-Interest clause. \n \nReporting Concerns: \nIf any employee feels uncomfortable or believes that a personal relationship is affecting the \nworkplace, they are encouraged to report their concerns to the Human Resources \nDepartment. All reports will be handled confidentially and investigated promptly.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n74 \n \n \n \nSection 4: Data Privacy  \nThe Company maintains certain personal information about employees as part of its general \nemployee records. This may include, but is not limited to, your: \n• \nAddress and contact details \n• \nMarital status \n• \nExpertise and qualifications \n• \nSalary and benefits information \n• \nBank account details \n• \nPerformance appraisals and salary reviews \n• \nRecords of vacation, other leaves, and working hours \n• \nOther management records \n \nThis information is obtained directly from you, from third parties, or through the course of your \nemployment relationship with the Company. It may be stored and retained in various formats, \nincluding written, electronic, verbal, or other forms. \n \nThe Company uses this information for personal administration, employee management, and \ngeneral business purposes, such as: \n \n• \nAdministering payroll and benefits, including leave entitlements \n• \nManaging employee work and performance, including performance reviews and salary \nadjustments \n• \nOperating the Company’s IT and communication systems \n• \nEnsuring compliance with legal and record-keeping obligations \n \nThe Company may also process health-related information, including sensitive personal data, \nsuch as records of sickness absences, medical certificates, and medical reports provided by \nemployees. This information is used to: \n• \nAdminister health and sickness benefits \n• \nMonitor and manage sickness absences \n• \nFulfill legal obligations related to employee health and safety \n \nPersonal information is stored securely within the Company’s premises. Some data is stored \nelectronically in databases controlled by administrative staff with security measures to ensure\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n75 \n \nconfidentiality. These measures are reviewed regularly and upgraded as necessary to keep \npace with technological developments. Physical records, such as personnel files, are securely \nmaintained by the HR Department. \nEmployees are encouraged to review and update their personal information regularly. The \nCompany will periodically request updates, but you may also initiate updates at any time by \ncontacting HR. For more details about data privacy policies or to request a copy of your \npersonal information, subject to legal exceptions, please contact HR. \n \nWorkplace Privacy \nTo maintain a respectable and secure workplace, employees are reminded that any form of \nrecording—audio, video, or otherwise—within the workplace that involves other employees or \nCompany activities requires explicit consent from all individuals involved. Unauthorized \nrecordings are strictly prohibited and may result in disciplinary action. \nExplicit consent means that all parties involved must be informed about the purpose of the \nrecording and provide their agreement to be recorded. This includes but is not limited to: \n• \nMeetings \n• \nConversations or interviews \n• \nWorkplace interactions or events \n \nData Privacy Consent \n \nThe Company maintains certain personal information about you as part of our general \nemployee records.  The Company’s records may include your address and contact details, \nmarital status, and expertise, details of salary and benefits, bank details, performance \nappraisals and salary reviews, records relating to vacation and other leaves, working time \nrecords and other management records.  The Company has become aware of this information \nin several ways – directly from you, from others and otherwise over time through our \nrelationship with you – and may receive and/or retain it in various forms (whether in writing, \nelectronically, verbally or otherwise). \n \nThe Company uses this information for a variety of personal administration and employee \nwork and general business management purposes. For example, it needs this information to \nadminister payroll, improve and maintain the administration of employee benefits (such as \nleave entitlement, facilitating the management of work and employees, operate performance \nand salary reviews, operate the Firm’s IT and communications systems, comply with record \nkeeping and other legal obligations. \n \nThe Company also processes information relating to your health which may amount to \nsensitive personal data.  The information that the Company holds relating to your health is the \nrecords of sickness absence and medical certificates (including any medical reports that \nemployees have provided).  The purpose of keeping this sort of information is to administer\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n76 \n \nhealth and sickness benefits, monitor and manage sickness absence and comply with \nobligations under the law. \n \nSome of this personal information is stored in a database located at the Company’s premises. \nThe database is controlled by our administrative staff and can be accessed electronically. The \nCompany m has security measures in place which will ensure the confidentiality of the \ninformation contained in the database and these measures will be reviewed over time and \nupgraded in line with technological developments.  The remainder of the personal information \nis held on Personnel files which are kept by HR. \n \nFrom time to time, the Company will ask you to review and update the personal information it \nholds about you (although you are welcome to review and update this information more or \nless frequently, as you wish).  Please contact HR at any time for more information about the \ndata privacy act and to obtain an up-to-date copy of the information held about you, subject to \nthe various exceptions allowed by law. \n \nThe Company reserves the right to conduct authorized recordings for legitimate business \npurposes, such as training, security, or compliance. In such cases, affected employees will be \nduly informed and their consent sought, except were permitted by law.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n77 \n \n \nPart 10: Revisions \n \n1. Any revision of in whole or in part of this handbook shall become effective upon receipt \nand written acknowledgment by the employee of such revision. \n \n2. This handbook is considered supplementary to the Labor Code and all existing Labor \nLaws. Should there be differences, the Labor Code and all existing Labor Laws shall \nprevail.  \n \n3. The Company also reserves its right to modify, amend, and/or revise this Handbook \nas may be warranted by the circumstances, and/or to conform with the laws and \npolicies that may hereafter be adopted or implemented by the Company or the \ngovernment agencies. \n \nDate of Effectivity, Miscellaneous and Transitory Provisions \n \n \n1. Notwithstanding the above-mentioned penalties for the corresponding infractions and \noffenses, the Company reserves the right to institute any applicable civil or criminal \nactions to erring employees. \n2. Notwithstanding the compliance of all the disciplinary rules of the Company, an \nemployee who shall furnish false, fraudulent or falsified documents or information on \nhis health, state of well-being, education, professional capacity, competence in work \nor other material information and documents supplied by the employee upon the \nlatter’s application to the Company shall be met with the penalty of termination and/or \nany other applicable civil of criminal action.  \n3. Termination proceedings will be initiated by the Company pursuant to the provisions \nof the Labor Code on termination of employees for just causes. \n4. Any employee given three (3) suspensions on any violations of the handbook within \nsix (6) months may be terminated from employment. \n5. The Code of Conduct, Rules and Regulations and Disciplinary Rules or the client/s of \nthe Company being served by its employees shall form part of the foregoing Rules and \nRegulations and shall be imposed on its employees together with its penalties.  \n6. A copy of this code shall be furnished to all employees of ECEnterprises Group Manila \nInc. \n \nEFFECTIVE AS OF: March 1, 2025\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA, INC. \nC&P Noel Bldg., Dr. V. Locsin St., \nDumaguete City, 6200, Negros Oriental \n \nwww.ececonsultinggroup.com \n \n \n78 \n \n \nACKNOWLEDGMENT RECEIPT \n \nThis is to certify that I have received a copy of the Employee Handbook of ECEnterprises \nGroup Manila, Inc. (the “Company”) and that I have completely read and fully understood the \nterms and provisions thereof. \n \nI understand all of the rules, policies, terms and conditions, and agree to abide by them, \nrealizing that failure to do so may result in corrective action, including termination of \nemployment. \n \nI understand that the Company will monitor my computer files, internet activity, email \nmessages and voicemail messages for various reasons which are not limited to the provision \ncontained in the Policies. The Company will disclose such activity and messages to a third \nparty without my consent when it deems such action necessary. I consent to the Company’s \nmonitoring of my computer files, internet activity, email messages, and voicemail messages. \n \nI fully understand that this Handbook is intended to foster a better working atmosphere while \nthe employer-employee relationship exists. I also understand that I may be subjected to drug \nand alcohol testing and other physical examinations, as a condition of my employment. \n \n \n \nEmployee signature over printed name \n \n \n \nDate \n \n \n \n \nAnna Katrina Espino \nHR Director  \n \n \n \n \nJennifer Carabeo \nPhilippines Executive Director\n\nAgent SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \nWalmart Associates SOP: Table of contents​\nSubtab 1: Leaves Filing SOP \nSteps for agents to submit time-off requests, including the required notification windows for both \nplanned and emergency absences. \nSubtab 2: Overtime Filing SOP \nThe process for agents to volunteer for OT and the specific steps required to log those extra \nhours for payroll accuracy. \nSubtab 3: Schedule Request SOP \nHow to request shift swaps or temporary schedule changes and how to confirm they are \nreflected correctly on the Transition Sheet. \nSubtab 4: Attendance SOP \nClear guidelines on punctuality, login expectations, and the proper way to notify RTA of any shift \ndeviations. \nSubtab 5: Disputes Filing SOP \nInstructions for agents on how to file a formal dispute if they notice errors in their attendance \nlogs or \"Missing Punches\" in Zoho. \nSubtab 6: Line Assignment SOP \nThe manual process for requesting work in the team chat and interpreting emoji reactions for \nUS, CA, or Pre-Item tasks. \nSubtab 7: RO Line Assignment SOP \nSpecific procedures for the Read-Only workflow, detailing how these specific tasks are assigned \nand prioritized. \nSubtab 8: System Issue Notification SOP \nProtocol for reporting technical blockers or tool outages, including the exact information needed \nby the RTA to document the downtime.\nLeave Management\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Walmart \nAssociates | Leave Management & Attendance \nObjective: To provide you with a clear, step-by-step guide on how to manage your time off, ensure your \nleaves are documented correctly, and maintain a healthy work-life balance while supporting our \noperations. \n \n \n1. Vacation Leave (VL): Planning Your Time Off \nUse this for planned trips, rest, or personal errands. \n●​ Timeline: Submit your request at least 7 days in advance. You can plan up to 14 days \nahead. \n○​ Note: To keep things fair for the whole team, any requests filed more than 14 \ndays before the start date will be automatically disapproved by RTA. \n●​ Availability Check: Before filing, check the Walmart VL Dashboard on Zoho. If a date \nis \"at capacity\" (full), look for alternative dates.​\n \n●​ The Process: ​\n1. Check the dashboard for open slots.​\n2. Sync with your Team Lead (TL) to ensure no critical tasks or \"Critical Support\" shifts \nare missed.​\n3. Once your TL gives the \"OK,\" submit your application on Zoho. Click on Status and\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \nselect VL in Hours and fill in the information needed such as reason and remarks, do not \nforget to upload supporting documents.​\n​\nNote that if the VL’s are in 3 or more days consecutive, supporting documents are \nmandatory revealing the STL or OM approval of your leave.  \n \n●​ Note: You will file your leave in zoho with the attachment of approval from STL or OM, \nbut final approval depends on the RTA based on real-time slot availability. \n2. Sick Leave (SL): Prioritizing Your Health \nUse this when you are unwell and unable to perform your duties. \n●​ Notification: You must notify your TL and/or fill out the Attendance Notification Form \non Zoho at least 2 hours before your shift starts (or no later than 2 hours after it starts).\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n○​ Why? This prevents you from being tagged as NCNS (No Call, No Show), which \nprotects your APS points.\n●​ The 2-Day Rule: If you are sick for 2 or more consecutive days, you must provide a \nMedical Certificate when you return to work. \n●​ Filing: Once you have recovered, file your SL via the Zoho Leave Request form. Be \nready to upload your medical documents for the application to be processed. \n \n3. Bereavement Leave: Support in Times of Loss \nUse this for the passing of an immediate family member. \n●​ Entitlement: Typically 3–5 days, depending on current policy. \n●​ Immediate Needs: If you are currently on shift and receive the news, notify your TL \nimmediately. They will help you coordinate with RTA/Ops for an Emergency Leave so \nyou can log off right away. \n●​ Documentation: You can file for Bereavement Leave on Zoho via the Leave Request \npage. Please upload the Death Certificate to Zoho for HR approval.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n4. Solo Parent Leave: Parental Support \nSpecifically for our colleagues registered as solo parents. \n●​ Eligibility: 7 days per year, available once you have reached regularization on the \ncompany.  \n●​ Validation: Ensure your Solo Parent ID is updated and not expired before you file. \n●​ Filing: Always tag your leave as \"Solo Parent Leave\" in the system—do not use the VL \ncategory, as these are separate credits.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n​\n \n●​ Notice: For planned events (school meetings, etc.), please give at least 48 hours\' \nnotice so the team can adjust.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \nLeave Type Quick-Reference \nLeave Type \nYour Primary Responsibility \nTimeline \nVL \nCheck Dashboard & consult TL \n7-14 days notice \nSL \nNotify TL & file upon recovery \nPost absence \nBereavement \nNotify TL & upload Death Cert \nImmediate (for exit) \nSolo Parent \nKeep ID updated & tag correctly \n48 hours notice preferred \n \n \n6. Retracting a Leave \nIf your plans change and you no longer need the time off. \n●​ When to Act: You must notify your TL and RTA at least 24 hours before the leave was \nsupposed to start. \n●​ System Steps: 1. Go to Leave Request System > My Records > For Approval.​\n2. Select \"Retract.\" \n●​ Requirement: RTA can only approve retractions if the request is made before the actual \ndate of the leave.\nOvertime Filing SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Walmart \nAssociates | Client-Mandated Overtime (OT): Agent Participation & \nExecution\n \n \n1. Purpose/ Scope \nThis procedure outlines how Agents can volunteer for and execute Client-Mandated Overtime \n(OT). It ensures that when there is a sudden spike in customer volume, you know exactly how to \nsign up, where to monitor for instructions, and how to ensure your extra hours are recorded for \npayroll. \n2. Roles & Responsibilities \nRole \nResponsibility \nAgent (OT \nTaker) \nMonitors communication channels, volunteers for slots, and maintains \n100% productivity during OT hours. \nTeam Lead (TL) \nAnnounces OT needs to the team and collects the list of volunteers. \nRTA / \nOperations \nValidates the roster and ensures OT hours are active in the monitoring \ntools. \n \n3. Tools / Requirements \n●​ Workforce Management: InContact Tool (to remain in production). \n●​ Communication: Zoho Cliq, MS Teams, or Email. \n●​ Monitoring: ECE IRR Realtime Dashboard (monitored by RTA).\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n4. Procedure (Steps) \nStep 1: Monitor the OT Announcement \nWhen the Client identifies a volume spike, your Team Lead will post an \"OT Call\" in your team\'s \nchat group or via email. \n●​ Action: Check the message for the specific Line of Business (LOB) affected and the \nnumber of hours/agents needed. \nStep 2: Volunteer for the Shift \nIf you are available to work, reply to the TL\'s post immediately. \n●​ Action: Provide your name and the specific hours you are available to extend. \n●​ Example: \"I am available for 2 hours of OT after my shift ends at 5:00 PM.\" \n●​ Note: Selection is often based on your proficiency in the specific queue experiencing the \nspike. \nStep 3: Confirmation and Log-in \nWait for a \"Go Signal\" from your TL before beginning work. \n●​ Action: Log into the InContact Tool. Stay in a working aux status to ensure the volume \nis being cleared. \nStep 4: Execution & Mandatory Breaks \nWhile on OT, you must maintain high productivity. \n●​ Action: If your OT extends your total workday significantly, coordinate with your TL to \nschedule any additional rest breaks. Do not log out until your approved OT window is \ncomplete or until you receive a \"Cease OT\" notification.​\nNote: There is a 4 hour max limit every shift date.  \nNotes / Tips: \n●​ Productivity is Key: RTAs monitor adherence in real-time. Being on OT means being \n\"in production.\"\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n5. Exceptions & Escalation \nIssue \nAction \nEscalate \nQueue remains high \nafter OT \nContinue production as usual. \nTL will coordinate with RTA for \n\"Cross-LOB\" support. \nPersonal \nEmergency \nIf you must leave during your OT, \nnotify your TL immediately. \nTL will update RTA to find a \nreplacement. \n \n6. Quality Check / Expected Result \n●​ Verification: Check your InContact status to ensure you are correctly logged in and \n\"Active.\" \n●​ Result: The volume spike (backlog) decreases, and Service Level Agreements (SLA) \nreturn to green. \n7. Records / Outputs \n●​ Payroll Export: This process ensures your approved OT hours are reflected in your \nadjusted timesheet.\nSchedule Request SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Walmart \nAssociates | Operations Schedule Synchronization & Shift Updates \n(Schedule Change Request)\n \n1. Purpose/ Scope \nTo provide Agents with a streamlined process for updating their official schedules in Zoho. This \nprocedure ensures that your attendance records, payroll, and adherence metrics accurately \nreflect the shifts assigned in the Transition Workbook, preventing unnecessary attendance \npoints or \"Mismatch\" errors. \n2. Roles & Responsibilities \nRole \nResponsibility \nAgent \nInitiates the formal request in Zoho and ensures all shift details match the \nTransition Workbook. \nTeam \nLead (TL) \nReviews and approves the request to validate it against operational needs. \nWFM / \nRTA \nPerforms final validation and plots the approved schedule into the master \nsystem. \n \n3. Tools / Requirements \n●​ Zoho People: The primary system for filing shift change requests. \n●​ Transition Workbook: The source of truth for your assigned schedule and rest days. \n●​ Imgur: (Optional) For hosting and linking supporting screenshots. \n4. Procedure (Steps) \nStep 1: Initiate the Schedule Change Request \nNavigate to the My Schedule Change Request page within Zoho/ECETrack.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n●​ Action: Click the green plus sign (+) located in the upper right-hand corner to open a \nnew request form. \nStep 2: Complete Shift Details with Precision \nFill out the form accurately. Discrepancies between this form and the Transition Workbook will \nlead to a rejection. \n●​ Full Name: Enter your registered name. \n●​ Requested Date Range: Specify the FROM and TO dates for the new schedule. \n●​ Working Hours: Select either 8 or 10 hours based on your specific LOB requirement. \n●​ New Time Schedule: Enter the START and END times. Crucial: These must mirror the \ntimes listed in the Transition Workbook. \n●​ Rest Days: Check the boxes for your assigned days off according to the Workbook. \n●​ Reason/Note: Provide a clear explanation for the change. You may include an Imgur \nlink for any supporting screenshots. \nStep 3: Submission and Interim Period \nDouble-check all entries before hitting Submit. \n●​ Action: Continue to follow your current/old schedule until you receive a notification \nthat the new request has been officially approved. \n●​ Monitoring: Regularly check your Zoho notifications or official communication channels \n(Cliq/Email) for status updates. \nStep 4: Execute the Approved Schedule \nOnce the status changes to \"Approved,\" strictly adhere to the new timings. \n●​ Action: Update your personal alarms or reminders. Any deviation from this approved \nwindow must be reported to your Team Lead immediately to avoid disciplinary action. \nNotes / Tips: \n●​ Timelines: Always submit requests within the prescribed window to allow WFM enough \ntime for validation. \n●​ Accuracy: One wrong digit in the \"Start Time\" can result in a \"Late\" tag. Cross-reference \nwith the Transition Workbook twice! \n \n \n5. Exceptions & Escalation\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \nIssue \nAction \nEscalate \nRequest Rejected \nReview the rejection note (usually a \ndata mismatch). \nRe-file or consult your \nTL. \nSystem Glitch/No Plus \nSign \nRefresh the page or clear browser \ncache. \nReport to IT/Support. \nUrgent/Last Minute \nMove \nFile the request immediately after the \nverbal instruction. \nNotify RTA for manual \noverride. \n \n6. Quality Check / Expected Result \n●​ Successful Sync: Your \"Shift\" view in Zoho should match the Transition Workbook, and \nyour check-in/out times should no longer trigger \"Mismatch\" alerts. \n7. Records / Outputs \n●​ Approved Shift Change Log: Digital record in Zoho used for payroll auditing. \n●​ WFM Validation Report: Final roster update in the master scheduling tool. \n8. References \n●​ Employee Handbook: Section on Attendance and Punctuality. \n●​ Attendance Policy: Guidelines on unauthorized schedule changes and penalty points.\nAttendance SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Walmart \nAssociates | How to Submit an Attendance Notification in Zoho \nStep-by-step Guide for Submitting of Attendance Notification​\n \n1. Purpose/ Scope \nThis procedure ensures that all absences and tardiness are communicated and documented \nformally. Following this guide prevents you from being tagged as No Call, No Show (NCNS), \nprotects your attendance record from unnecessary disciplinary points, and allows the RTA team \nto adjust staffing levels in real-time. \n2. Roles & Responsibilities \nRole \nResponsibility \nAgent \nProvides timely notice of absence/lateness and submits the Zoho \nform accurately. \nImmediate \nSupervisor / RTA \nAcknowledges the notification and coordinates coverage; may file \non behalf of the agent in emergencies. \nWFM / RTA \nPlots the notification into the master schedule for real-time \ntracking. \n \n3. Tools / Requirements \n●​ Zoho Creator: The official platform for the Attendance Notification Form. \n●​ Communication Channels: Batphone, Cliq/Direct Message, or Email. \n4. Procedure (Steps) \nStep 1: The Initial Alert (Communication) \nAs soon as you realize you will be late or absent, you must inform your immediate supervisor or \nRTA.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n●​ Timeline: Notifications must be made within the window of 2 hours before up to 2 \nhours after your scheduled shift start. \n●​ Warning: Failure to communicate within this window is automatically considered an \nNCNS, which carries heavier disciplinary consequences. \nStep 2: Access the Zoho Attendance Notification Form \nFormalize your notification by recording it in the system. \n●​ Action: Log in to Zoho Creator / Zoho Attendance Tool and select the Attendance \nNotification Form. \n●​\n \nStep 3: Complete the Required Details \nEnsure all fields are filled out accurately to avoid your submission being tagged as \"Unexcused\": \n●​ Employee Name: Your full registered name. \n●​ Channel of Notification: Select how you first reached out (e.g., Batphone, Direct \nMessage, Email). \n●​ Type of Notification: Choose Absent or Late. \n●​ Reason: Select the most accurate category (Personal, Medical, Emergency, etc.). \n●​ Expected Return Date: Specify when you will be back in production. \n●​ Additional Notes: Add specific details or mention if you have supporting documents \n(like a Medical Certificate) to upload later.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \n \nStep 4: Post-Submission Responsibilities \nSubmitting the form is only the first half of the process. \n●​ Action: Stay reachable via your chosen communication channel for any follow-up \nquestions from your TL or Operations. \n●​ Action: Follow any specific instructions given by your TL regarding shift make-ups or \ndocumentation requirements. \nNotes / Tips: \n●​ Emergency Filing: If you are physically unable to access Zoho (e.g., no internet or \nmedical emergency), ensure your supervisor is aware so they can file the form on your \nbehalf. \n●​ Audit Ready: All records are audited daily. Ensure your \"Reason\" matches the \ndocumentation you provide later. \n5. Exceptions & Escalation \nIssue \nAction \nEscalate \nZoho System is \nDown \nTake a screenshot of the error and notify \nyour TL immediately. \nRTA for manual logging. \nExtended Absence \nIf you will be out for more than one day, \nupdate your \"Expected Return\" in the \nform. \nTL / STL / OM \nMissed Notification \nWindow \nIf you notify after the 2-hour post-shift \nwindow, explain the delay clearly. \nOperations / WFM for \ncase-by-case review. \n \n6. Quality Check / Expected Result\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n●​ Successful Notification: You receive a confirmation of submission, and your status is \nupdated in the RTA\'s real-time tracker, preventing an NCNS tag. \n \n7. Records / Outputs \n●​ Attendance Notification Log: A digital record in Zoho used for attendance audits. \n●​ RTA Intraday Report: Real-time headcount adjustment based on your submission. \n8. References \n●​ Employee Handbook: Section on Attendance and NCNS Policies. \n●​ APS (Attendance Points System) Guidelines.\nDisputes Filing SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Walmart \nAssociates | Agent Timekeeping & Break Tagging Disputes​\n \n1. Purpose/ Scope \nThis procedure provides a clear framework for you to contest inaccuracies in your time logs or \nbreak taggings. Whether caused by system glitches or tool errors, this process ensures your \nactual work hours and productivity are recorded fairly.  \nNote: Accuracy is vital as these adjustments directly impact your payroll and performance \nmetrics. \n2. Roles & Responsibilities \nRole \nResponsibility \nAgent \n(Disputant) \nFiles the dispute within the 5-day window and provides high-quality, \ntimestamped proof. \nRTA (Approver) \nActs as the \"Gatekeeper.\" Validates proof against system logs (IC \nstamps) and approves/rejects based on evidence. \nTeam Lead (TL) \nProvides a \"Vouch\" only as a last resort if technical proof is unavailable. \n \n3. Tools / Requirements \n●​ In-Contact (IC) Logs: The primary source for timestamp verification. \n●​ Zoho Dispute: The platform where disputes are filed. \n●​ Screenshot Tool: To capture errors, timestamps, or connectivity issues. \n4. Procedure (Steps) \nStep 1: Identify the Dispute Type \nDetermine what needs correcting before filing:\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n●​ Break Disputes: Used for incorrect taggings during breaks, meetings, or coaching \nsessions. The goal is to prove you returned to work on time. \n●​ Time Log Disputes: Used when Zoho fails to capture your actual Login or Logout time \ndue to tool or connectivity issues. \nStep 2: Gather Valid Proof (Hierarchy of Evidence) \nRTAs do not approve disputes based on explanations alone; you must provide proof. RTAs \nfollow this priority list—higher-ranked proof is more likely to be approved without hesitations: \n1.​ IC (In-Contact) Stamps: The most reliable evidence.​\n​\nRequest for IC stamps: Navigate on Cliq and find the specific thread for IC screenshot \nrequest.​\n​\nFormat for request:​\nRequesting IC Stamp:​\nDate:​\nShift:​\nReason:​\n \n2.​ Availability Messages: Chat timestamps showing you messaged \"Back from break\" or \n\"Logged in.\" \n3.​ Issue Tracker: Screenshots of the tracker with visible timestamps. \n4.​ Error Screenshots: Visible proof of login errors or system downtime (Date/Time must \nbe visible). \n5.​ Team Lead Vouch: A confirmation from your TL (Use this only if steps 1-4 are \nimpossible). \nStep 3: Filing the Dispute \nSubmit your request in the Zoho Dispute Module within 5 days of the occurrence. \n●​ Action: Ensure the Time In and Time Out you request match your proof to the minute. \n●​ Checklist: Verify the Date, Scheduled Shift, and Working Hours (8 or 10) are all correct. \n●​ Reason: Choose the reason that best fits your evidence (e.g., \"Tool Issue\" vs. \"System \nDowntime\"). \nStep 4: RTA Review and Outcome \nThe RTA will cross-reference your submission against the RTA Validation Checklist. \n●​ Approval: If your proof is clear, timestamped, and matches your request. \n●​ Rejection: If proof is missing, unclear, filed after 5 days, or if the timestamps do not \nmatch the requested adjustment.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \nNotes / Tips: \n●​ 5-Day Rule: Any dispute filed after 5 days will be automatically rejected. For late filings, \nyou must be redirected to Lemuel Ching. \n●​ Quality Matters: A clear screenshot of an IC Stamp is worth more than a long written \nexplanation. \n5. Exceptions & Escalation \nIssue \nAction \nEscalate \nDispute Rejected/Needs \nCorrection \nReview the RTA notes, fix the \nerror, and re-submit. \nConsult your TL for guidance. \nMissing Proof for System \nOutage \nUse the \"TL Vouch\" method if \nit was a site-wide issue. \nTL to coordinate with RTA. \nRepeated System Errors \nKeep a log of all incident \ntickets. \nEscalate to IT and Operations \nfor a permanent fix. \n \n6. Quality Check / Expected Result \n●​ Successful Dispute: Your Zoho attendance record is updated to reflect your actual \nworking time, ensuring your productivity (Adherence) and Payroll are correct. \n7. Records / Outputs \n●​ Dispute History Report: A digital record of all approved/rejected adjustments. \n●​ Updated Payroll Export: Reflects the corrected hours for the cutoff. \n8. References \n●​ Attendance & Timekeeping Policy. \n●​ Payroll Processing Guidelines.\nLine Assignment SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Walmart \nAssociates | Trust & Safety: Queue Prioritization, Assignment, and LATTE \nMonitoring​\n \n1. Purpose/ Scope \nThis procedure ensures that US, CA, and Pre-Item tasks are handled with the correct urgency \nto meet our Service Level Agreements (SLA). As an agent, this guide helps you understand how \ntasks are assigned to you based on your training, how to request work via chat, and the \nimportance of maintaining an \"Online\" status in LATTE. \n2. Roles & Responsibilities \nRole \nResponsibility \nAgent \nRequests lines via chat, follows SLA priority, stays \"Online\" in LATTE, and \nprocesses assigned tasks. \nRTA / Support Monitors SLA urgency, assigns lines manually or via LATTE upload, and \nmonitors agent idle time. \nTeam Lead \n(TL) \nSupports agents in meeting SLAs and follows up on unresponsiveness \nreports from RTA. \n \n3. Tools / Requirements \n●​ ServiceNow Dashboard: Used to view live queue volume and priority levels. \n●​ Chat Channel: [WMT - Trust & Safety] for work requests. \n●​ Latte Tool: Used for automated line assigning.  \n4. Procedure (Steps) \nPart A: Requesting and Receiving Work (Manual) \nStep 1: Requesting Lines\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \nWhen you are ready for a new batch of work, post your request in the [WMT - Trust & Safety] \nteam chat. \n●​ Action: Clearly state your availability and which queues you are trained for (US, CA, or \nPre-Items). \nStep 2: Understanding Your Assignment (Emoji Reactions) \nThe RTA will assign lines based on which queue (US or CA) is closest to an SLA breach. Once \nassigned, the RTA will react to your message with an emoji. \n●​ Action: Look for these emojis to know what work you have been given: \n○​ US flag = US Line assigned. \n○​ CA flag = CA Line assigned. \n○​ Check mark = Pre-Item assigned. \nStep 3: Processing by Priority \nAlways work your assigned lines based on the following SLA Tiering: \n1.​ P1: Must be completed within 2 Hours. \n2.​ P2: Must be completed within 12 Hours. \n3.​ P3: Must be completed within 24 Hours. \n4.​ P4/Pre-Items: Must be completed within 48 Hours. \n \nPart B: LATTE Volume & Status Monitoring \nStep 4: Ensuring \"Online\" Status​\n​\nReceiving Automated Assignments In this method, assignments are pushed to you \nautomatically through the tool rather than through chat requests. \n●​ Action: Monitor your LATTE interface for incoming line assignments. \n●​ Action: Once a line is assigned by the tool, begin processing immediately following the \nSLA priority rules mentioned in Step 3. \n●​ Note: You do not need to request lines in the chat if the LATTE tool is actively pushing \nassignments to your dashboard. \nThe RTA uses the LATTE Admin Dashboard to monitor queue health and agent activity. \n●​ Action: Ensure your status is ONLINE in LATTE at all times during production. \n●​ Action: If you are tagged as IDLE, the RTA will reach out via the Shoutout Channel \nand CC your Team Lead. \n●​ Note: Unresponsiveness while idle may result in an entry in the WFM Logs for \ndocumentation.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \nNotes / Tips: \n●​ Equal Priority: US and CA lines are equally important; the \"Tie-breaker\" is always the \nSLA clock. \n●​ Pre-Items: These can only be handled by Pre-Item trained agents. Do not attempt these \nif you haven\'t completed the specific training. \n5. Exceptions & Escalation \nIssue \nAction \nEscalate \nUnresponsive \nAgent \nRTA reaches out via the Shoutout Channel. \nIf no reply, file a WFM \nLog entry. \nNo Volume \nInform RTA via the Latte Meet or send a \nDM.  \nNotify RTA for re-upload  \nRRtask not visible \nin SNW \nComplete task on latte to move on to \nanother set of assignments \nInform RTA for \nacknowledgment via \nincall messages \n \n6. Quality Check / Expected Result \n●​ Success: All P1 lines are cleared within the 2-hour window. \n●​ Success: Agents show 100% adherence to their \"Online\" status in LATTE. \n7. Records / Outputs \n●​ ServiceNow Volume Exports: Daily record of incoming tasks. \n●​ WFM Logs: Documentation of idle time and unresponsiveness. \n●​ LATTE Queue Statistics: Summary of completed vs. pending tasks. \n8. References \n●​ Trust & Safety Training Manual. \n●​ SLA Priority Matrix (P1-P4).\nRO Line Assignment SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Walmart \nAssociates | RO Claim Assignment & Brand-Specific Routing​\n \n \n1. Purpose/ Scope \nThis procedure outlines how Rights Owner (RO) claims are prioritized and assigned. It ensures \nthat regardless of whether you are working on US or CA queues, the most urgent \nclaims—specifically Counterfeit and Oldest Created Dates—are handled first, while respecting \nspecial routing rules for specific brands. \n2. Roles & Responsibilities \nRole \nResponsibility \nRO Agent \nRequests claims via the designated thread, follows specific brand \nrules (Apple/Stripe), and processes assigned claims. \nRTA / Support \nMonitors volume, shares counts, and manually assigns claims using \nthe \"Locked To\" field and emoji confirmations. \nApple Dedicated \nAgent \nSpecifically handles all Apple-related brand claims. \n \n3. Tools / Requirements \n●​ RO Portal/System: For claim processing and \"Locked To\" updates. \n●​ Chat Channels: Designated threads for US Claim Requests and CA Claim Requests. \n4. Procedure (Steps) \nStep 1: Start-of-Shift & Low Volume Protocol \nAt the beginning of the shift, the RTA will broadcast the current US and CA claim counts. \n●​ Action: If the RTA announces No Incoming Volume, do not remain idle.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n●​ Action: Immediately pivot to working on your Pending Claims or any Unprocessed \nEmails in your inbox to maintain productivity. \nStep 2: Requesting Claims via Thread \nYou must never cherry-pick claims. All work must be requested through the official channels. \n●​ US Requests: Post in the [Claim request Thread here ⬇️] \n●​ CA Requests: Post in the [CA Thread Claims] \n●​ Requirement: Wait for the RTA to react with an Emoji to your message. This emoji is \nyour official confirmation that a claim has been locked to you. \nStep 3: Understanding Prioritization & Assignment \nThe RTA assigns claims based on a specific hierarchy. Even though all types have an 8-hour \nSLA, they are not treated equally. \n1.​ US and CA Queues: Treated with equal importance. \n2.​ Claim Hierarchy: Counterfeit claims are assigned first, followed by Copyright, \nTrademark, and Patent. \n3.​ Age: The oldest \"Created Date\" is always prioritized to prevent SLA breaches. \nStep 4: Following Brand-Specific Rules \nCertain brands have mandatory routing that overrides the standard flow: \n●​ Apple Brand: If you are not an \"Apple Dedicated Agent,\" you cannot be assigned these \nclaims. \n●​ StripeDevice: These are automatically routed/assigned to Teresa Lin (t0l013q). \nStep 5: Receiving the Assignment \nThe RTA will perform the following in the system: \n1.​ Open the claim and update the \"Locked To\" field with your name. \n2.​ Click Save and select Start Review. \n3.​ Your Action: Once you see the emoji reaction in the chat, refresh your dashboard to \nsee the locked claim and begin your review immediately. \nNotes / Tips: \n●​ System Assignments: If you work on a claim that was automatically assigned by the \nsystem (instead of requested via thread), an NTE will be issued for documentation. \n●​ Manual Locks: Always verify the claim is \"Locked To\" you before you start the \"Start \nReview\" process to avoid duplicate work. \n5. Exceptions & Escalation\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \nIssue \nAction \nEscalate \nClaim Assigned to Wrong \nPerson \nDo not process. Notify the RTA to \nunlock the claim. \nRTA \nUrgent SLA (Under 1 \nHour) \nFlag the claim in the chat immediately. \nRTA / TL \nApple Claim in General \nQueue \nDo not assign to general agents. \nEscalate to RTA for \nthe transferring \nprocess. \n \n6. Quality Check / Expected Result \n●​ Compliance: 100% of Apple claims are handled by dedicated agents. \n●​ SLA Health: Zero claims exceed the 8-hour limit, with Counterfeit claims resolved \nfastest. \n7. Records / Outputs \n●​ NTE Records: For tracking unauthorized system-assignment work. \n●​ Locked To Logs: Audit trail of who handled which Rights Owner claim. \n8. References \n●​ Rights Owner (RO) Brand Guidelines. \n●​ SLA and Hierarchy Matrix.\nSystem Issue Notification SOP\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for Walmart \nAssociates | System Issue Reporting & Item Transfer Protocol (Agent View)​\n \n1. Purpose/ Scope \nThis procedure provides you with a clear roadmap for handling \"Ghost\" or \"Erroring\" items in \nServiceNow (SNW). When an item becomes non-actionable or refuses to leave your queue, \nthese steps ensure the item is properly transferred to the RTA team, keeping your productivity \nmetrics clean and preventing queue inflation. \n2. Roles & Responsibilities \nRole \nResponsibility \nAgent \nIdentifies the error, captures evidence, and initiates the reporting process \nwith the Team Lead (TL). \nTeam Lead \n(TL) \nValidates the issue, ensures the Google Form is filed, and supervises the \nitem transfer. \nOn-shift RTA \nReceives the \"Ghost\" item and confirms the transfer was successful in the \nsystem. \n \n3. Tools / Requirements \n●​ SNW (ServiceNow): To perform the item review and transfer. \n●​ Google Reporting Form: [Official Reporting Link] \n●​ Internal Chat (Cliq/Slack): For real-time coordination with your TL and the RTA. \n4. Procedure (Steps) \nStep 1: Identify \"Non-Actionable\" Items \nRecognize when an item needs to be reported. Look for these three scenarios: \n●​ Ghost Items: You completed the task, but it remains in your bucket.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n●​ Erroring Items: The system shows a failure message or behaves abnormally when you \ntry to process it. \n●​ Active Queue Mismatch: The item is marked \"Done\" but refuses to disappear from your \nactive view. \nStep 2: Perform a Technical Reset \nBefore reporting, try a quick self-audit to ensure it isn\'t a temporary lag. \n●​ Action: Refresh your SNW workspace. \n●​ Action: Check if there is a known delay from another department. \n●​ Result: If the item is still stuck after a hard refresh, move to Step 3. \nStep 3: Capture Evidence & File the Report \nYou must document the bug before the item is moved. This data is used to report system bugs \nto the client. \n●​ Action: Access the Google Reporting Form. \n●​ Required Data: Enter your name, the Item ID, a brief description of the error, and an \nattached Screenshot of the issue. \nStep 4: Execute Item Transfer in SNW \nWith your TL’s guidance, move the item out of your queue so you can continue working on \nactive tasks. \n●​ Action: Open the stuck item in SNW and select the Transfer action. \n●​ Target: Assign the item to an On-shift RTA. \n●​ Fallback: If no RTA is available, transfer the item to John Vergara – Vendor (vn5774u). \nStep 5: Confirm Completion \nEnsure the handoff is finalized so the item doesn\'t \"bounce back.\" \n●​ Action: Your TL (or you, if instructed) must message the receiving RTA via Cliq: \"I have \ntransferred Item ID [Number] to you; please confirm if visible.\" \n●​ Verification: Check your SNW dashboard to confirm your queue is now clear of the \nreported item.\nECENTERPRISES GROUP MANILA INC  \n33F Robinsons Summit Center, 6783 Ayala Avenue Makati City, 1200  \nMetro Manila, 1200, Philippines  \n \n \nNotes / Tips: \n●​ Don\'t Skip the Form: The Google Form is the only way the RTA team can prove to the \nclient that a system bug exists. \n●​ Real-time Sync: Always communicate the transfer immediately; RTAs cannot approve \nwhat they cannot see. \n5. Exceptions & Escalation \nIssue \nAction \nEscalate To \nTransfer Blocked \nAttempt to \"Allow\" or \"Block\" the item first. Take a \nscreenshot of the block error. \nRTA / RTA \nLead \nGoogle Form is \nOffline \nKeep a manual log (Word/Excel) with the Item ID \nand Reason. \nRTA Team \nItem Still Visible \nRe-verify the Item ID and ensure \"Submit\" was \nclicked in SNW. \nTL / RTA \nLead \nThe \"Returning\" \nGhost \nFlag items that keep appearing in your queue after \nbeing transferred. \nRTA Lead \n6. Quality Check / Expected Result \n●​ Traceability: Every \"Ghost\" item has a matching entry in the Google Form. \n●​ Queue Health: Your active queue only contains items you can actually work on, \nensuring your AHT and productivity are accurate. \n7. Records / Outputs \n●​ Google Form Entries: The master audit log for system bugs. \n●​ SNW Audit Trail: The history log showing the item moving from your ID to the RTA."
